window.COURSE = {"modules":[{"k":"0","name":"Start here"},{"k":"1","name":"AI in plain English"},{"k":"2","name":"Your PC"},{"k":"3","name":"Model files and the memory math"},{"k":"4","name":"Run your first models"},{"k":"5","name":"Aligned vs uncensored models"},{"k":"6","name":"Use it for real"},{"k":"7","name":"How models really work"},{"k":"8","name":"Customize without training"},{"k":"9","name":"Fine-tune your own model"},{"k":"10","name":"Uncensor and edit models"},{"k":"11","name":"Capstone"},{"k":"R","name":"Reference"}],"lessons":[{"id":"welcome","mod":"0","num":"","title":"Welcome and how to use this course","mins":8,"optional":false,"text":"You already use ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini. This course takes you from there to running, understanding, training and customising open-weight models on your own Windows PC with an NVIDIA graphics card, including making your own uncensored variants. It assumes no AI knowledge and only basic Python. What you will learn What the course covers and what you will be able to do at the end How each lesson is laid out and how to study so things stick What to set up on day one Where you will end up By the end you will be able to: Explain how a language model works, from tokens to the transformer to training. Pick models that fit your graphics card, run them in LM Studio and Ollama, and tune every setting. Use local models from your own Python code, with structured output, tools and your own documents. Fine-tune a model on your own data with LoRA, evaluate it honestly, and export it to GGUF. Change a model's behaviour by steering and by editing its weights, including abliteration, and build your own uncensored custom model. The path You are here → 1 AI in plain English → 2 Your PC → 3 Model files and memory math → 4 Run models → 5 Aligned vs uncensored → 6 Use it for real → 7 How models work → 8 Customize → 9 Fine-tune → 10 Uncensor and edit → 11 Capstone Modules 1 to 6 are the foundations: concepts, hardware, running models and using them well. Modules 7 to 11 are training and editing. The suggested pace is eight weeks at about six hours a week. The 8-week plan lists exactly which lessons to read each week and what to build. Four milestones Milestone After You can… 1. Running locally Week 2 (modules 1 to 3, lesson 4.1) Run a model on your GPU and explain every number: size, VRAM, context, speed 2. Using it for real Week 4 (modules 4 to 6) Pick models, tune settings, and use a model from Python with RAG and tools 3. Your first fine-tune Week 7 (modules 7 to 9) Train a LoRA on your own data, evaluate it, and run it in LM Studio 4. Your own model Week 8 (modules 10 and 11) Steer and edit weights, and build your own uncensored custom model Lessons marked Optional are deep dives or reference material. Skip them on a first pass; the core path still works without them. How each lesson works A short list of what you will be able to do at the end. Concept boxes hold the one idea worth remembering. Say it back in your own words. On your PC boxes tell you what to click or type on Windows. Calculators and demos are live. Change the numbers and watch what moves. Scripts are complete and can be copied with one click. Run them; do not just read them. Every lesson ends with a short \"Check yourself\" quiz. Try to answer before you read the explanation. Retrieval practice is the most effective study method there is. How to study Make a folder called ai-lab in your user folder and a file in it called NOTES.md . Write down your GPU, your numbers and your surprises. You will use these notes in later lessons. Do the hands-on steps on your own machine. Reading about VRAM is not the same as watching it fill up. Change one thing at a time. If you change the model and the context length together you learn nothing. Expect to be confused for a while. Terms like \"quantization\" sound intimidating and turn out to be simple. The glossary and search ( Ctrl + K ) are one click away. When something breaks, write down the exact error. That is the start of fixing it. Day one checklist Create the ai-lab folder and NOTES.md Find out which graphics card you have (lesson 2.1 shows how) Free up at least 50 GB of disk space. Models and training outputs are big files. Skim the 8-week plan A note on dates Local AI moves fast. Specific model names, versions and app screens in this course reflect November 2026. The ideas (memory math, file formats, sampling, LoRA, directions) do not change. When a model name here looks old, search for the newest model in the same size class and apply the same method. Check yourself Why does this course start with concepts and hardware instead of training? Training is not possible on Windows Training is less important than chatting Training decisions (model size, VRAM, templates, evaluation) depend on understanding how models and your hardware work Every choice in module 9 builds on modules 1 to 7. What is the best use of the \"Check yourself\" questions? Try to answer before reading the explanation Skip them if you understood the lesson Read the answers first so you do not get anything wrong Trying to recall an answer, even when you get it wrong, strengthens memory far more than rereading."},{"id":"what-is-ai","mod":"1","num":"1.1","title":"What AI, machine learning and LLMs actually are","mins":10,"optional":false,"text":"\"AI\" is used for everything from spam filters to chatbots. Before running a model on your PC it helps to know exactly what the thing in the file is. What you will learn Place AI, machine learning, deep learning and LLMs in one picture Explain what a \"model\" and a \"weight\" are Explain why a language model is a next-word predictor and why that is enough to look intelligent Explain why it makes things up Four nested words These four terms get mixed up constantly. They are nested, like Russian dolls. Term Meaning Example Artificial intelligence Any software that does something we would call intelligent. The widest ring. A chess engine, a route planner, a chatbot Machine learning AI where the rules are learned from examples instead of written by hand. A spam filter trained on millions of emails Deep learning Machine learning that uses many-layered neural networks. Image recognition, speech to text Large language model (LLM) A deep-learning model trained on huge amounts of text to continue text. GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama, Qwen A model is a function with knobs Forget AI for a moment. A function takes numbers in and gives numbers out. f(x) = 3 × x turns 2 into 6. A model is a function too, but instead of one number (the 3) it has billions of adjustable numbers called weights or parameters . You do not set them by hand. A training process sets them automatically by showing the model many examples and nudging every weight a tiny bit each time the answer is wrong. Concept: weights Weights are the learned numbers inside a model. A \"9B model\" has about 9 billion of them. The file you download is essentially just those numbers, plus a little metadata. There is no database of text inside and no internet connection needed to use it. What an LLM does An LLM has one job: given some text, score every possible next piece of text. Type \"The capital of France is\" and the model gives a high score to \" Paris\" and a very low score to \" banana\". A program then picks one piece, adds it to the text, and asks again. Repeat a few hundred times and you have a paragraph. That is the whole mechanism behind every chatbot you have used. Everything else (answering questions, writing code, following instructions) emerges from getting extremely good at this one prediction task on a huge amount of text. To predict the next word in a physics textbook well, the model has to pick up physics. To predict the next line of Python, it has to pick up Python. Concept: predict, pick, repeat The model never writes a whole answer at once. It produces one small piece, appends it, and runs again. This is why long answers take time and why a model cannot \"go back and fix\" what it already wrote. Why models make things up Because the job is \"produce plausible continuations\", not \"look up the truth\". If the training text rarely mentioned a fact, the model still has to say something, and the most plausible-sounding thing is often wrong. This is called hallucination . Smaller models hallucinate more. The fix is not a better prompt alone. It is giving the model the facts to work with (lesson 6.3) and checking what it says. Knowledge cutoff A model only knows what was in its training text, up to a certain date. A local model has no idea what happened last week unless you tell it. The cloud chat apps get around this by adding web search. A local model can too, but only if you connect it. Check yourself What is physically inside a model file? A compressed copy of the internet A database of sentences it looks up Billions of learned numbers (weights) and some metadata There is no text database. The knowledge is spread across the weights, which is why a model can be wrong and cannot cite where it learned something. Why can a model that only predicts the next word still answer questions and write code? It secretly runs a search engine Predicting text well on a huge amount of data forces it to absorb facts and patterns It has a separate module for each task Good prediction needs understanding of the content. Instruction-following is added on top with extra training (lesson 5.1)."},{"id":"open-vs-closed","mod":"1","num":"1.2","title":"Cloud AI vs local AI: closed, open-weight and open-source","mins":9,"optional":false,"text":"When you use ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini, your text travels to a data centre and a giant model answers. Running locally flips that around. The words \"open\" and \"open-source\" are used loosely, so this lesson sorts them out. What you will learn Tell closed, open-weight and open-source models apart Understand what a model licence lets you do Know the major open-weight families and who makes them Know honestly where local models are weaker than the cloud ones Three kinds of model Closed (API-only) Open-weight Truly open-source Examples GPT, Claude, Gemini Llama, Qwen, Gemma, gpt-oss, Mistral, DeepSeek A few research models that also publish training data and code You can download the weights No Yes Yes You can run it offline No Yes Yes You can fine-tune it Only through the vendor, if at all Yes, within the licence Yes You can see the training data No Usually no Yes Most of what people call \"open-source AI\" is really open-weight : you get the finished model but not the recipe. That is still plenty. It means you can run it, inspect it and customise it. Concept: licence Every open-weight model comes with a licence. Some are permissive (Apache 2.0 and MIT allow almost any use, including commercial). Others add rules, such as a limit on very large companies or a list of banned uses. Read the licence on the model's page before building a product on it. For learning and personal use, nearly all are fine. Where the major families come from Qwen (Alibaba): a large range of sizes, strong at coding and many languages. Llama (Meta): the family that started the open-weight boom. Gemma (Google): compact, well-tuned models. gpt-oss (OpenAI): open-weight models released under Apache 2.0. Mistral / Ministral (Mistral AI): efficient European models. DeepSeek (DeepSeek): very strong but very large flagship models, plus smaller distilled versions. Phi (Microsoft), Granite (IBM), Nemotron (NVIDIA), GLM (Z.ai): other capable families. Lesson 4.5 shows how to pick between them. Honest comparison Cloud chat apps Local model on your PC Capability Frontier-level, very strong at hard reasoning Good, and improving fast, but a model that fits a home GPU is far smaller than a frontier model Privacy Your text goes to a provider Stays on your machine Cost Subscription or per-token Free after hardware and electricity Works offline No Yes Limits and rules Set by the provider You choose the model and settings Extras (web search, image generation, file upload) Built in You add them yourself Effort None Setup and tuning The sensible mindset Keep using the cloud assistants for hard problems. Use local models for private data, bulk work, offline use, learning, and the things only open weights allow, such as inspecting and training. You are not replacing one with the other. Check yourself What does \"open-weight\" mean? You can download the finished model's weights and run them yourself The company published its full training data The model is free for any use whatsoever Open-weight does not promise training data or unlimited rights. Check the licence. Which is the strongest reason to use a local model for a confidential contract? Local models are always smarter It is faster than any cloud model The text never leaves your computer Privacy is a core advantage. Capability on very hard tasks is usually still better in the cloud."},{"id":"train-vs-infer","mod":"1","num":"1.3","title":"Training vs inference: what your PC will actually do","mins":8,"optional":false,"text":"Two very different jobs hide behind the phrase \"running AI\". Most people on a home PC only ever do one of them, and knowing which saves you from buying the wrong hardware. What you will learn Define inference and training and say which needs more hardware Describe the stages a model goes through from raw text to a chatbot Know where fine-tuning fits, which is the part you will eventually do Inference: using a trained model Inference means giving a finished model some text and getting a response. Every time you chat with a local model, that is inference. It needs enough memory to hold the weights and a bit of working space. A home GPU can do it for models up to a certain size. Training: creating or changing the weights Training means adjusting the weights. It needs far more: the weights, plus a copy of the training signals for every weight, plus the data flowing through. As a rough rule, fully training a model needs several times the memory that running it needs, and it takes huge amounts of computation. Concept: why you can run a big model but cannot train one Running a model reads the weights once per word. Training reads and rewrites them millions of times and stores extra numbers for each. Frontier models are trained on thousands of GPUs for weeks. A single home GPU cannot do that, but it can do something much smaller: fine-tuning. The life of a model Pre-training. The model reads trillions of words and learns to predict the next one. This costs millions of dollars. The result is a base model . It continues text but does not follow instructions well. Instruction tuning (SFT). The base model is trained further on examples of questions and good answers so it behaves like an assistant. The result is an instruct or chat model. Preference tuning / alignment (RLHF, DPO and similar). People or other models rank answers, and the model is nudged toward the preferred ones and away from harmful ones. This is where refusals come from (lesson 5.1). Optional: reasoning training. Some models are trained to think step by step before answering. These are often called \"thinking\" models. Release. The weights are published. You download them and do inference. Where you come in: fine-tuning Fine-tuning takes a finished model and trains it a little more on your own examples, to teach a style, a format or a specialty. Techniques like LoRA and QLoRA train only a small add-on instead of every weight, which makes fine-tuning possible on one home GPU. That is the subject of module 9. Inference Fine-tuning (LoRA) Pre-training What changes Nothing A small add-on to the weights All weights, from scratch Needs One GPU, sized to the model One GPU with more headroom, or a rented one Thousands of GPUs Realistic on your PC Yes For small models, yes No Check yourself Chatting with a model you downloaded is which activity? Training Inference Pre-training The weights are fixed and only read. Which stage gives a model its refusal behaviour? Preference tuning / alignment Reading the raw text corpus Downloading from Hugging Face Pre-training only learns text patterns. Alignment steps teach what to decline. Why can LoRA fine-tuning fit on a home GPU? It skips the data It only works on tiny 100-parameter models It trains a small add-on instead of every weight Far fewer trainable numbers means far less memory for the training signals."},{"id":"tokens-context","mod":"1","num":"1.4","title":"Tokens and the context window (the model's memory)","mins":12,"optional":false,"text":"A model does not read letters or words. It reads tokens. And it can only \"remember\" a limited number of them at a time. This is the one kind of memory you really need to understand, and it explains a lot of confusing behaviour. What you will learn Explain what a token is and why token counts differ from word counts Build a tokenizer by hand with byte-pair encoding Explain the context window and what happens when it fills up Explain why a model forgets earlier parts of a long chat Understand why bigger context costs memory on your graphics card Tokens Computers need numbers, so text is first chopped into pieces called tokens and each piece gets an id. A token can be a whole common word ( the ), part of a word ( un + believ + able ), a number, or a punctuation mark. In English, one token is roughly four characters or three quarters of a word. Code and non-English text usually need more tokens per word. Try the toy tokenizer below. It uses a simple rule so you can feel the idea. Real tokenizers are learned from data, so exact counts differ between models. How a tokenizer is built: byte-pair encoding Most tokenizers are made with byte-pair encoding (BPE) . Start with single characters. Count every pair of neighbouring symbols in a big body of text, merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol, and repeat until the vocabulary is the size you want. The ordered list of merges is the tokenizer. A tiny example. Imagine the whole training text is these words, with how often each appears: play ×6, player ×5, playing ×3, replay ×2, replayed ×1. start: p l a y | p l a y e r | p l a y i n g | r e p l a y | r e p l a y e d merge 1: \"p l\" appears 17 times -> pl merge 2: \"pl a\" appears 17 times -> pla merge 3: \"pla y\" appears 17 times -> play merge 4: \"play e\" appears 6 times -> playe merge 5: \"playe r\" appears 5 times -> player result: play | player | play i n g | r e play | r e playe d Frequent words became single tokens. Rarer ones are built from pieces. Now tokenize a word that was never in the training text, displaying , by applying the same merges in order: d i s p l a y i n g → d i s pl a y i n g → d i s pla y i n g → d i s play i n g . Seven tokens. Unseen words still work; they just cost more tokens. A real tokenizer runs this on a huge multilingual corpus until it has 100,000 to 250,000 tokens. What costs more tokens Kind of text Typical cost Common English prose About 4 characters per token Code More tokens: symbols, indentation and unusual names split up Other languages Often 1.5 to 3 times more than English, depending on the tokenizer Numbers Split into chunks of digits, which is one reason models are clumsy at arithmetic Random strings, hashes, base64 Very expensive: almost every few characters is a token Special tokens Every tokenizer also has special tokens : single ids with a structural meaning, such as \"start of a message\", \"end of a message\" or \"end of text\". They are how a chat model knows where your message ends and its reply begins. Lesson 7.3 shows them in a real chat template, and they matter a lot when you train. See a real tokenizer After you set up the AI Python environment in lesson 2.3, activate it and run: from transformers import AutoTokenizer tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\") # downloads only the tokenizer files for text in [\"Unbelievable!\", \"def add(a, b): return a + b\", \"नमस्ते दुनिया\", \"1234567\", \"aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ=\"]: ids = tok.encode(text) print(len(ids), [tok.decode([i]) for i in ids]) The context window A model can only look at a fixed number of tokens at once. That limit is the context window , for example 8,192 or 32,768 or 131,072 tokens. It must hold everything: your system prompt, the whole conversation so far, any pasted documents, and the answer being written. Concept: the context window is the model's working memory The model has no memory between messages. Chat apps create the illusion of memory by re-sending the entire conversation every turn. If the conversation grows past the window, the oldest parts must be dropped or summarised, and the model genuinely cannot see them any more. That is why a model \"forgets\" the start of a long chat. What this means in practice A bigger window lets you paste longer documents but does not make the model smarter. Models often get worse at using information buried in the middle of very long context. Shorter and more focused is usually better. Starting a fresh chat for a new task is not a trick. It frees the window and removes distracting history. Why context costs graphics memory While generating, the model keeps a running record of every token it has already processed, called the KV cache . It grows with every token. At 4,000 tokens it is small. At 128,000 tokens it can be larger than the model itself. This is the main reason you will choose context length carefully on a GPU with limited memory. Lesson 3.3 has a calculator for it. On your PC When you load a model in LM Studio or Ollama you pick a context length. Many tools default to a small value so that the model fits. If a model suddenly \"forgets\" instructions or your pasted document is ignored, check this setting first. It is the most common beginner problem. Check yourself A chat app seems to remember what you said ten messages ago. How? The model updates its weights as you chat The app re-sends the whole conversation to the model every turn The model stores your messages in a database inside the file Weights are frozen during a chat. \"Memory\" within a conversation is just text placed back into the context. You double the context length you load. What mainly grows in graphics memory? The model weights The size of your prompt The KV cache Weights stay the same size. The cache that tracks processed tokens grows with context. Roughly how many English words are 1,000 tokens? About 750 About 4,000 About 100 A token is about three quarters of a word in English."},{"id":"why-local","mod":"1","num":"1.5","title":"Why run AI locally, and what to expect","mins":7,"optional":false,"text":"Before spending hours on setup it is worth being clear about what you get and what you do not. Setting expectations now saves frustration later. What you will learn List the real benefits of running locally Know the limits and what \"good enough\" looks like Decide which jobs go to which kind of model The real benefits Privacy. Notes, source code, client documents and personal files never leave your computer. No metering. Run a model on ten thousand files overnight and it costs only electricity. Offline. It works on a plane, on a locked-down network, or when a service is down. Control. You pick the model, the settings and the system prompt. Nobody changes the model under you. Learning. You can see tokens, memory use, speeds and settings directly. Nothing teaches faster. Customisation. Open weights can be fine-tuned, inspected and edited, which modules 9 and 10 teach. The real limits A model that fits a home GPU is much smaller than a frontier cloud model. Expect weaker reasoning on hard tasks, more mistakes, and less knowledge of obscure topics. Speed depends on your hardware. Short answers feel instant on a good GPU. Long reasoning chains can take minutes. You are the system administrator. You download updates, handle disk space and fix problems. No built-in web search, file upload or image tools. You add them. Match the job to the tool Task Good fit Summarise a confidential document Local Draft emails, rewrite text, brainstorm Local is fine Classify or extract data from thousands of files Local (no per-token cost) Explain code, small scripts, refactors Local, ideally a coding-tuned model Hard maths, long multi-step research, big codebases Cloud frontier model Up-to-the-minute facts Cloud with web search Your first goal Do not aim to replace the cloud models. Aim to get one model running well on your own PC, understand why it behaves the way it does, and be able to explain every number on the screen. That understanding is what makes training possible later. Check yourself Which task is the best fit for a local model? Searching today's news Tagging 5,000 private support emails overnight Solving a research-level maths problem Bulk, private, repeatable work is where local shines. Fresh facts and the hardest reasoning favour cloud models."},{"id":"pc-hardware","mod":"2","num":"2.1","title":"Your PC: CPU, RAM, GPU and VRAM","mins":10,"optional":false,"text":"Almost every question about local AI comes down to one number: how much video memory your graphics card has. This lesson explains the parts of your PC, which ones matter, and how to read your own specs. What you will learn Say what the CPU, RAM, GPU and VRAM each do for a language model Explain why VRAM and memory bandwidth matter more than anything else Find your own graphics card, VRAM and RAM in Windows The four parts Part What it is Role for local AI CPU The general-purpose processor Can run models by itself, but slowly. Handles everything the GPU does not. RAM System memory (for example 16 or 32 GB) Holds whatever does not fit on the GPU. Much slower to use for AI than VRAM. GPU The graphics processor, with thousands of small cores Does the heavy arithmetic of a model many times faster than a CPU. VRAM The GPU's own fast memory (for example 8, 12 or 24 GB) The model's weights must live here to run fast. This is the limiting resource. Concept: VRAM sets the speed, VRAM + RAM sets the limit A model runs fastest when all of its weights and working memory sit in VRAM. If it does not fit, LM Studio and Ollama put as many layers as they can on the GPU and run the rest from system RAM on the CPU. That works, just more slowly. So two questions decide what you can run: does it fit in VRAM (fast), or in VRAM plus free system RAM (works, slower)? Module 3 teaches you to answer both with arithmetic. Why memory bandwidth matters To produce each token, the GPU must read essentially all the model's weights from memory once. So the speed of generation is limited by how fast memory can be read, called memory bandwidth and measured in GB/s, rather than by raw computing power. A card with twice the bandwidth generates roughly twice as fast with the same model. Lesson 3.4 turns this into a speed estimate. Here is why this matters when shopping or comparing: two cards with the same VRAM can feel very different. A card with a narrow memory bus can be several times slower than one with a wide bus. Why NVIDIA? NVIDIA GPUs use a software platform called CUDA . Almost every AI tool is built first for CUDA: LM Studio and Ollama use it to accelerate models, PyTorch uses it for training, and fine-tuning libraries such as Unsloth rely on it. AMD and Intel GPUs also work in several tools, but NVIDIA has the smoothest path, and it is what the rest of this course assumes. Find out what you have On your PC Press Ctrl + Shift + Esc to open Task Manager and click Performance . Click GPU (there may be two: a weak integrated one and your NVIDIA card). Note the name, Dedicated GPU memory (this is your VRAM) and the driver version. Click Memory and note total RAM and speed. Click CPU and note the model name. Or from a terminal, once the driver is installed (next lesson): nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,driver_version,memory.total,memory.used --format=csv Write the result in your NOTES.md . You will need these numbers in nearly every later lesson. Laptops Laptop GPUs often have less VRAM than the desktop card with the same name, and may be throttled by heat. Windows can also switch between the integrated and NVIDIA GPU. In the NVIDIA Control Panel or Windows Graphics settings you can set the AI app to use the NVIDIA GPU. Pick your card Choose your graphics card to see its VRAM and bandwidth and a rough idea of what it can run. Figures are approximate. Check yourself What decides whether a model runs fast on your PC? The number of CPU cores How much storage space you have Whether the model fits in your VRAM Models that fit in VRAM run on the GPU at full speed. Spilling to system RAM is far slower. Two GPUs both have 12 GB. One has double the memory bandwidth. Which generates tokens faster with the same model? The higher-bandwidth one, roughly twice as fast They are the same The one with the lower bandwidth Generation reads all weights per token, so bandwidth sets the speed ceiling."},{"id":"nvidia-driver","mod":"2","num":"2.2","title":"NVIDIA driver, CUDA and checking that the GPU works","mins":9,"optional":false,"text":"Most \"my GPU is not being used\" problems come from the driver. This lesson gets the foundations right and explains the CUDA jargon that confuses everyone. What you will learn Install or update the NVIDIA driver Read the output of nvidia-smi Know when you need the CUDA Toolkit and when you do not Watch VRAM use live while a model runs Driver first The driver is the software that lets Windows and apps talk to the card. For LM Studio and Ollama, a reasonably current driver is all you need. Ollama's Windows documentation lists NVIDIA driver 551.61 or newer as the minimum, and newer cards such as the RTX 50 series need much newer drivers anyway, so simply update to the latest. On your PC Open the NVIDIA app (or GeForce Experience) and let it install the latest Game Ready or Studio driver. Either works for AI. Or download directly from nvidia.com/Download by choosing your card. Restart if asked. Check it with nvidia-smi Open Windows Terminal or PowerShell and run: nvidia-smi You get a table. The parts to read: Driver Version : what is installed. CUDA Version : the highest CUDA version this driver supports. It does not mean CUDA is installed. Memory-Usage : for example 1200MiB / 12288MiB , meaning used and total VRAM. Windows and your browser already use some. GPU-Util : how busy the GPU is. It jumps while a model generates. To watch it update once a second while you chat with a model: nvidia-smi -l 1 Concept: you will see the model in VRAM Load a model and run this command in another window. Memory-Usage jumps by roughly the model size plus its context cache. This is the quickest way to confirm the model is really on the GPU, and you will use it to check the calculators in module 3. CUDA, cuDNN and the toolkit: what you need What you want to do Install CUDA Toolkit? Run models with LM Studio or Ollama No. They ship their own GPU libraries. Train or run models with PyTorch from pip No. The PyTorch download bundles the CUDA runtime it needs. You only need a recent enough driver. Compile llama.cpp or other CUDA code yourself Yes. Install the Toolkit from NVIDIA. Do not install everything \"just in case\" Beginners often install several CUDA versions and end up with confusing conflicts. Install only what the task in front of you requires. If the GPU is not being used Update the driver and restart. Close other heavy VRAM users such as games, video editors and many browser tabs with hardware acceleration. On a laptop, make sure the app is set to use the NVIDIA GPU, not the integrated one. Check the app's runtime setting. In LM Studio this is the CUDA runtime under the runtimes settings. In Ollama the server log shows which GPU it found. Check that the model is not so large that the app chose CPU instead. Check yourself nvidia-smi shows \"CUDA Version: 13.0\". What does that tell you? CUDA Toolkit 13.0 is installed Your driver supports up to CUDA 13.0 Your GPU has 13 GB of VRAM It is the maximum supported by the driver, not an installed toolkit. Do you need to install the CUDA Toolkit to use LM Studio? Yes, always Yes, but only version 11 No, it ships its own libraries You only need the driver. The Toolkit is for compiling CUDA code yourself."},{"id":"windows-dev-setup","mod":"2","num":"2.3","title":"Windows toolbox: terminal, Python, Git and optional WSL2","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"You said you know basic Python and some terminal. This lesson tightens that up into a working setup on Windows, so later lessons can say \"run this command\" without surprises. What you will learn Use Windows Terminal and PowerShell for the commands in this course Install Python and Git with winget Create and use a virtual environment Set up one AI Python environment with PyTorch and Transformers for the whole course Know what WSL2 is and when you will want it Terminal basics Install Windows Terminal from the Microsoft Store if it is not already there (Windows 11 includes it). It hosts PowerShell tabs. Commands in this course are written for PowerShell. A handful you will use constantly: cd $HOME\\ai-lab # change folder ls # list files (alias for Get-ChildItem) mkdir experiments # make a folder code . # open the folder in VS Code, if installed cls # clear the screen Install Python and Git winget is Windows' built-in package manager. winget install Python.Python.3.12 winget install Git.Git winget install Microsoft.VisualStudioCode Close and reopen the terminal, then check: python --version git --version If python opens the Microsoft Store Windows has \"app execution aliases\" that can hijack the command. Open Settings, search \"Manage app execution aliases\" and turn off the Python entries, or use py (the Python launcher) instead of python . Virtual environments A virtual environment is a private folder of Python packages for one project. AI libraries are large and have strict version needs, so keeping each project separate prevents breakage. cd $HOME\\ai-lab python -m venv .venv .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install --upgrade pip pip install openai requests Your prompt shows (.venv) when it is active. Run deactivate to leave it. If activation is blocked PowerShell may say scripts are disabled. Allow scripts you wrote or downloaded for your own account only: Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned A Python refresher you will actually use You will not need advanced Python for most of this course. This small script is the pattern behind lesson 6.2. Make sure every line makes sense: import requests url = \"http://localhost:1234/v1/chat/completions\" payload = { \"model\": \"your-model-name\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Say hello in five words.\"}], \"temperature\": 0.7, } reply = requests.post(url, json=payload, timeout=120) print(reply.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]) That is a dictionary, a list of dictionaries, an HTTP request and an index into the JSON response. If any part is unfamiliar, spend an hour on a beginner Python course first. It will pay off many times over. Your AI Python environment (set up once) Several later lessons run small models directly in Python: looking at a real tokenizer (1.4), counting parameters (7.2), watching generation (7.4), and steering and editing weights (module 10). They all use one environment with PyTorch (the engine that runs models on your GPU) and Hugging Face Transformers (the library that downloads and loads models). Set it up now, while you are in the terminal, so it is ready when you need it. Go to pytorch.org/get-started/locally and choose Stable, Windows, Pip, Python, CUDA . Copy the command it shows. Run these, pasting the PyTorch command where shown: cd $HOME\\ai-lab python -m venv .venv-ai .venv-ai\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 python -m pip install --upgrade pip # paste the PyTorch command from pytorch.org here, for example: # pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 pip install transformers accelerate safetensors openai numpy pydantic Then check that PyTorch sees your GPU: python -c \"import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\" If it prints True and your card's name, you are done. If it prints False , you probably installed the CPU-only build: run pip uninstall torch and paste the CUDA command again. RTX 50-series cards need a build made with CUDA 12.8 or newer. GTX 16-series and RTX 20-series cards work fine; the scripts in this course automatically use float16 on them because they lack bfloat16 support. Two environments, two jobs .venv (above) is a light environment for calling LM Studio or Ollama. .venv-ai is the heavier one for running models in Python. Training with Unsloth gets its own Linux environment in lesson 9.3. WSL2: Linux inside Windows (optional for now) WSL2 runs a real Linux system inside Windows, and it can use your NVIDIA GPU. You do not need it to run models with LM Studio or Ollama. You will want it later, because some training tools, including parts of the fine-tuning ecosystem, are developed for Linux first and are often smoother there. When you are ready: wsl --install This installs Ubuntu. After a restart you get a Linux terminal where Linux commands from tutorials work as written. Module 9 uses it for training. Keep project files inside the Linux filesystem (your Linux home folder), not under /mnt/c , for much better speed. Check yourself Why use a virtual environment? Each project gets its own packages, so versions do not clash It makes Python run on the GPU It is required to open the terminal AI libraries are large and version-sensitive. Separate environments keep projects from breaking each other. When do you first really need WSL2? To run LM Studio To install the NVIDIA driver When a training tool is easier to run on Linux Inference apps run natively on Windows. WSL2 is a convenience for Linux-first training tools."},{"id":"gpu-tiers","mod":"2","num":"2.4","title":"What your PC can run: the tier map","mins":11,"optional":false,"text":"This lesson is a first map. Find your row and you will know roughly which models run fast, which run with help from system RAM, and which are out of reach. Module 3 then teaches you to compute it exactly. What you will learn Know what size of model runs fully on the GPU, and what runs with GPU + RAM offload Read a real example: a 4 GB GTX 1650 laptop with 16 GB RAM Understand the idea of \"dense\" and \"mixture-of-experts\" models Have a plan for your own card The tier map These are practical ranges for a typical 4-bit \"Q4_K_M\" version of a model (lesson 3.1 explains that term) with a moderate context and 16 GB of system RAM . \"Fast\" means fully on the GPU. \"With offload\" means part of the model runs from system RAM: it works, at a few tokens per second. More RAM raises the offload ceiling. Your VRAM Fast (fully on GPU) With GPU + RAM offload Example cards 4 GB 1B to 4B 7B to 12B work well, 14B is experimental, about 20B is the ceiling with 16 GB RAM GTX 1650, RTX 3050 laptop 6 GB Up to about 7B 8B to 14B, about 20B is the ceiling GTX 1660, RTX 2060, RTX 4050 laptop 8 GB 7B to 9B 12B to 20B RTX 3060 Ti, 4060, 5060 12 GB 9B at higher quality, 12B to 14B 20B to 27B RTX 3060 12 GB, 4070, 5070 16 GB 14B at good quality, 20B-class models 27B to 32B RTX 4060 Ti 16 GB, 4080, 5060 Ti 16 GB, 5070 Ti, 5080 24 GB 27B to 32B dense, 30B-class mixture-of-experts 70B with 32 GB+ RAM, slowly RTX 3090, 4090 32 GB Same as 24 GB with long context and higher quality 70B with 32 GB+ RAM RTX 5090 Check your own PC Worked example: GTX 1650 (4 GB) laptop, 16 GB RAM A real setup: Intel Core i5 11th-gen H-series, 16 GB RAM, GTX 1650 with 4 GB VRAM, Windows 11. Gemma 3 12B at Q4 (a file of about 7 GB) runs fine on it, even though the file is larger than the VRAM, because about a third of the model sits on the GPU and the rest runs from RAM. Model size How well it runs Good for 1B to 3B Excellent, fully on the GPU Fast experimentation 4B Very good Everyday local AI 7B to 8B Good with GPU + RAM offload Chat, coding, RAG 12B Works, a few tokens per second More capable chat and reasoning 14B Experimental, needs careful quantization Testing 20B and above Not recommended RAM and speed limits A sensible model kit for this PC: Purpose Model Size General chat Qwen3 4B Reasoning and harder chat Gemma 3 12B Coding Qwen2.5 Coder 7B Lightweight experiments Llama 3.2 3B Embeddings for RAG nomic-embed-text 137M And which size to use while working through this course: 3B to 4B for setup, prompting, settings, RAG and tool calling (fast feedback matters more than raw quality), and 7B to 12B when you study quantization, offloading and speed. Training on this card is covered in lesson 9.1. Laptops with a 4 GB card On most laptops the NVIDIA GPU does not drive the screen, so nearly all 4 GB is available for models. Plug in the charger: on battery the GPU slows down a lot. Concept: dense vs mixture-of-experts (MoE) A dense model uses all its parameters for every token. A mixture-of-experts model has many specialist \"experts\" but only activates a few per token. A name like \"35B-A3B\" means 35 billion total, 3 billion active. Two consequences: all 35 billion must still be stored (so memory is sized by the total ), but each token only reads the active part, so it can generate much faster than a dense model of the same size. Example models in each size class (late 2026) Several recent guides published in October 2026 recommend these as sensible fits. Names change quickly, so treat them as examples of a size class and check what is newest. 8 GB: Qwen3.5 9B at Q4_K_M (about 5.6 GB), Gemma 4 E4B. 12 GB: Gemma 4 12B at Q4 (about 7 GB), Qwen3.5 9B at higher quality. 16 GB: gpt-oss-20b (about 12 GB in its native 4-bit format), Gemma 4 12B at Q8. 24 to 32 GB: 27B to 35B models such as Qwen's 27B dense and 35B-A3B mixture-of-experts, Gemma 4 26B and 31B. No big GPU? You are not locked out CPU-only and small-VRAM machines can still learn everything in this course using 1B to 4B models. They are surprisingly capable for summaries, rewriting, extraction and learning how it all works. For training later, rented cloud GPUs are cheap per hour (lesson 9.3). Your plan Write these three lines in NOTES.md : My GPU and VRAM: … The biggest model that runs fully on my GPU, and the biggest that runs with offload: … The model sizes I will use: a small fast one for learning, a bigger one for quality Check yourself \"Qwen3.5-35B-A3B\" is a mixture-of-experts model. Which number sets how much memory you must have? 3 billion (active) 35 billion (total) The context length only All experts must be stored. Only the active ones are read for each token, which helps speed, not memory. With 8 GB of VRAM, which is the most sensible first choice? A 7B to 9B model at Q4 A 70B model at Q8 A 30B dense model at FP16 A 7B to 9B Q4 model is about 4 to 6 GB, leaving room for context."},{"id":"quantization","mod":"3","num":"3.1","title":"Parameters, precision and quantization","mins":12,"optional":false,"text":"A 9-billion-parameter model sounds like it must be enormous. How big it is on disk and in memory depends on how many bits each number uses. Shrinking the bits is called quantization, and it is the trick that lets big models fit on small cards. What you will learn Compute a model's size from its parameter count and precision Explain what quantization is and what it costs in quality Quantize a block of weights by hand and see where the error comes from Read names like Q4_K_M and Q8_0 Choose a sensible quantization for your VRAM Size = parameters × bits per weight Each weight is a number, and a number takes memory. Models are usually trained in 16-bit precision (2 bytes per weight). So a 9B model at 16 bits is about 9 × 2 = 18 GB. A 70B model is 140 GB. That is too big for any home graphics card. Concept: quantization Quantization stores each weight with fewer bits, for example 4 bits instead of 16. The model becomes about four times smaller. Because the weights are stored less precisely, there is a small loss of quality. It is like saving a photo as JPEG: much smaller, and for most purposes you cannot tell. The formula Size in GB ≈ parameters (in billions) × bits per weight ÷ 8. Example: 9B at Q4_K_M, which averages about 4.9 bits per weight: 9 × 4.9 ÷ 8 ≈ 5.5 GB. A real 9B Q4_K_M file measures about 5.6 GB, which matches. How quantization works Quantization stores each weight as a small integer plus a shared scale . To use a weight, the runtime multiplies the integer by the scale. Weights are handled in small blocks (32 or 256 weights in llama.cpp), each with its own scale, so one unusual value only affects its own block. Worked example: eight weights to 4 bits Take a block of eight weights: [0.20, −0.35, 0.10, 0.04, −0.15, 0.70, −0.22, 0.30] . Four bits gives 16 integer levels, −8 to 7. Scale. Map the largest magnitude (0.70) to 7: scale = 0.70 ÷ 7 = 0.10. Round. Divide each weight by the scale and round to the nearest integer. Dequantize. Multiply back: the model computes with these values. Weight ÷ 0.10 4-bit integer Back to a number Error 0.20 2.0 2 0.20 0.00 −0.35 −3.5 −4 −0.40 −0.05 0.10 1.0 1 0.10 0.00 0.04 0.4 0 0.00 −0.04 −0.15 −1.5 −2 −0.20 −0.05 0.70 7.0 7 0.70 0.00 −0.22 −2.2 −2 −0.20 +0.02 0.30 3.0 3 0.30 0.00 The typical (root-mean-square) error is about 0.03, roughly 12% of an average weight here. Notice that the tiny weight 0.04 became exactly zero. Storage. 8 weights × 4 bits plus one 16-bit scale = 48 bits, or 6 bits per weight. With blocks of 32, the scale is shared more widely: (32 × 4 + 16) ÷ 32 = 4.5 bits per weight. That is exactly llama.cpp's Q4_0 layout. The outlier problem. Change 0.70 to 0.32 and the scale halves, so the grid is finer and the error drops to about a third. One big weight stretches the grid for its whole block. This is why real schemes use small blocks and keep sensitive layers at higher precision. Scale plus minimum. Mapping the block's actual range (−0.35 to 0.70) onto the 16 levels instead of a symmetric grid cuts the error by about 40% here. The K-quants ( Q4_K_M and friends) use this idea, with blocks grouped into \"super-blocks\". Importance matrix (imatrix). Statistics from running the model on sample text tell the quantizer which weights matter most, so it spends precision where it counts. Files labelled \"imatrix\" or \"i1\" usually lose less quality at 4 bits and below. Reading quantization names The popular format for local models is GGUF . Its quantization names look like Q4_K_M : Q4 : about 4 bits per weight on average. K : the \"K-quant\" method, which gives more bits to the most sensitive parts. M (medium): a size variant. S is small, L is large. Q8_0 : 8 bits, an older simple scheme. Near-lossless. F16 or BF16 : the original 16-bit precision. Names like IQ3_XS use cleverer compression for very low bit counts. MXFP4 : a 4-bit floating-point format some newer models are released in directly. Explore it The quality curve Level Rough effect When to use F16 / BF16 Original Reference, fine-tuning inputs Q8_0 Almost no loss When you have spare VRAM Q6_K Very close to original Good when it fits Q5_K_M Close Good balance Q4_K_M Small, usually acceptable loss The usual sweet spot Q3_K_M Noticeable loss Only to squeeze a bigger model in Q2_K Large loss, often unreliable Rarely a good idea How quality loss is measured Perplexity (PPL) : how surprised the model is by real text. Lower is better. For quantization you look at the increase compared with the original. KL divergence (KLD) : how different the quantized model's whole probability distribution is from the original's at each step. Zero means identical. It catches changes perplexity can hide. llama.cpp publishes measurements like these for Llama 3 8B. The pattern holds for most models: Quantization Size (GiB) Perplexity increase KL divergence Q8_0 7.96 +0.003 0.001 Q6_K 6.14 +0.022 0.006 Q5_K_M 5.33 +0.057 0.011 Q4_K_M (with imatrix) 4.58 +0.151 0.028 Q3_K_M 3.74 +0.657 0.102 Q2_K (with imatrix) 2.96 +2.416 0.332 Notice the knee: going from 8 bits down to about 4.5 costs little, and every step below 4 bits costs much more than the last. A note on 16-bit formats Training normally uses BF16 , a 16-bit floating-point format with the same range as 32-bit numbers but less precision. The older FP16 has more precision but a much smaller range, so large values can overflow during training. That is why you will see bf16 in training scripts and in conversion commands (lesson 9.6). The golden rule A bigger model at Q4 usually beats a smaller model at Q8. A 14B model at Q4 (about 8.5 GB) is typically better than a 7B model at Q8 (about 7.5 GB). So the best use of your VRAM is to pick the biggest model that fits at around Q4 to Q5, then spend leftover memory on context. The exception is very small models (under about 4B). They suffer more from aggressive compression, so use Q6 or Q8 for them if they fit. Check yourself About how large is a 14B model at roughly 4.9 bits per weight? 14 GB About 8.6 GB About 28 GB 14 × 4.9 ÷ 8 ≈ 8.6 GB. Which usually gives better results on the same 8 GB card? A 3B model at F16 A 1B model at Q8 A 9B model at Q4 Parameter count matters more than precision once you are at about 4 bits. What does the \"K\" in Q4_K_M mean? The K-quant method, which spends more bits on sensitive weights 1,000 parameters The model has been keyed to your GPU It names the quantization method, not a hardware setting."},{"id":"file-formats","mod":"3","num":"3.2","title":"Model file formats: GGUF, safetensors and friends","mins":9,"optional":false,"text":"On a model download page you will see several file types. Picking the wrong one is a common beginner stumble, and some formats carry a real security risk. What you will learn Tell GGUF, safetensors and pickle-based files apart Know which format your tools expect Understand why safetensors and GGUF are safer than older formats Read a model's file list on Hugging Face The main formats Format What it is Used by GGUF ( .gguf ) One self-contained file with weights, tokenizer and settings, designed for quantized local inference LM Studio, Ollama, llama.cpp safetensors ( .safetensors ) A safe, fast file of raw tensors, often split into several shards, plus separate config and tokenizer files Hugging Face Transformers, PyTorch, fine-tuning tools, vLLM PyTorch checkpoints ( .bin , .pt , .pth ) Python \"pickle\" files. Older, and can execute code when loaded. Legacy models AWQ / GPTQ / EXL2 Other quantization styles for GPU-only servers vLLM, ExLlama and similar Concept: pick by purpose To run a model in LM Studio or Ollama, download a GGUF . To train or modify a model, you want the original safetensors version. You will use both in module 9. A GGUF is what you get at the end of fine-tuning, after converting. Why safety differs Pickle-based files ( .bin , .pt ) are Python objects. Loading them can run arbitrary code hidden inside. A downloaded checkpoint from an untrusted source is therefore like running an unknown program. Safetensors and GGUF are plain data formats designed so that loading does not execute code. That is a big reason the community moved to them. Safe habits Prefer GGUF and safetensors. Be suspicious of .bin , .pt and .pkl files from unknown publishers. Download from the model's real publisher, or from well-known community uploaders with many downloads and an active discussion page. Check that the model card names its base model and licence. Never run an installer, script or \"launcher\" that comes bundled with a model. Weights are data and need nothing like that. Reading a Hugging Face file list A GGUF repository typically lists the same model in many sizes: Qwen-example-9B-Q4_K_M.gguf 5.6 GB Qwen-example-9B-Q6_K.gguf 7.4 GB Qwen-example-9B-Q8_0.gguf 9.5 GB mmproj-F16.gguf 0.9 GB (vision add-on, only for image input) You pick one quantization file, not all of them. If you see an mmproj file, that is the part that lets a vision-capable model look at images. Tools like LM Studio fetch it automatically if needed. Two more things you will see Sharded files such as model-00001-of-00004.safetensors are one model split into parts. Download all parts. Tools do it for you. Base vs instruct in the name. \"-Instruct\" or \"-it\" models follow instructions. Base models only continue text. For chatting, always pick the instruct or chat version. Check yourself Which file do you download to chat with a model in LM Studio? A GGUF file A .pt checkpoint A zip of Python scripts LM Studio runs GGUF models via llama.cpp. Why is a .bin pickle checkpoint from a stranger risky? It is always corrupted It is too large Loading it can execute hidden code Pickle can run arbitrary code. Safetensors and GGUF cannot do that by design."},{"id":"will-it-fit","mod":"3","num":"3.3","title":"Will it fit? The VRAM and RAM calculator","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"This is the most useful skill in the course. In one minute, with simple arithmetic, you can say whether any model will run fully on your GPU, run with help from system RAM, or not run at all, roughly how fast, and what to change. What you will learn Add up the four things that use VRAM Estimate the KV cache for a given context length Estimate the GPU + RAM split and its speed when a model does not fit in VRAM Decide what to adjust when a model does not fit Use the calculator and then check it against reality The four consumers of VRAM Weights : parameters × bits ÷ 8 (lesson 3.1). KV cache : grows with context length. About 2 × layers × KV heads × head size × bytes per token. Overhead : the CUDA context and temporary buffers, roughly 1 to 1.5 GB. Windows itself : your desktop and browser take around 0.5 to 1 GB if this GPU also drives your monitor. Concept: the context bill For a typical 8B-class model the cache costs about 0.14 MB per token with the default 16-bit cache. At 32,000 tokens that is roughly 4.7 GB. That is nearly as big as the 4-bit weights. Halving the cache precision to 8-bit halves that cost. The calculator The calculator uses typical layer and head counts for each size class. Real models differ, and some use tricks such as sliding-window or hybrid layers that shrink the cache a lot. Use it for a first estimate, then confirm with nvidia-smi . What to adjust when it does not fit Work down this list in order: Lower the context length. The cheapest fix. Most chats do not need more than 8K. Quantize the KV cache to 8-bit. About half the cache memory, with small quality cost. In LM Studio this is the K and V cache quantization setting (marked experimental, and it needs Flash Attention on). Choose a lower weight quantization , for example Q5 to Q4. Choose a smaller model. Offload layers to system RAM. It works, more slowly. On small cards this is normal, and it is how a 4 GB GPU runs 7B to 12B models. What spilling to RAM costs Models are made of layers. If only some layers fit in VRAM, the rest run on the CPU using system RAM. For each token the GPU reads its share of the weights at GPU speed and the CPU reads the rest at RAM speed, so the time per token is roughly the sum of the two. System RAM is much slower to read than VRAM (dual-channel DDR4 is around 40 GB/s, versus 128 GB/s even on a GTX 1650 and 500+ GB/s on mid-range cards), so the RAM part dominates. Example: a 12B model at Q4 (about 7 GB) on a 4 GB card with 16 GB RAM. About 2 GB of weights fit on the GPU and about 5 GB run from RAM, giving roughly 3 to 5 tokens per second. That is slower than reading speed but perfectly usable, and many people run models this way. The calculator above shows this split and estimates the speed. The real limit is free system RAM. Windows and your apps need about 5 GB, so with 16 GB RAM roughly 11 GB is left for the part of the model that does not fit on the GPU. Past that, Windows swaps to disk and the model crawls. Try it now Put your own VRAM and a model you are curious about into the calculator. Write the predicted total in NOTES.md . In lesson 4.1 you will load that model and compare against nvidia-smi . Being within about 10 to 15% is normal. Check yourself A model fits at 8K context but not at 32K. What is the cheapest first fix? Buy more RAM Lower the context length Reinstall the driver The cache grows with context, so shortening it frees VRAM immediately. What happens if a few layers do not fit in VRAM? Nothing, speed is unchanged The model becomes more accurate Those layers run on the CPU and the whole model slows down Spilled layers use system RAM and the CPU, which are much slower. Which of these is NOT a normal consumer of VRAM while chatting? Your chat history saved on disk The model weights The KV cache Saved history on disk uses disk, not VRAM. Only the text in the active context becomes cache."},{"id":"speed","mod":"3","num":"3.4","title":"Speed: tokens per second and what limits it","mins":9,"optional":false,"text":"Two models can both fit and still feel completely different to use. Speed comes from memory bandwidth and model size, and you can estimate it before you download anything. What you will learn Distinguish prompt processing speed from generation speed Estimate tokens per second from bandwidth and model size Understand why mixture-of-experts models feel fast Know what \"good\" speed feels like Two speeds Prompt processing (also called prefill or time to first token): reading your whole prompt. It is compute-heavy and fast on a GPU, usually hundreds to thousands of tokens per second. Generation (decoding): producing the answer one token at a time. This is what \"tokens per second\" usually means, and it is limited by memory bandwidth. The ceiling formula Concept: bandwidth ÷ model size Generating one token reads all the active weights once. So tokens per second can never exceed memory bandwidth divided by the size of the weights read. A card with 500 GB/s running a 5 GB model has a ceiling of about 100 tokens per second. Real speed is usually 55 to 75% of that. Why mixture-of-experts feels fast A 35B-A3B model reads only about 3 billion active parameters per token, so its per-token cost resembles a 3B model while its answers resemble a much larger one. The price is that all 35B must still be stored. On a 24 GB card that is a great trade. What is \"fast enough\"? Tokens per second Feel Under 5 Painful. Usually a sign of CPU spill. 5 to 15 About reading speed. Usable for short answers. 20 to 40 Comfortable for chat 50 and above Feels instant. Great for agents and bulk jobs. Thinking models are slower than their speed suggests Reasoning models write a long hidden chain of thought before the answer. At 30 tokens per second, 2,000 tokens of thinking is over a minute. You can usually shorten or disable thinking in the settings. Measure your own LM Studio shows tokens per second under each reply, and Ollama prints it with ollama run MODEL --verbose . Record it in your notes next to the model, quantization and context length. Over the weeks you will build a personal table of what your PC does. Check yourself A GPU has 1,000 GB/s bandwidth and the model weights are 10 GB. What is the speed ceiling? About 100 tokens per second About 10,000 tokens per second About 10 tokens per second 1,000 ÷ 10 = 100. Real speed will be somewhat lower. Why is a 30B-A3B mixture-of-experts model usually much faster than a 30B dense model? It uses less memory in total It reads only the active experts for each token It does not need a GPU Total memory is the same or larger, but per-token reads are small."},{"id":"lm-studio","mod":"4","num":"4.1","title":"Your first local model with LM Studio","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"LM Studio is the friendliest way to start: a desktop app with a model browser, a chat window and a local server. By the end of this lesson you will have a model answering questions on your own GPU. What you will learn Install LM Studio and download a model Load it with the GPU and chat Check that the GPU is really being used Compare real VRAM use with your calculator estimate 1. Install Download the Windows installer from lmstudio.ai and run it. It needs a 64-bit PC with a CPU that supports AVX2 (almost every processor from the last decade does). A GPU is optional but makes it much faster. Or from a terminal: winget install ElementLabs.LMStudio If the winget name does not work, use the installer. On first launch you may be offered a \"developer\" or \"power user\" view. Choose that, because it shows the settings this course explains. 2. Find and download a model On your PC Click the Discover tab (magnifying glass). Search for a model in the size class from lesson 2.4, for example \"qwen 9b\" or \"gemma 12b\". LM Studio marks each file with a badge showing whether it will fully fit your GPU. Choose a Q4_K_M file for your first model. Click Download . It saves into the My Models folder. Pick an instruct version, not a base model. 3. Load and chat Open the Chat tab and click the model selector at the top. Before loading, click the gear or \"Load settings\" for the model. Set GPU Offload to the maximum so all layers go to the GPU. Set Context Length to 8192 for now. Click Load , then type a message. Try these three to see how the model behaves: Explain what a token is to a ten-year-old in three sentences. Write a Python function that returns the nth Fibonacci number. What happened in the news yesterday? The third one will fail or invent something. That is your first real lesson about knowledge cutoffs. 4. Check the GPU is being used Open a second terminal and run nvidia-smi -l 1 while the model answers. You should see Memory-Usage jump by roughly the model's size plus the context cache, and GPU-Util rise while it generates. LM Studio also shows tokens per second under each reply. Lab: verify the maths Note VRAM used before loading, after loading, and after a long reply. Compare with your lesson 3.3 calculator prediction. The weights part should be within a few percent. Overhead varies by about a gigabyte. Record tokens per second. Repeat once with 32K context and see how VRAM changes. Useful tour Chat : conversations, system prompt box, presets. Discover : find and download models. You can also search Hugging Face directly. My Models : the folder of downloaded files. Delete from here to free disk space. Developer : the local server (lesson 6.2) and logs. Logs are the first place to look when something is wrong. Settings : choose the runtime. The CUDA runtime is the one that uses your NVIDIA GPU, and LM Studio tells you when updates are available. Guardrails LM Studio can refuse to load a model that it thinks will not fit. That protects your system from freezing. You can relax it in settings, but a model that truly cannot fit will make Windows crawl. Check yourself How do you confirm the GPU is doing the work? Check the CPU temperature only Count the words in the reply Watch Memory-Usage and GPU-Util in nvidia-smi while it generates VRAM use and GPU utilisation rising together show the model is on the GPU. Which setting do you raise to put all model layers in VRAM? GPU Offload Temperature Top-p GPU Offload sets how many layers go to the GPU."},{"id":"settings-guide","mod":"4","num":"4.2","title":"Settings that matter: load options and sampling","mins":16,"optional":false,"text":"The settings panel looks intimidating until you notice that it has two halves: how the model is loaded into memory, and how the next word is chosen . Learn both and you can tune any model. What you will learn Explain every important load setting and when to change it Explain temperature, top-k, top-p and min-p, and see their effect Choose sensible starting values for different tasks Names can differ by version LM Studio and Ollama rename and reorganise settings from time to time. The ideas below stay the same. Part 1: load settings (how the model sits in memory) Setting What it does Guidance GPU Offload How many model layers go to the GPU Max, unless the model does not fit Context Length Size of the context window 4K to 8K for chat, more only when you need it Flash Attention A faster, leaner way to compute attention On. Faster and uses less memory. K and V Cache Quantization Store the KV cache in 8-bit or lower 8-bit to double your context for a small cost. Needs Flash Attention. Keep model in memory Do not swap the model out to disk On if you have spare RAM Try mmap() Map the file into memory for fast loading Leave default CPU Thread Pool Size CPU threads used for any CPU work Default is fine when fully on GPU Offload KV Cache to GPU Memory Keep the context cache in VRAM rather than system RAM On. Turning it off saves VRAM but slows generation a lot. Max Concurrent Predictions How many requests the server handles at once; each needs its own share of the cache 1 for personal use. Raise it only for apps sending parallel requests. Evaluation Batch Size How many prompt tokens are processed per chunk during prefill Default. Larger can speed up long prompts but uses more memory. RoPE Frequency Base / Scale Stretches position encoding to exceed the trained context Leave on auto. Manual values usually hurt quality. Seed Fixes the random choices during sampling Set a number when comparing settings so runs are repeatable Number of Experts (MoE) Experts activated per token Leave default Part 2: sampling (how the next word is chosen) Remember that the model outputs a score for every possible next token. Sampling is the rule that turns those scores into a pick. If you always chose the top token the writing would be flat and repetitive. A little randomness makes it natural. Temperature Temperature scales the scores before they become probabilities. Low temperature sharpens the differences, so the top choice dominates. High temperature flattens them, so unlikely words get a real chance. At zero the model is deterministic. Top-k, top-p and min-p These trim the list of candidates before picking: Top-k keeps only the k most likely tokens. Top-p (nucleus) keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p, for example 0.9. Min-p removes tokens whose probability is below a fraction of the best token's. It adapts nicely to how confident the model is. Starting values Task Temperature Others Facts, extraction, code, data tasks 0 to 0.3 Defaults are fine General chat and Q&A 0.6 to 0.8 Top-p 0.9 to 0.95 Creative writing and brainstorming 0.9 to 1.1 Min-p 0.05 Use the model card Many model authors publish recommended sampling values on the model's page (some thinking models especially). Start there, since they tested those values. Repeat penalty Discourages the model from repeating the same words. A small value (around 1.05 to 1.1) fixes loops. Large values make the text strange. System prompt The system prompt is an instruction the model sees before the conversation: its role, tone and rules. It is part of the context, so a long system prompt costs context length. Keep it short and specific. Presets Once you find values you like, save them as a preset in LM Studio. Make one per use: \"precise\", \"chat\", \"creative\". Switching presets is faster than retyping. Concept: change one knob at a time Ask the same question five times at each setting and watch what changes. Log it in your notes. This habit is what separates tuning from guessing. Check yourself You need the model to extract the same fields from a thousand invoices reliably. Which temperature? 1.2 0 to 0.3 2.0 Extraction wants consistency. Low temperature picks the most likely answer. What does a higher temperature do? Flattens probabilities, giving unlikely tokens a bigger chance Makes the model run hotter Makes the context longer Temperature reshapes the probability distribution. Which setting increases how much context fits in the same VRAM? Top-k Repeat penalty KV cache quantization An 8-bit cache is about half the size of a 16-bit one."},{"id":"ollama","mod":"4","num":"4.3","title":"Ollama on Windows: command line and server","mins":13,"optional":false,"text":"Ollama is the other popular way to run models. It has no chat window of its own, just a tiny command-line tool and a background server, which makes it the favourite for scripts and apps. Learning both tools makes you flexible. What you will learn Install Ollama and run a model in one command Use the everyday commands Know where models are stored and how to move them Set context length, which Ollama keeps small by default Install Download OllamaSetup.exe from ollama.com/download . It installs for your user account without needing administrator rights, starts in the background (look for the llama icon in the tray), and adds ollama to your terminal. The documentation lists Windows 10 22H2 or newer and NVIDIA driver 551.61 or newer. Or: winget install Ollama.Ollama First run ollama run llama3.2 The first time, it downloads the model, then opens an interactive chat. Type /bye to leave. Browse the model list at ollama.com/library . Names look like name:size , for example llama3.2:3b . A name with no tag uses a default size. Everyday commands ollama pull MODEL # download without chatting ollama list # models on disk ollama ps # models loaded now, with VRAM use and CPU/GPU split ollama show MODEL # details: size, quantization, context, template ollama run MODEL --verbose # prints speed after each reply ollama stop MODEL # unload from memory ollama rm MODEL # delete from disk Concept: ollama ps is your friend The PROCESSOR column in ollama ps says for example 100% GPU or 35%/65% CPU/GPU . If any part says CPU, the model did not fully fit and you are in the slow spill zone from lesson 3.3. Where the models live By default in %HOMEPATH%\\.ollama . Models are large, so many people move them to a bigger drive by setting a user environment variable, then restarting Ollama from the tray: [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(\"OLLAMA_MODELS\", \"D:\\ollama\\models\", \"User\") Context length On GPUs with less than 24 GB of memory, Ollama has historically used a small default context window (around 4,096 tokens), which silently truncates long prompts. Raise it explicitly: [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(\"OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH\", \"8192\", \"User\") Or inside a chat: /set parameter num_ctx 8192 . Check the current Ollama docs for the exact default, which has changed between versions. The server Ollama's server listens on http://localhost:11434 . Any program on your PC can call it. Lesson 6.2 shows how. By default it only accepts connections from your own machine, which is what you want. Do not expose it to the network Setting OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 makes it reachable from other devices. The server has no login, so anyone who can reach it can use your GPU and your models. Only do this inside a trusted network or behind a proper authentication proxy. Modelfiles A Modelfile bundles a model with your settings and system prompt as a reusable custom model: FROM llama3.2 SYSTEM You are a concise technical tutor. Answer in plain English. PARAMETER temperature 0.4 PARAMETER num_ctx 8192 ollama create tutor -f Modelfile ollama run tutor LM Studio or Ollama? LM Studio Ollama Best for Exploring, tuning, comparing Scripts, apps, always-on server Interface Full desktop app Command line and API Model source Hugging Face GGUF files Its own library, plus import of GGUF Server port 1234 11434 Using both is normal. Both can read GGUF files, and both offer an OpenAI-compatible API. Check yourself Which command shows whether a loaded model is fully on the GPU? ollama list ollama ps ollama rm ollama ps shows the GPU/CPU split for loaded models. Why set the context length in Ollama explicitly? To change the model's weights To make downloads faster The default may be small and silently cut off long prompts A small default window means anything past it is dropped."},{"id":"hugging-face","mod":"4","num":"4.4","title":"Hugging Face: finding and judging models","mins":11,"optional":false,"text":"Hugging Face is the main place where open models live, a mix of GitHub and an app store for models. Knowing how to read a page there lets you move beyond whatever a tool suggests. What you will learn Search for models and read a model card Judge whether a repository is trustworthy Pick the right file for your hardware Use the model's recommended settings Anatomy of a model page Name and publisher : publisher/model-name . The publisher matters for trust. Model card (the README): what it is, training notes, licence, recommended settings, limitations. Files and versions : the actual files and their sizes. Community : discussions, where people report bugs, problems and benchmarks. Tags and counts : parameter size, downloads and likes. Who made this version? Models come in layers of re-uploads: The original from the lab, such as Qwen, Google or Meta, in safetensors. GGUF conversions , made by community members who specialise in quantizing (several well-known names do this for nearly every release), or by the lab itself. Fine-tunes and merges , where someone trained further or blended models. This includes \"uncensored\" or \"abliterated\" versions (module 5). The model card of a derivative should name its base model . If it does not, treat it with caution. Trust checklist Publisher is the original lab or a known community uploader Many downloads, and a recent update Model card names the base model, licence and how it was made Files are .gguf or .safetensors , not unusual executables The discussion tab has no reports of broken or malicious files Choosing the file For a GGUF repository, choose a quantization from the list so that file size plus 1.5 to 3 GB for cache and overhead fits your VRAM. As a quick check: VRAM minus about 2.5 GB is the largest file you should consider for decent context. LM Studio's Discover tab does this for you with a \"will it fit\" badge. Read the recommended settings Look for a \"Best practices\" or \"Usage\" section. Authors often specify temperature, top-p, and whether to enable thinking. Following them avoids a lot of \"why is this model terrible\" confusion. Gated models and tokens Some models require you to accept a licence on the page before downloading. You do this with a free Hugging Face account. If you use scripts, you create an access token in your account settings. Treat tokens like passwords: never paste them in public places. Downloading from a script pip install -U \"huggingface_hub[cli]\" hf download publisher/model-name-GGUF model-name-Q4_K_M.gguf --local-dir .\\models The tool was renamed from huggingface-cli to hf in newer versions. Run hf --help if the command is not found. Check yourself A repository has no model card, no stated base model and 12 downloads. What should you do? Be cautious and prefer a better-documented alternative Download it because the file is small Run the installer that comes with it Missing provenance is a red flag. There are nearly always well-documented versions of any popular model. You have 12 GB of VRAM. Roughly what is the biggest GGUF file to consider? 12 GB 20 GB About 9 to 9.5 GB Leave 2.5 to 3 GB for cache and overhead."},{"id":"choose-model","mod":"4","num":"4.5","title":"Choosing the right model for the job","mins":12,"optional":false,"text":"With so many models, what you need is not a ranking but a method. This lesson gives you a repeatable way to choose and a quick way to test. What you will learn Know the main kinds of model: base, instruct, thinking, coder, vision, embedding Apply a three-step method for picking a model Run your own small \"shootout\" to compare candidates Read benchmarks with the right amount of scepticism Kinds of model Kind What it is Use it for Base Continues text, does not follow instructions Fine-tuning starting point Instruct / chat Follows instructions and chats Nearly everything Thinking / reasoning Writes a chain of thought before answering Maths, logic, planning. Slower. Coder Tuned on code Programming help Vision (VLM) Accepts images as well as text Screenshots, diagrams, documents Embedding Turns text into vectors for search. Does not chat. Search and RAG (lesson 6.3) A three-step method Fit. Use the lesson 3.3 calculator to list the size classes and quantizations that fit your VRAM with the context you need. Fit the job. Take a model of the right kind for the task. Search for recent models in that size class with strong community feedback. Test on your own tasks. Public benchmarks do not know your work. The mini shootout Pick two or three candidates. Prepare ten real prompts from your work. Run each prompt on each model with the same settings, and score the answers 1 to 5 in a simple table. Also note tokens per second and VRAM used. prompt | model A | model B | model C | notes -------+---------+---------+---------+------ 1 | 4 | 3 | 5 | C wrote cleaner code 2 | 2 | 4 | 4 | A hallucinated an API ... It takes an hour and teaches you more than any leaderboard. Reading benchmarks Benchmarks measure narrow things. A high score on a maths test does not mean a model writes good emails. Some models are tuned to the tests. Treat leaderboards as a way to find candidates, not to pick a winner. Quantized, abliterated or merged versions score differently from the original. Test the exact file you will use. Check the date. Rankings from six months ago are out of date. Rules of thumb that last The newest model in a size class usually beats older ones. Bigger parameter count beats smaller at the same quantization, up to what you can run fast enough. Mixture-of-experts models give the best quality per second when you have the memory. For coding, choose a model that was trained for code or is known to be strong at it. For long documents, check that its real context length is useful, not only advertised. Check yourself Why run your own ten-prompt comparison? Because it is required by Hugging Face Public benchmarks do not measure your tasks It makes the models faster Only your own prompts show which model works for your needs. You want to search your notes by meaning. Which model kind do you need? An embedding model A base model A thinking model Embedding models turn text into vectors that can be compared for similarity."},{"id":"troubleshooting","mod":"4","num":"4.6","title":"Troubleshooting: when it is slow, broken or strange","mins":10,"optional":true,"text":"Every local AI user hits the same dozen problems. Here they are, with the fastest checks first. What you will learn Diagnose slowness, out-of-memory errors and strange output Know where to find logs Build a habit of changing one thing at a time It is extremely slow (a few tokens per second) Almost always the model is partly or fully on the CPU. Check nvidia-smi to see if VRAM rose. In Ollama run ollama ps and look at the processor column. Fix: lower the context length, use a smaller quantization, close other apps using VRAM, or choose a smaller model. Out of memory, failed to load, or crash The model plus cache exceeded VRAM. Lower the context, enable 8-bit KV cache, pick a smaller quantization, close games and video apps, and check that another AI app is not holding a model in memory. The GPU is not used at all Update the NVIDIA driver and restart. On a laptop confirm the app runs on the NVIDIA GPU. In LM Studio check the selected runtime is CUDA. Look at the logs for lines about GPU detection. Gibberish, repeating text or odd tokens at the start Often a wrong chat template , or the wrong kind of model (a base model used as a chatbot). Use the instruct version and let the app auto-detect the template. A very low-bit quantization (Q2 or Q3) can also produce gibberish. Try Q4 or higher. It ignores my long document or my instructions Context is too small and the start was cut off. Raise the context length or shorten the input. Put the key instruction at the end as well as the beginning. It loops or repeats itself Raise the repeat penalty a little (1.05 to 1.1), lower temperature, or check the model card's recommended settings. Some thinking models loop when sampling is wrong. It makes up facts This is expected, especially for small models. Supply the facts in the prompt, ask it to say \"I do not know\" when unsure, and verify anything important. Answers start with a long \"thinking\" section You loaded a reasoning model. You can usually hide or collapse it in the UI, and some models allow a switch or prompt flag to turn thinking off for speed. Disk is full Models are 2 to 50 GB each. In LM Studio delete unused ones from My Models. In Ollama use ollama rm . Consider moving the model folder to a larger drive. The PC gets hot and loud Generation uses the GPU heavily. Make sure the case has airflow, and on a laptop use a hard surface and plug in the charger. Lower the GPU power limit if needed. Where the logs are LM Studio : Developer tab, then the log view. Ollama : %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Ollama contains app.log and server.log . A calm method Write down exactly what you did and what happened. Check VRAM, GPU use and logs before guessing. Change one setting, retest, and note the result. Search the exact error text. Chances are someone solved it. Check yourself A model that used to run fast is now at 3 tokens per second after you raised the context from 8K to 64K. Most likely cause? The model weights got corrupted The internet is slow The cache grew and some layers spilled to the CPU Larger context means a bigger cache, which can push the model over the VRAM limit."},{"id":"why-refusals","mod":"5","num":"5.1","title":"Why models refuse: alignment explained","mins":10,"optional":false,"text":"Sometimes a model says \"I can't help with that.\" Before looking at models that do not do this, you need to understand where refusals come from, because the answer changes how you think about \"uncensored\". What you will learn Explain how a model learns to refuse Separate three layers of behaviour control: weights, system prompts and external filters Understand over-refusal, why learned refusals misfire, and how to spot silent degradation Understand what real safety work is trying to achieve Where refusals come from A model fresh from pre-training (lesson 1.3) has read everything from medical textbooks to hateful forums. It will happily continue any kind of text. The assistant behaviour you know is added afterwards. Instruction tuning teaches the model to answer as a helpful assistant. Preference training (RLHF, DPO and similar) uses human or model ratings of answers to push the model towards helpful, honest, harmless replies, including declining some requests. System prompts add rules at run time (\"never discuss X\"). External filters are separate programs that check inputs and outputs. They exist around cloud products but not inside the weights you download. Concept: refusal is a learned habit, not a wall In an open-weight model, refusals live in the weights, as a trained tendency. There is no separate \"censor\" module to switch off. That is why the community talks about changing the weights (lesson 5.2 and module 10) rather than turning off a setting. The alignment pipeline in more detail Supervised fine-tuning (SFT). The model is trained on curated example conversations, including examples of declining some requests (\"I can't help with that, but here is…\"). It learns the pattern of refusing along with format and tone. Reward model and RLHF. Raters compare two answers to the same prompt. A separate reward model learns to predict which answer people prefer. Reinforcement learning then nudges the chat model toward answers the reward model scores highly, with a penalty that stops it drifting too far. This was popularised by OpenAI's InstructGPT work (2022). DPO. A simpler method that learns directly from chosen-versus-rejected pairs, with no separate reward model. Cheap and stable, and you will run it yourself in lesson 9.7. AI feedback. To save human effort, some labels come from models instead: RLAIF uses a model as the rater, and Anthropic's Constitutional AI has the model critique and revise its own answers against a written list of principles. Reasoning training adds reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers, and safety behaviour is trained alongside it. Why refusals misfire The model never learns a rule like \"refuse if the user is not authorised\", because it cannot check that. It learns statistical associations: requests that look like these got refusals during training. Learned filters generalise by resemblance, so they produce false positives: \"How do I kill a Python process that ignores Ctrl+C?\" (the word \"kill\") \"Write a villain's threatening monologue for my novel\" (threatening language) \"How does this malware sample stay installed, for my incident report?\" (defensive, but alarming keywords) Researchers measure this with benchmarks like XSTest, which contains safe prompts that look risky. Over-refusal shows up in three ways: hard refusals, hedged answers where half the reply is warnings, and the worst kind, silent degradation , where the model appears to help but gives a vaguer or deliberately weakened answer. What safety training is for Developers train refusals for reasons most people agree with: not helping with serious harm such as weapons capable of mass casualties, child exploitation, targeted harassment or fraud, and reducing dangerous misinformation. A model that never refused anything would be a different product, and it would be the same model's builder who is responsible when it is misused. Over-refusal The problem is that the training is blunt. Models sometimes decline harmless requests because they look like harmful ones: a novelist asking for a villain's menacing speech, a nurse asking about drug doses, a security professional asking how a known attack works, a student asking about history's atrocities. This is called over-refusal , and it is the single biggest reason people look for different models. Smaller and older safety-tuned models over-refuse more. Clear context often fixes it: say who you are, what the legitimate purpose is, and what you need. A different aligned model may handle the same request fine. Models differ a lot. Try this before reaching for anything else When a model refuses a legitimate request, rewrite it with the context and purpose, make it specific and professional, and try another well-regarded model. Most over-refusals disappear without changing any weights. Three ways people get around refusals Approach What changes Lasting? Prompt tricks (\"jailbreaks\") Only the text you send No. Per conversation, and providers patch them. Fine-tuning on unfiltered data The weights are trained further Yes, baked into a new model Editing the weights directly (abliteration) A learned direction is removed from the weights Yes, baked into a new model The next lesson explains the last two. Check yourself In a downloaded open-weight model, where does refusal behaviour live? In the weights, as learned tendencies from alignment training In a separate filter program on your PC In the GGUF file's header only There is no external censor to disable. It is part of what the model learned. What is over-refusal? A model refusing to load A model that answers too slowly A model declining harmless requests that merely resemble harmful ones Blunt safety training can misfire on legitimate requests like fiction, medicine and security education."},{"id":"what-uncensored","mod":"5","num":"5.2","title":"What \"uncensored\" and \"abliterated\" models really are","mins":12,"optional":false,"text":"You have seen names like \"uncensored\", \"abliterated\" and \"Dolphin\". Here is what they mean, how they differ, and what you give up, with real measurements instead of hype. What you will learn Describe how uncensored fine-tunes and abliterated models are made, at a conceptual level Know the measured costs in quality and reliability Recognise the naming conventions Understand what these models cannot do Scope of this lesson This lesson explains the technology so you can judge it. It does not give instructions for making a model produce harmful content, and nothing in the course is a reason to do so. How you use any model is your decision and your responsibility. Type 1: uncensored fine-tunes The creator takes a model and trains it further on a dataset from which the refusals have been removed (replies like \"I'm sorry, I can't\" are filtered out). The model then learns to answer instead of declining. The best-known family is Dolphin , started by Eric Hartford. Names often include \"uncensored\". Type 2: abliterated models Researchers found that in many chat models, refusal is mediated largely by a single direction in the model's internal activations (Arditi et al., 2024, \"Refusal in Language Models Is Mediated by a Single Direction\"). Abliteration identifies that direction and removes it from the weights, so the model can no longer represent the \"I should refuse\" signal. It needs no big training run, just a modest set of example prompts and a consumer GPU for a short time. The result is a new model file. Names often end in \"-abliterated\". Concept: it is a weight edit, not a setting This is exactly the kind of weight editing you will do yourself in module 10. It is also a small, concrete example of why interpretability research matters: finding meaningful directions inside a model lets you steer it. What it costs Editing a model has side effects. A June 2026 LessWrong post measured one popular community abliterated model against its base (a 27B Qwen model): Benchmark Base Abliterated Change MMLU (broad knowledge) 84.44% 83.78% −0.66 points TruthfulQA MC1 40.27% 34.52% −5.75 points TruthfulQA MC2 58.25% 51.39% −6.87 points Two lessons follow. Knowledge barely changed, but truthfulness dropped noticeably, so cost is uneven across tasks. And this was one model with, by its author's own description, a crude proof-of-concept edit, so it is not a universal figure. Community reports for other builds describe the damage as usually small but sometimes real: weaker instruction following, more hallucination and more variance between releases. What these models are not Not smarter. Removing refusals adds no knowledge. The model knows exactly what it knew before. Not guaranteed to comply. Some still decline sometimes, and many simply give weaker answers. Not a good default. For ordinary work, a good aligned model with a clear prompt is usually more accurate and more reliable. Why people legitimately want them Fiction and role-play where over-refusal ruins the story. Security, medical or legal education where a cautious model keeps refusing. Red-teaming and research, including measuring how much alignment changes a model. Private offline drafting where they would rather avoid a provider's rules. Synthetic data generation and as a baseline for comparison. Vetting an uncensored model before you trust it Model cards for uncensored releases often make big claims (\"zero refusals\", \"no capability loss\") without saying how the model was made or tested. Treat them like unaudited vendor claims and check what you can: Check How What it tells you Lineage The card's base_model field and licence What it was made from and what you may do with it Chat template Compare the template in its GGUF with the official model's (lesson 9.6 has a script) A changed template can cause odd behaviour File structure Compare file sizes and tensor lists with the same quantization of the official model Same structure means the edit changed weight values, not the architecture Refusals Your own held-out legitimate prompts Whether it actually answers what you need Quality A regression set of normal questions (lesson 9.5) Whether it got worse at everything else Remember what \"0 refusals\" does not mean: it says the model answers, not that the answers are correct. Two more risks to watch for: confident compliance with wrong premises (a model tuned never to say no may also be less inclined to say \"that will not work\"), and sycophancy , the tendency of preference-trained models to agree with the user, which removing refusals does nothing to fix. Spotting them Sign Usually means \"-abliterated\" in the name Weights edited to remove the refusal direction \"uncensored\" in the name Usually a fine-tune on filtered data Dolphin (cognitivecomputations) Uncensored fine-tune family Merge or \"-v2\" with no explanation Check the card. Provenance is unclear. They are hosted on Hugging Face like any other model, mostly as GGUF, and run in LM Studio or Ollama in the same way. Lab: see it for yourself with harmless prompts Download an aligned model and, if you wish, an abliterated version of the same base and size. Run ten benign but edgy prompts, such as a villain's monologue, a morally grey short story, an honest discussion of how a historical atrocity happened, or explaining at a textbook level how a famous cyberattack worked. Score completeness, accuracy and style. Compare tokens per second too. Notes on what actually differed, and whether it mattered for your tasks, are the point. Check yourself What does abliteration change? The text of the system prompt The weights, by removing a direction linked to refusal The model's licence It is a direct weight edit, found through interpretability research. Does an uncensored model know more than its aligned original? No. It has the same knowledge, with fewer refusals and possibly some quality loss Yes, it unlocks hidden knowledge Yes, because it is larger Removing refusals adds nothing. The knowledge was already there. Which is the best first step when a model refuses a legitimate request? Switch to an abliterated model immediately Give up Add the legitimate context and purpose to the prompt, or try another aligned model Many refusals disappear with clear context, and aligned models are usually more reliable."},{"id":"uncensored-safely","mod":"5","num":"5.3","title":"Using unrestricted models responsibly and safely","mins":9,"optional":false,"text":"A model with fewer guardrails shifts the responsibility to you. This lesson covers the practical rules: legal, safety, security and file hygiene. What you will learn Understand where the responsibility sits Apply security habits specific to these models Download and vet files safely Know when not to use one Responsibility Running a model locally is legal in essentially every country. What is regulated is what is done with the output. Fraud, harassment, defamation, child sexual abuse material, and help with serious violence are unlawful whether a person, a script or a model produced them. Laws differ by place, so check your own. This is general information, not legal advice. The cloud providers' policies do not apply on your PC, so the judgement is yours. Hard limits Do not use any model to create sexual content involving minors, to target or harass real people, to produce malware or fraud tools for use against others, or to help harm anyone. Beyond the legal risk, these are plainly wrong, and many hosting platforms will ban you. Security habits Keep the server local. Do not bind Ollama or LM Studio to your network or the internet. An unauthenticated model server is a free GPU for strangers. Treat the model as untrusted. It can be steered by text it reads (prompt injection). If you connect it to tools or files, it may follow instructions hidden in a web page or document. Without refusal training there is even less of a brake. Do not give it dangerous tools. If you build an agent, limit what it can run and ask for confirmation before it deletes, sends or spends anything. Keep separate models for separate jobs. Use a normal aligned model for anything public-facing or shared with other people. File hygiene Prefer GGUF and safetensors, as in lesson 3.2. Avoid pickle formats. Prefer well-known uploaders with many downloads and a documented base model. Check the licence. Fine-tunes inherit the base model's terms, and some bases restrict certain uses. Do not run extra \"launcher\" executables, scripts or cracks bundled with a model download. Where the uploader provides checksums, compare them. Download only from the official repository link, not from links in comments or forums. Test new models in LM Studio or Ollama, not in scripts with access to your files. Good reasons not to use one You need accuracy. Abliterated models can be less truthful (lesson 5.2). You are building something other people use. Keep guardrails in public products. A simple prompt fix would solve your problem. Concept: control and responsibility come together The point of local AI is control. The cost of control is that nobody else is checking. Good practice is to be your own safety layer: local-only server, limited tools, honest use. Check yourself What is the most important network rule for a local model server? Make it public so you can reach it anywhere Keep it bound to your own computer unless you add authentication Disable your firewall Local servers usually have no login, so exposing them gives strangers free access. Which file would you trust least from an unknown uploader? A .gguf with a documented base model A .safetensors from the original lab A .exe \"launcher\" bundled with a model Weights are data. Executables have no place in a model download."},{"id":"prompting","mod":"6","num":"6.1","title":"Prompting small local models well","mins":11,"optional":false,"text":"You already prompt cloud models. Smaller local models need a slightly different approach: clearer, more explicit and more structured. These habits improve results enormously. What you will learn Write a prompt with role, task, context and format Use examples to control style and output Ask for step-by-step reasoning when it helps Fix common failure patterns The four-part prompt Part Example Role You are a careful technical editor. Task Summarise the text below in five bullet points. Context The audience is non-technical managers. Format Return only a numbered list. No introduction. Habits that help small models Be explicit. Smaller models do not infer as much. Say what you want, in what length and shape. Give one task at a time. Break a big job into steps and feed the results forward. Show examples. Two or three input-output examples (few-shot prompting) teach the pattern better than a paragraph of description. Use delimiters. Mark where the document begins and ends so instructions are not confused with content. Ask for step-by-step thinking on logic and maths: \"Work through it step by step, then give the final answer on a new line starting with ANSWER:\". Put instructions last as well. In long prompts, repeat the key instruction after the document. Lower the temperature for precise tasks. A before and after Weak: summarize this Better: You are a careful editor. Summarise the text between the triple quotes in exactly 5 bullet points, each under 20 words. Keep names and numbers exact. Output only the bullets. \"\"\" ...document... \"\"\" Few-shot example Classify each support email as BILLING, BUG, or OTHER. Email: \"I was charged twice this month.\" -> BILLING Email: \"The export button crashes the app.\" -> BUG Email: \"Do you have a student discount?\" -> OTHER Email: \"My invoice shows the wrong company name.\" -> Structured output When code needs to read the answer, ask for JSON and give the exact shape: Return only valid JSON in this shape, with no other text: {\"sentiment\": \"positive|neutral|negative\", \"topics\": [\"...\"], \"confidence\": 0.0} Many tools, including LM Studio and Ollama, can also enforce a JSON schema so the model cannot produce anything else. Lesson 6.5 shows exactly how. Common failures and fixes Symptom Fix Rambling, long answers State a length limit, and say \"Output only…\" Ignores part of the prompt Shorten it. Number the requirements. Invents facts Paste the facts. Add \"If the text does not say, answer UNKNOWN.\" Wrong format Provide an example of exactly the format Inconsistent between runs Lower temperature. Add examples. Concept: prompts are programs Save your best prompts in files, version them, and test changes on the same ten inputs. That is how professionals do it, and it is the basis for the evaluation work in lesson 9.5. Check yourself What most reliably teaches a small model a specific output pattern? A few input-output examples in the prompt Writing the prompt in capital letters Raising the temperature Few-shot examples demonstrate the pattern directly. How do you reduce invented facts when summarising a report? Ask the model to be creative Provide the report text and tell it to answer only from the text Increase context length only Grounding the model in supplied text and allowing \"unknown\" cuts hallucination."},{"id":"local-api","mod":"6","num":"6.2","title":"Use your model from Python: the local API","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"Chatting in an app is fun. The real power arrives when your own scripts can call the model. Both LM Studio and Ollama run a local web server that speaks the same API as OpenAI, so almost any tutorial code works with a one-line change. What you will learn Start the LM Studio and Ollama servers Call them from Python with the OpenAI library and with plain requests Stream tokens as they are generated Build a small reusable function you will use in later modules Start a server LM Studio Ollama Start Developer tab, then \"Start server\", or lms server start Runs automatically after install Base URL http://localhost:1234/v1 http://localhost:11434/v1 Model name Shown in the app, for example the model identifier As in ollama list API key Not checked Not checked In LM Studio, load a model first or enable just-in-time loading. Check it responds: curl http://localhost:1234/v1/models Your first call cd $HOME\\ai-lab .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install openai from openai import OpenAI # LM Studio: port 1234. Ollama: use \"http://localhost:11434/v1\" client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"not-needed\") reply = client.chat.completions.create( model=\"your-model-name\", # copy from the app or \"ollama list\" messages=[ {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a concise tutor.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain VRAM in two sentences.\"}, ], temperature=0.3, ) print(reply.choices[0].message.content) Concept: messages are the memory The model has no memory between calls. To continue a conversation, you append each answer and each new question to the messages list and send the whole list again. This is exactly the \"context window as memory\" idea from lesson 1.4, now written in code. A tiny chat loop from openai import OpenAI client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"x\") messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a friendly tutor.\"}] while True: user = input(\"You: \") if user.strip().lower() in {\"quit\", \"exit\"}: break messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user}) out = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"your-model-name\", messages=messages) text = out.choices[0].message.content print(\"AI:\", text) messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": text}) Notice the list keeps growing. After many turns it will approach the context limit. That is why long chats degrade. Streaming stream = client.chat.completions.create( model=\"your-model-name\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a haiku about GPUs.\"}], stream=True, ) for chunk in stream: print(chunk.choices[0].delta.content or \"\", end=\"\", flush=True) print() Measure speed in code import time start = time.time() out = client.chat.completions.create( model=\"your-model-name\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write 150 words about oceans.\"}], ) secs = time.time() - start tokens = out.usage.completion_tokens print(f\"{tokens} tokens in {secs:.1f}s = {tokens/secs:.1f} tokens/sec\") Native Ollama API Ollama also has its own simpler endpoint: import requests r = requests.post(\"http://localhost:11434/api/generate\", json={\"model\": \"llama3.2\", \"prompt\": \"Why is the sky blue?\", \"stream\": False}, timeout=300) print(r.json()[\"response\"]) Mini project Write ask.py : it reads a text file from the command line, sends it to your model with a summarising prompt at temperature 0.2, prints the summary and the tokens per second. Run it on three files. Save it in ai-lab . You will reuse the pattern constantly. Keep it local The examples use localhost , which only your own PC can reach. Do not change the address to your network address unless you understand the security implications (lesson 6.4). Check yourself To continue a conversation through the API you must: Wait for the model to remember Reload the model every time Send the earlier messages again in the messages list The API is stateless. You supply the history each time. What must change when moving the OpenAI-library code from LM Studio to Ollama? The base URL (port) and the model name The whole library Nothing at all Both speak the same API, so only the address and model name differ."},{"id":"memory-rag","mod":"6","num":"6.3","title":"Memory and RAG: give your model your documents","mins":18,"optional":false,"text":"A model only knows what was in its training data and what is in its context window. \"Memory\" and RAG are ways of choosing which extra text to put in front of it. This lesson explains the memory ladder, then builds a working RAG system over your own notes in two short scripts. What you will learn See the four levels of \"memory\" as one ladder Explain embeddings, chunking and similarity search Build a working RAG pipeline with LM Studio's embeddings endpoint Know the rules that silently break RAG, and how to check retrieval quality The memory ladder Context window (short-term memory). Everything in the current conversation (lesson 1.4). Saved notes. A file of facts about you or your project pasted into every conversation. ChatGPT's \"memory\" feature works much like this. In LM Studio it is a saved system prompt or preset. In Ollama it is the SYSTEM of a Modelfile (lesson 8.2). RAG. Retrieval-Augmented Generation: search your documents for the few passages relevant to the question and paste only those. Agent memory. A program that writes summaries of past conversations into a store and retrieves them later. It is RAG applied to your own history. Concept: every kind of memory is \"put text into the context\" The model itself never remembers. Each memory system decides which text to put in front of it this time. That is also why RAG beats fine-tuning for facts (lesson 8.1): the facts are read, not recalled. Embeddings An embedding model turns a piece of text into a list of numbers (a vector, often 768 or 1,024 long) that captures its meaning. Texts with similar meaning get vectors that point in similar directions, even with no words in common: \"How do I reset my router?\" lands near \"Steps to restart your home Wi-Fi box\". Similarity is measured with cosine similarity : the cosine of the angle between two vectors. 1 means same direction, 0 means unrelated. Embedding model Notes nomic-embed-text-v1.5 Small and fast, ships with LM Studio for its document chat. Needs the prefixes search_document: and search_query: . Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B Multilingual and stronger, still small EmbeddingGemma-300M Compact, with its own prompt format Three rules that break RAG silently Use the same embedding model for documents and questions. If you change the embedding model, re-embed everything. Use the prefixes or instructions the model card asks for. Nothing errors if you forget; results just get worse. Chunking You do not embed whole documents. You cut them into chunks of a few hundred tokens, each about one thing. For Markdown notes, split at headings and pack paragraphs up to about 1,500 characters. Two refinements matter a lot: overlap (repeat the end of one chunk at the start of the next, so facts on the boundary are not lost) and context headers (prefix each chunk with its file name and heading, so a chunk like \"set it to 30 days\" still makes sense alone). The pipeline Ingest : chunk your files, embed each chunk, save vectors and text. Ask : embed the question, find the most similar chunks, put them in the prompt with numbered sources, and ask the model to answer only from them and cite. Setup In LM Studio, download nomic embed text v1.5 (search \"nomic embed\") and a chat model. Load both and start the server. Check the embedding model's exact id with curl http://localhost:1234/v1/models . It usually looks like text-embedding-nomic-embed-text-v1.5 . pip install openai numpy ingest.py \"\"\"ingest.py: chunk your notes, embed them with LM Studio, save to index.npz.\"\"\" import json, pathlib, re import numpy as np from openai import OpenAI client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"lm-studio\") EMBED = \"text-embedding-nomic-embed-text-v1.5\" # check the id with /v1/models NOTES, MAX, OVERLAP = pathlib.Path(\"notes\"), 1500, 200 def chunk_file(path): text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") sections = re.split(r\"(?m)^(#{1,3} .*)$\", text) # keep headings as separators heading, chunks = \"\", [] for part in sections: if re.match(r\"^#{1,3} \", part): heading = part.strip(\"# \").strip() continue body = part.strip() start = 0 while start {heading}]\\n{piece}\") start += MAX - OVERLAP return chunks chunks = [c for f in sorted(NOTES.glob(\"*.md\")) + sorted(NOTES.glob(\"*.txt\")) for c in chunk_file(f)] vectors = [] for i in range(0, len(chunks), 32): # embed in batches batch = [\"search_document: \" + c for c in chunks[i:i + 32]] vectors += [d.embedding for d in client.embeddings.create(model=EMBED, input=batch).data] v = np.array(vectors, dtype=np.float32) v /= np.linalg.norm(v, axis=1, keepdims=True) # normalise once, so dot product = cosine np.savez(\"index.npz\", vectors=v, chunks=np.array(chunks, dtype=object)) print(f\"indexed {len(chunks)} chunks from {NOTES}\") ask_docs.py \"\"\"ask_docs.py: answer a question from your indexed notes, with citations.\"\"\" import sys import numpy as np from openai import OpenAI client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"lm-studio\") EMBED, CHAT, TOP_K = \"text-embedding-nomic-embed-text-v1.5\", \"your-chat-model-id\", 4 idx = np.load(\"index.npz\", allow_pickle=True) vectors, chunks = idx[\"vectors\"], idx[\"chunks\"] question = \" \".join(sys.argv[1:]) or \"What did I write about backups?\" q = np.array(client.embeddings.create(model=EMBED, input=[\"search_query: \" + question]).data[0].embedding) q /= np.linalg.norm(q) scores = vectors @ q best = np.argsort(-scores)[:TOP_K] sources = \"\\n\\n\".join(f\"[{n + 1}] {chunks[i]}\" for n, i in enumerate(best)) for n, i in enumerate(best): print(f\"[{n + 1}] score {scores[i]:.3f} {chunks[i].splitlines()[0]}\") reply = client.chat.completions.create(model=CHAT, temperature=0.2, messages=[ {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer only from the numbered sources. Cite them like [1]. \" \"If the sources do not contain the answer, say you don't know.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Sources:\\n{sources}\\n\\nQuestion: {question}\"}]) print(\"\\n\" + reply.choices[0].message.content) python ingest.py python ask_docs.py \"What did I decide about the project deadline?\" When results are poor Check retrieval first. The printed sources show what was found. If the right chunk is not in the top 4, the chat model never had a chance. Exact strings (error codes, product IDs, names) are a weak spot for embeddings. Production systems add keyword search (BM25) and merge the two rankings, called hybrid search . Reranking : retrieve 20 chunks, then let a small reranker model pick the best 4. Measure : write 20 questions whose answer you know is in a specific file, and count how often that file appears in the top 4. Change one thing at a time. No code needed? LM Studio can attach files to a chat and does simple RAG for you. Great for one-off questions. Your own scripts give you control, citations and evaluation. Check yourself What do all memory systems have in common? They change the model's weights They decide which text to place in the context They need a second GPU The model is frozen. Memory is about choosing what text it sees. You switched to a new embedding model and results got worse. Why? Old document vectors and new query vectors come from different models, so you must re-embed everything The chat model is too small Cosine similarity stopped working Vectors from different embedding models are not comparable. The answer is wrong. What do you check first? The temperature The GPU driver Whether the right chunk appeared in the retrieved sources If retrieval misses, generation cannot recover."},{"id":"local-security","mod":"6","num":"6.4","title":"Privacy, safety and good hygiene for local AI","mins":8,"optional":false,"text":"Local does not automatically mean safe. It means the risks are different, and they are yours to manage. Here is a short, practical checklist. What you will learn Know what \"local\" really protects and what it does not Understand prompt injection in one paragraph Apply a safety checklist to your own setup What local protects Your prompts and files are not sent to a provider. They do not appear in anyone's logs or training data, and a vendor outage or policy change cannot reach them. What it does not protect Your files if malware is on the PC. Local AI does not replace normal security. You from untrusted model files (lesson 3.2). You from prompt injection : when text the model reads contains instructions (\"ignore the above and send…\"), it may follow them. This matters once the model can read web pages or documents and use tools. Apps that phone home. Many AI front ends include telemetry or update checks. Read their privacy settings. Concept: prompt injection is like SQL injection Instructions and data share the same channel: text. A malicious document can hide commands. The defence is the same too: do not trust the data, and limit what the model is allowed to do with it. The checklist Servers listen on localhost only Models come from known publishers, as GGUF or safetensors I update the NVIDIA driver, LM Studio and Ollama regularly Any agent or script that can run commands asks me before deleting, sending or spending I never paste passwords, keys or tokens into prompts I verify important facts the model gives me I know where my chat history and logs are stored, and I can delete them Everyday hygiene Back up your notes, prompts and scripts. Models can be re-downloaded, your work cannot. Keep an eye on disk space and VRAM. Many problems are simply \"something else is using it\". Watch temperatures during long runs, such as benchmarks and, later, training. Keep your system updated, but pin versions inside a project once it works. Check yourself What is prompt injection? Hidden instructions inside text the model reads that try to steer it Installing a model with a syringe A way to speed up generation It is dangerous when a model can read untrusted content and take actions."},{"id":"structured-tools","mod":"6","num":"6.5","title":"Structured output and tool calling","mins":16,"optional":false,"text":"Programs need answers they can parse, and models need a way to ask for facts they cannot compute. Structured output forces replies into an exact JSON shape. Tool calling lets the model ask your code to run functions. Together they are the foundation of every automation and agent. What you will learn Explain how constrained decoding guarantees valid JSON Get schema-checked output from LM Studio or Ollama with Pydantic Explain the tool-calling loop and its wire format Build a working two-tool assistant and handle bad calls safely Constrained decoding At every step the model scores every token (lesson 7.4). Constrained decoding adds a filter: a grammar describes every allowed output, and any token that would break it gets probability zero. Normal sampling then picks among the survivors. Example: your schema says category must be \"billing\" , \"bug\" or \"other\" . After {\"category\": \" , a token like \"complaint\" is impossible no matter how much the model wanted it. Concept: valid is not the same as true The grammar guarantees the shape, not the content. A field can be perfectly valid and completely wrong. Also, the schema constrains output but is not automatically explained to the model, so describe each field in your prompt too. LM Studio and Ollama both run on llama.cpp, which converts a JSON Schema into its grammar format. Keep schemas simple: objects, arrays, strings, integers, booleans and enums. Some advanced JSON Schema features are not supported. Structured output with Pydantic Pydantic lets you define the schema as a Python class, send it, and validate the reply in one line. from typing import Literal from openai import OpenAI from pydantic import BaseModel, Field client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"lm-studio\") # Ollama: port 11434 MODEL = \"your-model-id\" class Ticket(BaseModel): category: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"other\"] = Field(description=\"The kind of request\") urgency: int = Field(ge=1, le=5, description=\"1 = can wait, 5 = urgent\") summary: str = Field(description=\"One sentence, under 20 words\") email = \"Hi, I was charged twice this month and need a refund before Friday.\" r = client.chat.completions.create( model=MODEL, temperature=0, messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Classify support emails. Fields: category, urgency 1-5, one-sentence summary.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": email}], response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"ticket\", \"strict\": True, \"schema\": Ticket.model_json_schema()}}) ticket = Ticket.model_validate_json(r.choices[0].message.content) print(ticket) pip install openai pydantic Tool calling: the concept A model can only produce text. It cannot check the time, do exact arithmetic or read a database. With tool calling you describe tools (name, purpose, argument schema). When the model wants one, it replies with a tool call instead of an answer. Your code runs the real function and sends the result back with role tool . The model then answers, or calls another tool. Concept: the model never executes anything Think of tool calls as requests. Your code decides whether to run them and with what arguments. That is why validating arguments in your tool code is not optional, especially with an uncensored model or untrusted input (lesson 6.4). The wire format Your request includes a tools list. The model replies with tool_calls : each has an id , a function name and arguments as a JSON string. You append that assistant message, then one {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": ..., \"content\": result} message per call. You call the model again with the extended messages. Behind the scenes, the chat template (lesson 7.3) renders the tool definitions and calls into special text the model was trained on. Models trained for tool use (most current instruct models) do this far more reliably than older ones. LM Studio marks such models with a tool-use badge. A working two-tool assistant \"\"\"tools_demo.py: a calculator and a clock, called by a local model.\"\"\" import ast, datetime, json, operator from openai import OpenAI client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"lm-studio\") MODEL = \"your-model-id\" OPS = {ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub, ast.Mult: operator.mul, ast.Div: operator.truediv, ast.Pow: operator.pow, ast.USub: operator.neg} def calculator(expression: str) -> str: \"\"\"Safely evaluate + - * / ** on numbers (no eval of arbitrary code).\"\"\" def ev(n): if isinstance(n, ast.Constant) and isinstance(n.value, (int, float)): return n.value if isinstance(n, ast.BinOp) and type(n.op) in OPS: return OPS[type(n.op)](ev(n.left), ev(n.right)) if isinstance(n, ast.UnaryOp) and type(n.op) in OPS: return OPS[type(n.op)](ev(n.operand)) raise ValueError(\"only numbers and + - * / ** are allowed\") return str(ev(ast.parse(expression, mode=\"eval\").body)) def current_time() -> str: return datetime.datetime.now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\") FUNCS = {\"calculator\": calculator, \"current_time\": current_time} TOOLS = [ {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"calculator\", \"description\": \"Evaluate an arithmetic expression exactly, e.g. '17.5 * 3 + 2'.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"expression\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"expression\"]}}}, {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"current_time\", \"description\": \"Get the current local date and time.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {}}}}, ] def ask(question: str) -> str: messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Use the tools for arithmetic and the current time. Never guess them.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}] for _ in range(5): # hard limit on tool rounds msg = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, tools=TOOLS, temperature=0).choices[0].message if not msg.tool_calls: return msg.content messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": msg.content or \"\", \"tool_calls\": [tc.model_dump() for tc in msg.tool_calls]}) for tc in msg.tool_calls: try: fn = FUNCS.get(tc.function.name) if fn is None: raise ValueError(f\"unknown tool {tc.function.name}\") result = fn(**json.loads(tc.function.arguments or \"{}\")) except Exception as e: # unknown tool, bad JSON, bad arguments result = f\"error: {e}\" print(f\" [tool] {tc.function.name}({tc.function.arguments}) -> {result}\") messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": tc.id, \"content\": str(result)}) return \"Stopped after 5 tool rounds.\" print(ask(\"What time is it, and what is 1234.5 * 17 minus 99?\")) Handling bad calls Malformed JSON or unknown tools : send the error back as the tool result. Models usually correct themselves. Loops : always cap the number of rounds. Dangerous tools : anything that deletes, sends, spends or runs commands should ask a human first. Never pass model output straight into a shell. Lab: add a third tool Add read_note(name) that returns the text of a file from your ai-lab\\notes folder. Validate that the name contains no .. or slashes so the model cannot read files elsewhere. Ask the model a question that needs one of your notes. Check yourself Schema enforcement is on. Can the model still return a wrong value in a valid field? No, the grammar prevents it Yes. The grammar guarantees shape, not truth. Only at high temperature Validate facts separately from format. Who executes a tool call? The model LM Studio automatically Your code, after checking the arguments The model only requests. Your code decides and runs. Why cap the number of tool rounds? To stop endless call loops Because the API requires it To save disk space Small models sometimes call tools repeatedly."},{"id":"gradient-descent","mod":"7","num":"7.1","title":"How a model learns: gradient descent by hand","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"Before you train anything, you should see training happen with numbers small enough to follow. This lesson trains a single weight by hand and then in ten lines of Python. Every fine-tune you run later is this same loop, repeated across billions of weights. What you will learn Explain loss, gradient and learning rate with a worked example Run gradient descent yourself in Python Explain why \"predict the next token\" is the training objective of every LLM Connect these ideas to the numbers you will watch while fine-tuning The setup: one knob Take the smallest possible model: y = w × x . It has one weight, w . You do not know the right value. You only have examples: Input x Correct output y 1 3 2 6 4 12 You can see the answer is 3, but the model cannot. It starts with a guess, say w = 1 , and must find its way there. Step 1: measure how wrong you are (the loss) With w = 1 the model predicts 1, 2 and 4. The errors are 2, 4 and 8. Square them (so negatives do not cancel and big misses count more) and average: (4 + 16 + 64) ÷ 3 ≈ 28. That single number is the loss . Lower is better. Zero means perfect. Concept: loss One number that says how wrong the model is on the examples. Training is nothing more than changing the weights to make this number smaller. When you fine-tune later, the trainer prints the loss every few steps and you watch it fall. Step 2: find which way is downhill (the gradient) If you nudge w up a little, does the loss go up or down? The gradient answers that: it is the slope of the loss with respect to the weight. For this model the slope works out to 2 × average((w·x − y) × x) . At w = 1 : errors × inputs are (−2×1, −4×2, −8×4) = (−2, −8, −32), average −14, times 2 = −28. A negative slope means \"increase w and the loss falls\". Step 3: take a small step and repeat Move the weight against the slope, scaled by a small number called the learning rate : w ← w − learning_rate × gradient . With a learning rate of 0.02: w becomes 1 − 0.02 × (−28) = 1.56. Recompute the loss (now smaller), recompute the slope, step again. After a few dozen steps, w is extremely close to 3. The learning rate is a balancing act Too small and training takes forever. Too large and each step overshoots the valley, so the loss bounces or explodes to nan . You will set exactly this number (for example 2e-4 ) when you fine-tune, and you will meet exactly these symptoms if it is wrong. Run it yourself xs = [1, 2, 4] ys = [3, 6, 12] w, lr = 1.0, 0.02 for step in range(30): preds = [w * x for x in xs] loss = sum((p - y) ** 2 for p, y in zip(preds, ys)) / len(xs) grad = 2 * sum((p - y) * x for p, y, x in zip(preds, ys, xs)) / len(xs) w -= lr * grad if step % 5 == 0: print(f\"step {step:2d} w = {w:.4f} loss = {loss:.4f}\") Try lr = 0.2 and watch it blow up. Try lr = 0.001 and watch it crawl. This is the most useful five minutes in the module. From one knob to billions A real model has billions of weights, so nobody computes slopes by hand. Libraries like PyTorch use backpropagation to compute the slope for every weight at once, automatically. The loop is identical: predict, measure loss, compute gradients, step every weight a little, repeat. Batch : the gradient is computed on a small group of examples at a time, not the whole dataset. Step : one update of the weights. Epoch : one full pass through the dataset. Optimizer : a smarter version of \"step downhill\". Adam (or AdamW) keeps running averages of past gradients so each weight gets a sensible step size. It stores extra numbers per weight, which is why full training needs so much memory. The objective of every LLM For a language model, an \"example\" is a piece of text and the \"correct output\" at each position is simply the next token. The loss measures how much probability the model gave to the token that actually came next. Push that loss down over trillions of tokens and the model has to learn grammar, facts and reasoning patterns, because they all help predict the next word. Concept: fine-tuning uses the same loss When you fine-tune on your own chat examples, the loss is still \"predict the next token\", but only on the assistant's replies. The model gets graded on writing your ideal answers, and its weights drift toward producing them. Lesson 9.4 does exactly this. Overfitting in one sentence If you train too long on too few examples, the model memorises them instead of learning the pattern, like a student who memorised the answer key. You detect it by keeping some examples aside (a validation set ): training loss keeps falling while validation loss starts rising. Check yourself During training the loss suddenly becomes nan . Most likely cause? The dataset is too small The learning rate is too high The GPU is too slow Steps that are too large overshoot and diverge. Lower the learning rate. Training loss falls steadily but validation loss rises. What is happening? The model is learning perfectly The learning rate is too low Overfitting: the model is memorising the training examples Stop earlier, use fewer epochs, or add more varied data. What is the gradient? The slope that says which way to move each weight to reduce the loss The size of the dataset The model's final answer Training steps each weight against its gradient."},{"id":"transformer","mod":"7","num":"7.2","title":"Inside the transformer","mins":15,"optional":false,"text":"You will soon choose which parts of a model to train and which directions to remove from it. That only makes sense once you can picture what is inside. This lesson walks through one token's journey through a transformer, with no maths beyond addition and multiplication. What you will learn Follow a token from embedding to prediction Explain the residual stream, attention and the MLP Name the weight matrices you will target with LoRA and edit with ablation Explain grouped-query attention, hybrid layers and mixture-of-experts at a high level The big picture Embedding. Each token id is looked up in a table and becomes a vector, a list of a few thousand numbers (for example 4,096 for a 9B model, 1,024 for a 0.8B). A stack of blocks. The vector passes through dozens of identical-shaped blocks (layers). Each block has two parts: attention and an MLP . Prediction. The final vector is turned into a score for every token in the vocabulary. That is the \"next token\" output from lesson 1.1. The residual stream: a shared bus Here is the idea that makes everything else click. Each block does not replace the token's vector. It reads the vector, computes something, and adds its result back. The running vector is called the residual stream . Think of it as a shared bus or whiteboard that every layer reads from and writes to. Concept: the residual stream The token's vector after layer N equals the embedding plus everything every layer so far has added. Because it is a sum, a concept the model tracks often shows up as a direction in this space. Module 10 exploits exactly that: find the direction for a behaviour, then add it (steering) or delete it (ablation). Attention: tokens looking at each other A word's meaning depends on context. \"Bank\" means something different after \"river\" than after \"loan\". Attention lets each token pull information from earlier tokens. Each token makes a query (\"what am I looking for?\"), a key (\"what do I contain?\") and a value (\"what will I share?\"). These are produced by three weight matrices called q_proj , k_proj and v_proj . A token compares its query with every earlier token's key. High match means \"pay attention to that one\". It then takes a weighted mix of their values. A fourth matrix, o_proj , writes the result back into the residual stream. A causal mask stops a token from looking at later tokens, so the model cannot cheat by seeing the answer. Multi-head attention runs many of these in parallel, each free to focus on a different kind of relationship. The keys and values of previous tokens are exactly what the KV cache stores (lesson 3.3). That is why the cache grows with context. Grouped-query attention (GQA) Modern models let several query heads share one set of keys and values. With 32 query heads but 8 key/value heads, the KV cache is four times smaller. That \"KV heads\" number is what the lesson 3.3 calculator uses. The MLP: where much of the knowledge lives After attention, each token's vector goes through a small two-layer network applied to that token alone. It expands the vector to a much wider size (for example 4,096 to 12,288), applies a non-linear function, and squeezes it back down. In most current models the matrices are called gate_proj , up_proj and down_proj . Research suggests MLPs act a bit like key-value memories, storing many associations (\"Paris\" → \"France\"). down_proj is the matrix that writes the result into the residual stream. Normalisation and position RMSNorm rescales the vector before each part so numbers stay in a healthy range. Position is injected so the model knows word order. Most current models use a method called RoPE, which rotates queries and keys by an angle that depends on position. The names you will use later Weight Part Reads or writes the stream? Used in embed_tokens Embedding table Writes the first vector Ablation (10.3) q_proj , k_proj , v_proj Attention Read LoRA targets (9.4) o_proj Attention output Writes LoRA, ablation gate_proj , up_proj MLP Read LoRA down_proj MLP output Writes LoRA, ablation lm_head Output scores Reads the final vector Rarely edited The \"writers\" ( embed_tokens , o_proj , down_proj , and in hybrid models the linear-attention out_proj ) are the only places anything enters the residual stream. Remember that, because abliteration edits exactly those. Variations you will meet Hybrid models. Some recent models, including the Qwen3.5 family, mix ordinary attention layers with \"linear attention\" layers (Gated DeltaNet) that keep a fixed-size running memory instead of a growing KV cache. In Qwen3.5 roughly three of every four layers are linear, which makes long context much cheaper. Their output matrix is called out_proj . Mixture-of-experts. The MLP is replaced by many expert MLPs and a router picks a few per token (lesson 2.4). Vision models. A separate vision encoder turns images into vectors that enter the same residual stream. In GGUF land that encoder ships as the mmproj file. Count the parameters A quick sanity check to run in the AI Python environment from lesson 2.3. It downloads only a small model's config and builds an empty skeleton, so it is fast and needs no GPU: from accelerate import init_empty_weights from transformers import AutoConfig, AutoModelForCausalLM cfg = AutoConfig.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\") # any small model id works with init_empty_weights(): model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_config(cfg) total = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters()) print(f\"{total/1e9:.2f} B parameters\") for name, p in list(model.named_parameters())[:12]: print(name, tuple(p.shape)) Read the printed names. You will see embed_tokens , then layers.0.self_attn.q_proj and friends, then layers.0.mlp.down_proj . Those are the exact strings you will put in a LoRA config. Check yourself What does each transformer block do to the residual stream? Reads it and adds its own result to it Deletes it and writes a new one Nothing; only the last layer uses it Every block adds to the running sum. That is why concepts appear as directions you can add or remove. Which matrices write into the residual stream? q_proj and k_proj gate_proj and up_proj embed_tokens , o_proj and down_proj (plus out_proj in hybrid models) Those are the \"writers\". Ablation edits them so a direction can never be written. Why does grouped-query attention make long context cheaper? It removes the MLP Several query heads share keys and values, so the KV cache is smaller It skips half the tokens Fewer KV heads means fewer numbers cached per token."},{"id":"chat-templates","mod":"7","num":"7.3","title":"Base, instruct, thinking models and chat templates","mins":13,"optional":false,"text":"The most common reason a fine-tune \"works in the notebook but is broken in LM Studio\" is the chat template. This lesson explains the hidden text format every chat model expects, so your training data and your inference match exactly. What you will learn Distinguish base, instruct and thinking models and how each is made Read a rendered chat template with its special tokens Know where the template lives and what breaks when it is wrong Decode model names and read a model card for training-relevant facts One pretraining run, three kinds of model Kind Made by Behaviour Train on it when… Base Pretraining only Continues any text. Does not follow instructions. You want full control and have lots of data Instruct / chat Base + SFT + preference tuning Follows instructions, uses a chat format Almost always. It already knows how to be an assistant. Thinking / reasoning Instruct + reasoning training Writes a hidden chain of thought, then answers You also train with reasoning in most examples Many recent models are hybrid: one model with a switch (often called enable_thinking ) that turns thinking on or off through the chat template. What a chat template is The model does not see \"messages\". It sees one long string. The chat template is a small program (written in a language called Jinja) that turns a list of messages into that string with special marker tokens. For Qwen-family models a two-turn chat renders roughly like this: <|im_start|>system You are a concise tutor.<|im_end|> <|im_start|>user What is VRAM?<|im_end|> <|im_start|>assistant <think> </think> VRAM is the graphics card's own memory.<|im_end|> <|im_start|> and <|im_end|> are special tokens : single token ids that mark the start and end of each turn. The role (system, user, assistant, and sometimes tool) follows the start marker. The empty <think></think> block is how this family represents \"thinking turned off\". Generation stops when the model produces the end-of-turn token. That is the end-of-sequence (EOS) behaviour. Other families use different markers (Llama, Gemma and Mistral each have their own). You never type these by hand. The tokenizer does it with apply_chat_template . See it yourself from transformers import AutoTokenizer tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\") msgs = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a concise tutor.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is VRAM?\"}] print(tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)) add_generation_prompt=True appends the opening of the assistant turn so the model knows it is its turn to speak. For training data you set it to False because the assistant answer is already included. Where the template lives In a Hugging Face model folder: inside tokenizer_config.json or a separate chat_template.jinja . In a GGUF: copied into the file's metadata ( tokenizer.chat_template ) at conversion time. In LM Studio: read from the GGUF, and overridable per model under My Models, then the settings for Prompt Template. In Ollama: the TEMPLATE in the Modelfile, usually filled in automatically from the GGUF. What breaks with a wrong template Symptom Cause Model keeps talking past its answer, or starts writing the user's next message EOS token not recognised Literal <|im_end|> or similar appears in the output Special tokens treated as plain text Fine-tune behaves like the original model Inference uses a different format from training, so the learned pattern never triggers Unexpected thinking text, or none when you want it Thinking switch differs between training and inference Concept: train and serve with the same template Your training script renders examples with the model's own template. LM Studio or Ollama must render conversations with the same template, the same system prompt style and the same thinking setting. Lesson 9.6 shows how to check the template inside your exported GGUF. Decoding model names A name like Qwen3.5-9B-Instruct-abliterated-Q4_K_M.gguf breaks down into family and version (Qwen3.5), size (9B), kind (Instruct), modification (abliterated), quantization (Q4_K_M) and format (gguf). Other tags you will see: -it (instruct), -Base , -A3B (active parameters in a mixture-of-experts model), -VL (vision-language), -Coder . Read a model card like a trainer Before fine-tuning a model, find these on its card: the licence, whether it is base or instruct, its maximum context, its recommended sampling settings, whether it has a thinking switch, and whether official safetensors weights exist. You can only train from safetensors, never from a GGUF. Check yourself Your fine-tune prints <|im_end|> and keeps going. What is wrong? The learning rate was too high The chat template or end-of-sequence token does not match at inference The model is too small The runtime is not treating the end-of-turn marker as the stop token. Which file can you fine-tune from? A Q4_K_M GGUF An Ollama blob The original safetensors weights Training libraries need the full-precision Hugging Face weights. GGUF is an inference format. What does add_generation_prompt=True do? Adds the opening of the assistant turn so the model starts replying Generates training data Turns thinking on It appends the assistant header. For training examples that already include the answer, set it to False."},{"id":"generation","mod":"7","num":"7.4","title":"How text gets generated, token by token","mins":13,"optional":true,"text":"You have tuned temperature and watched tokens per second. This lesson opens the loop that produces every reply: from raw scores to probabilities, how one token is picked, why the first token is slow and the rest are fast, and why models sometimes loop forever. What you will learn Turn logits into probabilities with softmax Explain greedy decoding versus sampling Explain prefill versus decode and what the KV cache saves Explain how generation stops and how to fix repetition loops From logits to probabilities At each step the model outputs one raw score, a logit , for every token in its vocabulary (often 150,000 to 250,000 of them). Scores can be any number. Softmax turns them into probabilities: raise e to each score, then divide by the total so everything adds up to 1. Token Logit e logit Probability Paris 5.0 148.4 0.84 the 3.0 20.1 0.11 a 2.0 7.4 0.04 Lyon 0.5 1.6 0.01 Temperature (lesson 4.2) divides every logit before softmax. Dividing by 0.5 doubles the gaps, so Paris takes almost everything. Dividing by 1.5 shrinks the gaps, so the others get a real chance. Picking a token Greedy decoding always takes the top token. Deterministic, good for extraction and code, but prone to bland, repetitive text. Sampling draws a token at random according to the probabilities, after filters like top-k, top-p and min-p (lesson 4.2). Natural-sounding, slightly different every time. A fixed seed makes sampling repeatable, which is useful when you compare settings. The autoregressive loop Tokenize the prompt (with the chat template, lesson 7.3). Run the model, get logits for the next position, pick a token. Append the token to the input and repeat. Stop when a stop condition is met. \"Autoregressive\" means each new token depends on all previous ones, including the ones the model just wrote. That is why it cannot go back and fix an earlier sentence. Prefill versus decode Prefill (reading the prompt) Decode (writing the answer) Work All prompt tokens processed in parallel One token per step Limited by GPU compute Memory bandwidth: every step reads all active weights (lesson 3.4) You notice it as Time to first token, longer with big prompts Tokens per second The KV cache Without a cache, every new token would require recomputing attention for the whole conversation. Instead the model stores each processed token's keys and values (lesson 7.2) and only computes the new token's. That is the KV cache: it turns quadratic work into roughly constant work per token, at the cost of VRAM that grows with context (lesson 3.3). It is also why sending a long conversation again is cheaper when the runtime can reuse a cached prefix. How generation stops The model produces the end-of-sequence or end-of-turn token. This is the normal way. A maximum token limit is reached ( max_tokens ). The answer is cut off. A stop string you configured appears. If the template is wrong, the model never produces a recognised stop token and keeps talking (lesson 7.3). Repetition loops Small models, low temperatures and long reasoning chains can fall into loops: once a phrase repeats, it becomes the most likely continuation of itself. Fixes, in order: use the sampling values on the model card, raise temperature slightly, add a small repeat or presence penalty (about 1.05 to 1.1), shorten the context, or switch to a larger model. Module 10 shows that loops can even correspond to a direction you can remove from the weights. Watch it happen This script runs greedy decoding by hand and prints the top candidates at each step. Run it in the AI Python environment from lesson 2.3. It takes seconds on your GPU: import torch from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer name = \"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\" tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name) model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, dtype=(torch.bfloat16 if torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported() else torch.float16), device_map=\"cuda\") text = tok.apply_chat_template([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"The capital of France is?\"}], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=False) ids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.to(\"cuda\") for step in range(8): with torch.no_grad(): logits = model(ids).logits[0, -1].float() probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=-1) top = torch.topk(probs, 5) shown = \", \".join(f\"{tok.decode(int(i))!r} {p:.2f}\" for p, i in zip(top.values, top.indices)) print(f\"step {step}: {shown}\") ids = torch.cat([ids, top.indices[:1].view(1, 1)], dim=1) # greedy: take the top token print(tok.decode(ids[0], skip_special_tokens=True)) This simple loop has no KV cache, so it recomputes everything each step. That is fine for 8 tokens and a good illustration of why real runtimes cache. Check yourself Why is the first token slower to appear when you paste a long document? The model is downloading Prefill must process every prompt token before decoding starts Temperature is too high Time to first token grows with prompt length. What does the KV cache save? Recomputing keys and values for all earlier tokens at every step Disk space The model's weights It trades VRAM for much less computation per token. A reply ends mid-sentence. Which stop condition most likely fired? End-of-sequence token A stop string The maximum token limit Raise max_tokens if answers are cut off."},{"id":"customize-ladder","mod":"8","num":"8.1","title":"The customization ladder: choose the right tool","mins":10,"optional":false,"text":"\"Customize my model\" can mean six very different things, from a one-line system prompt to editing weights. Each rung is more powerful, more expensive and harder to undo than the one below. This lesson helps you pick the lowest rung that does the job. What you will learn Name the six ways to change a model's behaviour Match a goal to the cheapest method that achieves it Know what fine-tuning is good and bad at before you spend hours on it The six rungs Rung What changes Effort Undo Lesson 1. Prompt and system prompt The text you send Minutes Instant 6.1 2. Preset or Modelfile Saved prompt, settings and template, packaged as a \"custom model\" Minutes Instant 8.2 3. RAG (your documents) Which facts go into the context Hours Instant 6.3 4. Activation steering A vector added inside the model while it runs An hour Instant (remove the vector) 10.2 5. Fine-tuning (LoRA) A small trained add-on to the weights Hours to days, a dataset, a GPU Keep the base and the adapter Module 9 6. Weight editing (ablation, merging) The weights themselves Hours, careful evaluation Keep the original file 10.3 to 10.5 Pick by goal You want the model to… Start with Use a persona, tone or language System prompt, then a Modelfile Know your documents, products or notes RAG. Not fine-tuning. Always answer in a strict format (JSON, a report skeleton) Prompt with examples. If it still slips, fine-tune. Be reliably shorter, more formal, less hedging Prompt, then steering, then fine-tuning Do a narrow task very consistently (classify, extract, rewrite in your house style) Fine-tuning Stop refusing legitimate requests Better context first, then an uncensored base, abliteration or fine-tuning (module 10) Combine skills from two fine-tunes Model merging (10.5) What fine-tuning is good at, and bad at This table saves more wasted GPU hours than anything else in the course. Good fit Poor fit Style and voice : your writing tone, your team's report wording Adding facts : product lists, recent events, your company's data. Use RAG. Format : always produce the same JSON keys or headings Facts that change . Retraining every week is pointless. Use RAG or tools. Narrow task skill : classify tickets, summarise code, extract fields Something one sentence of prompt fixes Habits : always check X before Y, always say \"unknown\" when unsure Making a small model as smart as a frontier model . It will not happen. Behaviour such as refusals, verbosity, hedging Why fine-tuning facts backfires A few hundred examples mentioning a fact do not reliably store it. The model learns that it should sound confident about that kind of detail, then invents details it never saw. Research has found that fine-tuning on knowledge the model did not already have tends to increase hallucination. Keep facts in retrieval, where you can inspect and update them, and use fine-tuning to teach the model how to work with them. A decision checklist Have I written a clear system prompt with two or three examples, and tested it on ten real inputs? Is the missing piece knowledge ? If yes, build RAG. Have I tried a newer or larger model that still fits my GPU? Is it one behavioural axis (length, tone)? Try steering. Do I have, or can I make, a few hundred high-quality examples of exactly the behaviour I want? If yes, fine-tune. Check yourself You want a model that answers questions about your 300-page product manual. What is the right tool? Fine-tune on the manual A longer system prompt RAG over the manual Facts belong in retrieval. Fine-tuning teaches habits, not reliable knowledge. Your model ignores a format instruction one time in five, even with examples. What next? Fine-tune on a few hundred correct examples of that format Pre-train a new model Raise the temperature Consistent formats are a classic fine-tuning win once prompting plateaus."},{"id":"modelfiles","mod":"8","num":"8.2","title":"Custom models with Modelfiles and presets","mins":13,"optional":false,"text":"You can build a \"custom model\" in five minutes with no training at all: a base model plus your system prompt, settings, and optionally a LoRA adapter, packaged under its own name. This is also how you will ship your fine-tunes later. What you will learn Write an Ollama Modelfile with every useful instruction Import any GGUF from Hugging Face or your own training into Ollama Attach a LoRA adapter without merging Do the equivalent in LM Studio with presets and imports The Modelfile instructions Instruction What it does Example FROM The base: a library model or a local GGUF file FROM ./my-model-Q4_K_M.gguf SYSTEM Default system prompt SYSTEM You are a terse code reviewer. PARAMETER Sampling and context settings PARAMETER temperature 0.3 TEMPLATE The chat template, in Go template syntax Usually inherited automatically ADAPTER A LoRA adapter applied on top of FROM ADAPTER ./my-lora.gguf MESSAGE Example turns placed before every conversation (few-shot) MESSAGE user Hi LICENSE Licence text shipped with the model Example 1: a persona on a library model FROM llama3.2 SYSTEM \"\"\"You are Basecamp Tutor. Explain AI concepts in plain English, use one short example, and end with a one-line summary.\"\"\" PARAMETER temperature 0.5 PARAMETER num_ctx 8192 PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.08 MESSAGE user What is a token? MESSAGE assistant A token is a small chunk of text, about three quarters of a word. \"Unbelievable\" might be three tokens. Summary: models read and write tokens, not letters. ollama create tutor -f Modelfile ollama run tutor ollama show tutor --modelfile # print what Ollama stored Example 2: import any GGUF Download a GGUF from Hugging Face (lesson 4.4), put it next to a Modelfile, and point FROM at it: FROM ./Some-Model-9B-Q4_K_M.gguf PARAMETER num_ctx 8192 Ollama reads the chat template from the GGUF metadata. If replies look wrong (lesson 7.3), copy the TEMPLATE block from a known-good model of the same family with ollama show FAMILY --modelfile . Shortcut Ollama can also pull GGUF files directly from Hugging Face: ollama run hf.co/USER/REPO:Q4_K_M . Example 3: base model plus your LoRA adapter After module 9 you will have an adapter. Instead of merging it, you can attach it: FROM ./Qwen3.5-2B-Q8_0.gguf ADAPTER ./my-lora-F16.gguf SYSTEM You are my house-style editor. The base must match An adapter only works on the exact base model it was trained on (same family, size and version). Applying it to a different model gives garbage or nothing. Keep a note of the base for every adapter you make. The same in LM Studio Presets save a system prompt plus sampling and load settings. Make one per job and switch in the chat sidebar. Importing your own GGUF : lms import .\\my-model-Q4_K_M.gguf --copy --user-repo you/my-model . It appears under My Models. Prompt Template override per model, under My Models settings, if the auto-detected template is wrong. At the time of writing, LM Studio's documentation does not describe loading a separate LoRA file onto a model, so for LM Studio you merge the adapter first (lesson 9.6). Which to use Need Ollama LM Studio A named custom model other apps can call Modelfile + ollama create Import + preset Try an adapter without merging ADAPTER Not supported, so merge Share it with a friend Send the GGUF and Modelfile Send the GGUF and the preset Lab Build the tutor model above and ask it three questions. Make a second version with temperature 1.0 and compare. Import one GGUF you downloaded in module 4 with a Modelfile, and confirm with ollama ps that it runs 100% on the GPU. Check yourself Which Modelfile instruction attaches a LoRA adapter? FROM ADAPTER PARAMETER ADAPTER applies a LoRA on top of the FROM base. Your adapter was trained on Qwen3.5-2B. Can you attach it to a Llama 3B GGUF? No. An adapter only fits the exact base it was trained on. Yes, any model works Yes, if the context length matches The adapter's matrices are shaped and learned for one specific base."},{"id":"training-101","mod":"9","num":"9.1","title":"Training 101: LoRA, QLoRA and what fits your GPU","mins":15,"optional":false,"text":"This lesson turns the gradient-descent idea from lesson 7.1 into the practical facts of fine-tuning on a home NVIDIA GPU: why full training is out of reach, how LoRA makes it possible, and exactly how much VRAM you need. What you will learn Explain why full fine-tuning needs far more memory than inference Explain LoRA with the matrix picture, and count its parameters Explain QLoRA and when not to use it Size a training run for your own card Why full fine-tuning does not fit Training every weight of a model with the Adam optimizer needs, per weight: the weight itself, its gradient, and two running averages for Adam, plus a full-precision master copy in many setups. That is roughly 16 bytes per parameter. For a 9B model: about 150 GB, before counting the activations of your training text. No home GPU comes close. LoRA: train a small patch Concept: LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) Freeze every original weight. Next to each chosen matrix W, add two small trainable matrices, A and B. The layer now computes W·x + (alpha/r) · B·(A·x) . Only A and B are trained. Together they are the adapter : a file of tens of megabytes that you can keep, swap, stack or merge into W later. Think of the base model as a signed program you never modify, and the adapter as a patch file. The matrix picture W (frozen): 4096 x 4096 = 16,777,216 numbers A: 16 x 4096 = 65,536 trainable B: 4096 x 16 = 65,536 trainable LoRA total = 131,072 (0.78% of W) x (4096) --A--> 16 numbers --B--> 4096 numbers (bottleneck width r = 16) Rank r is the width of that bottleneck: how many independent \"directions of change\" each adapter can learn. Typical values are 8 to 64. Alpha scales the adapter's effect by alpha / r . Raising alpha acts like raising the learning rate for the adapter. B starts at zero, so at step 0 the adapter changes nothing and the model behaves exactly like the base. Across a whole 9B model, a rank-16 adapter on the MLP layers is about 25 million trainable parameters, around a quarter of one percent. The optimizer state for that is under half a gigabyte instead of 150 GB. That is the entire reason individuals can fine-tune at all. Which matrices get adapters The setting is called target modules . Current practice is to target both attention and MLP projections ( q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj ), which tends to beat attention-only LoRA. Hybrid models add their own names, such as out_proj for Qwen3.5's linear-attention layers. You met these names in lesson 7.2. QLoRA: shrink the frozen part too Concept: QLoRA LoRA on top of a base model held in 4-bit. The frozen weights take about a quarter of the memory. They are expanded on the fly for each calculation, while the small adapter stays in 16-bit and receives all the updates. QLoRA is the standard way to fit 7B to 8B models of many families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral) onto consumer cards. One important exception: Unsloth's guide for the Qwen3.5 family says it does not recommend 4-bit QLoRA training for those models because of larger-than-normal quantization differences, and recommends 16-bit LoRA instead. Always check the current guide for the model family you train. What fits your card Unsloth's published figures for 16-bit LoRA on the Qwen3.5 family (approximate, at modest sequence length and batch size 1 to 2): Model VRAM for 16-bit LoRA Fits on Qwen3.5-0.8B ≈ 3 GB Almost any NVIDIA card, including 4 GB cards (tight) Qwen3.5-2B ≈ 5 GB 8 GB cards Qwen3.5-4B ≈ 10 GB 12 GB (tight), 16 GB comfortable Qwen3.5-9B ≈ 22 GB 24 GB (tight, short sequences), or rent 40 to 48 GB For other families with QLoRA, 7B to 8B models typically need roughly 8 to 12 GB depending on sequence length. Check Unsloth's table for the exact model. Training is stricter than running Running a model can spill into system RAM (lesson 3.3). Training cannot do that usefully: the weights, adapter, gradients and activations all need to be in VRAM. So a 4 GB card that happily runs a 12B model can only train about 1B-class models. On a 4 GB card (GTX 1650 and similar) Train locally : models under about 1B, such as Qwen3.5-0.8B or Qwen3-0.6B, with short sequences (512 to 1,024 tokens) and batch size 1. Perfect for learning the whole pipeline. Train 2B to 4B for free on Google Colab's 16 GB T4 GPU with Unsloth's notebooks, then download the GGUF and run it on your PC. A 4B fine-tune runs fully on your GPU; even a 9B one runs with offload. No bf16 : GTX 16-series and RTX 20-series cards (Turing) have no native bfloat16 support, so training uses float16 instead. Unsloth detects this automatically. The free Colab T4 is the same generation, which is why Unsloth's free notebooks work this way too. Memory levers when you run out Shorter max_seq_length (activations grow with it). Batch size 1 with more gradient accumulation steps. Gradient checkpointing (recompute instead of store). Unsloth enables its own version. An 8-bit optimizer ( adamw_8bit ). A smaller model. A well-trained 2B often beats a badly-fitted 9B. The key settings in one table Setting Meaning Starting value Learning rate Step size (lesson 7.1) 2e-4 for short LoRA runs Epochs Passes through the data 1 to 3 Batch × accumulation Examples per weight update 2 × 4 = 8 Rank r / alpha Adapter capacity / strength 16 / 16 (raise r to 32 for harder skills) Max sequence length Token cap per example Long enough for your longest example Warmup Ramp the learning rate up gently at the start 5 to 10% of steps Catastrophic forgetting Training hard on a narrow task can erode general abilities: the model gets great at your JSON format and worse at everything else. LoRA forgets less than full fine-tuning, but it still happens. Protect against it with few epochs, a modest learning rate, some general examples mixed into your data, and a \"regression set\" of unrelated questions you check after training (lesson 9.5). Check yourself Why does LoRA make fine-tuning possible on a home GPU? It removes the need for data Only a tiny add-on is trained, so gradients and optimizer state are tiny It runs on the CPU Optimizer memory scales with trainable parameters, and LoRA cuts those to well under 1%. You want to fine-tune Qwen3.5-4B on a 12 GB card. Which setup matches Unsloth's guidance? 4-bit QLoRA, to save memory Full fine-tuning 16-bit LoRA with short sequences and batch size 1 Unsloth advises against QLoRA for Qwen3.5. About 10 GB is needed, so keep activations small. At training step 0, how does a LoRA model behave? Exactly like the base model, because B starts at zero Randomly It refuses everything B·A is zero until training grows it."},{"id":"dataset","mod":"9","num":"9.2","title":"Build your training dataset","mins":18,"optional":false,"text":"Your fine-tune can never be better than its data. A dataset is a specification of behaviour, written as examples. This lesson covers the file format, how many examples you need, where they can come from, and a script that turns your own notes into a clean dataset using your local model as a teacher. What you will learn Write examples in the chat JSONL format trainers expect Judge how much data a goal needs and what \"quality\" means Generate synthetic examples with a local teacher model, then clean and split them Produce train.jsonl , valid.jsonl and test.jsonl The format: chat JSONL JSONL means one complete JSON object per line. Each line is one example conversation: {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are my house-style editor.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Rewrite: the meeting got moved cause of the outage\"}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"The meeting has been rescheduled because of the outage.\"}]} Use this chat format by default. The trainer renders each example with the model's own chat template (lesson 7.3) and computes the loss only on the assistant's reply. Two other formats exist: {\"prompt\": ..., \"completion\": ...} pairs, and {\"text\": ...} for raw text when you want the model to continue documents (continued pretraining, lesson 9.7). Watch the length Examples longer than the training sequence length get truncated, so the model learns answers that stop mid-sentence. Estimate 1 token ≈ 4 characters and drop or split long examples before training. How much data? Goal Typical size Lock in a format or house style 100 to 500 examples A narrow task skill 500 to 3,000 A broad domain assistant 5,000 and up, mixed with general data Quality beats quantity. A well-known 2023 study (LIMA) fine-tuned a strong base model on only 1,000 carefully chosen examples and got results that held up against far larger datasets. Every inconsistency in your data is also learned. What quality means Correct. Every claim true. If 10% of answers contain an invented fact, the model learns that inventing is part of the job. Consistent. Same system prompt, same headings, same tone in every example. Representative. Inputs look like what you will really type. Diverse. Many topics, lengths and phrasings, so the model learns the pattern, not the instances. Honest. Include examples where the right answer is \"I don't know\" or \"not enough information\". Where data can come from Your own writing : notes, emails, documents, code you wrote. The best source, because it is your voice. Openly licensed datasets on Hugging Face. Check each licence. Some forbid commercial use. Synthetic data drafted by a model and reviewed by you. Never other people's private data, credentials, client documents, or a benchmark you plan to evaluate on. Training on your test set makes your scores meaningless. Teacher models and terms of service If a cloud model (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) drafts your examples, check its current terms. Several providers restrict using outputs to build competing models. A local open-weight teacher avoids that question and keeps your data private. Synthetic data with a local teacher The teacher writes examples; the student is the model you train. The safest pattern with a small local teacher is grounded generation : give it a chunk of your text and ask for question-and-answer pairs whose facts come only from that chunk. Your text supplies correctness. The model supplies phrasing and variety. LM Studio can force the reply into an exact JSON shape (structured output), which makes parsing reliable. The script: make_dataset.py Put your notes as .md or .txt files in ai-lab\\finetune\\notes . Start LM Studio's server with a capable model loaded (lesson 6.2). Edit SYSTEM and STYLE , then run. \"\"\"Turn your notes into chat-format train/valid/test JSONL using a local teacher model.\"\"\" import argparse, hashlib, json, pathlib, random, re from openai import OpenAI SYSTEM = \"You are a helpful assistant that answers in my personal style.\" # the prompt your fine-tune will use STYLE = \"Answer clearly in 2-5 sentences, plain English, no bullet lists.\" # how answers should read SCHEMA = {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"pairs\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"question\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"answer\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"question\", \"answer\"]}}}, \"required\": [\"pairs\"]} SECRETS = [r\"-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----\", r\"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\", r\"eyJ[\\w-]+\\.[\\w-]+\\.[\\w-]+\", r\"(?i)(password|api[_-]?key|token)\\s*[:=]\\s*\\S+\", r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+\"] def chunks(text, size=2500): buf = \"\" for para in re.split(r\"\\n\\s*\\n\", text): if len(buf) + len(para) > size and buf: yield buf buf = \"\" buf += para + \"\\n\\n\" if buf.strip(): yield buf def scrub(s): for pat in SECRETS: s = re.sub(pat, \"[REDACTED]\", s) return s def main(): ap = argparse.ArgumentParser() ap.add_argument(\"--notes\", default=\"notes\") ap.add_argument(\"--out\", default=\"data\") ap.add_argument(\"--model\", default=\"local-model\") ap.add_argument(\"--per-chunk\", type=int, default=4) a = ap.parse_args() client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:1234/v1\", api_key=\"lm-studio\") by_source, seen = {}, set() for f in sorted(pathlib.Path(a.notes).glob(\"*.*\")): if f.suffix.lower() not in {\".md\", \".txt\"}: continue for chunk in chunks(scrub(f.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"))): prompt = (f\"From the text below, write {a.per_chunk} varied question-and-answer pairs. \" f\"Use ONLY facts stated in the text. {STYLE}\\n\\nTEXT:\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n{chunk}\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\") r = client.chat.completions.create( model=a.model, temperature=0.7, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}], response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"qa\", \"schema\": SCHEMA}}) try: pairs = json.loads(r.choices[0].message.content)[\"pairs\"] except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, TypeError): continue for p in pairs: q, ans = scrub(p[\"question\"].strip()), scrub(p[\"answer\"].strip()) key = hashlib.md5(re.sub(r\"\\W+\", \" \", q.lower()).encode()).hexdigest() if len(ans) cd $HOME\\ai-lab\\finetune python make_dataset.py --notes notes --out data --model \"the-model-id-from-LM-Studio\" Clean before you train Validate. Every line parses, every example has system, user and assistant. Filter lengths. The script drops very short answers. Also drop ones too long for your sequence length. Scrub secrets and personal data. The script catches common patterns. Add your own names and domains. Deduplicate. Duplicates silently over-weight one example. Make the style consistent. One system prompt, one tone. Read at least 30 to 50 random examples yourself. Fix or delete bad ones. Nothing replaces this. Splits and leakage The script splits by source file , about 80/10/10. If one document produced ten examples and they landed in both train and test, your test would measure memorisation, not skill. valid.jsonl is checked during training to spot overfitting. test.jsonl is touched only once, at the end (lesson 9.5). Lab Collect 10 to 30 documents of your own writing into notes . Run the script and open train.jsonl in VS Code. Review 30 examples. Delete anything wrong. Note what the teacher got wrong most often and adjust STYLE . Aim for at least 200 good training examples before moving on. Check yourself Why split by source document rather than by example? To stop near-identical examples from one document leaking into the test set To make training faster Because trainers require it Leakage makes the test measure memorisation instead of skill. What does grounded generation protect against? Slow training Large files The teacher inventing facts, since facts must come from your supplied text Your text supplies correctness. The model only phrases it. 500 examples, 50 of which contain wrong facts. What does the model learn? To ignore the wrong ones automatically That stating wrong facts confidently is part of the job Nothing, the dataset is too small Everything in the data is learned, including mistakes."},{"id":"train-env","mod":"9","num":"9.3","title":"Set up your training environment on Windows","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"Training tools are developed on Linux first. On Windows you have three good options. This lesson sets up the one most tutorials assume (WSL2 with your NVIDIA GPU), shows the cloud alternative, and proves everything works before you spend time on a real run. What you will learn Choose between WSL2, native Windows and a cloud GPU Set up WSL2 with GPU access, Python and Unsloth Verify PyTorch sees your GPU Rent a cloud GPU safely when your card is too small Three options Option Good for Watch out for WSL2 (Ubuntu) on your PC Following tutorials as written, using your own GPU Keep files in the Linux filesystem for speed Native Windows Python Simple PyTorch scripts (lesson 10.2 and 10.3 work here) Some training libraries lag on Windows Cloud GPU (Colab, rented) Models too big for your card Someone else's computer: upload only data you are comfortable leaking WSL2 with your NVIDIA GPU Update your Windows NVIDIA driver (lesson 2.2). That driver also serves WSL2. In PowerShell: wsl --install (installs Ubuntu), then restart. Open Ubuntu from the Start menu, create a user, then check the GPU: nvidia-smi # should show your card, from inside Linux Do not install a Linux NVIDIA driver inside WSL The Windows driver provides GPU access to WSL2. Installing a Linux display driver inside Ubuntu breaks it. PyTorch and Unsloth bring the CUDA libraries they need. Python and Unsloth inside WSL2 sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y python3-venv python3-pip git build-essential mkdir -p ~/ai-lab/finetune && cd ~/ai-lab/finetune python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install --upgrade pip pip install --upgrade unsloth unsloth_zoo python -c \"import torch, unsloth, transformers, trl; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available(), transformers.__version__)\" If the last line prints True , you are ready. Unsloth's installation steps change with CUDA and PyTorch versions, and newer model families need recent Transformers versions (Qwen3.5 needs Transformers 5). If anything fails, copy the install cell from Unsloth's current notebook for your model. RTX 50-series cards Blackwell GPUs need PyTorch built with CUDA 12.8 or newer. If you see \"no kernel image is available\", install the newest PyTorch build from pytorch.org first, then Unsloth. Move your dataset in Your Windows files are visible in WSL under /mnt/c . Copy the data into the Linux side for speed: cp -r /mnt/c/Users/$USER_WINDOWS/ai-lab/finetune/data ~/ai-lab/finetune/ # replace $USER_WINDOWS with your Windows user name ls ~/ai-lab/finetune/data Windows Explorer can open the Linux side too: type \\\\wsl$ in the address bar. Prove the GPU works for training import torch p = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0) print(p.name, round(p.total_memory / 1024**3, 1), \"GiB\") x = torch.randn(4096, 4096, device=\"cuda\", requires_grad=True) loss = (x @ x).mean() loss.backward() print(\"forward + backward on GPU OK; peak memory\", round(torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 1024**3, 2), \"GiB\") When your card is too small: cloud GPUs Option What you get Good for Google Colab (free) A shared 16 GB T4, time limits Models up to about 4B. Unsloth publishes free notebooks. Colab paid / Kaggle Bigger GPUs when available 9B-class runs RunPod, Lambda, Vast.ai A whole GPU machine by the hour Anything you pay for Cloud safety checklist Upload only scrubbed data you would be comfortable seeing leak. On marketplaces, the host has physical access to the machine. Log in with an SSH key, not a password. Keep Jupyter or any web UI bound to 127.0.0.1 and reach it through an SSH tunnel: ssh -L 8888:127.0.0.1:8888 user@host . No access tokens on the machine unless needed. If you must push to Hugging Face, use a fine-grained token for one private repo and revoke it afterwards. Check copied notebooks for push_to_hub calls before running them. After downloading results, verify checksums, then delete the instance and its storage volume. Check yourself Inside WSL2, nvidia-smi fails. What should you NOT do? Update the Windows NVIDIA driver Install a Linux NVIDIA display driver inside Ubuntu Restart WSL with wsl --shutdown The Windows driver provides GPU access. A Linux driver inside WSL breaks it. Where should you keep training files when using WSL2? On a USB stick Under /mnt/c In the Linux home folder Crossing the Windows-Linux boundary is much slower."},{"id":"first-lora","mod":"9","num":"9.4","title":"Train your first LoRA with Unsloth","mins":18,"optional":false,"text":"This is the moment the course has been building to. You will load a small model, attach a LoRA adapter, train it on your dataset, test it, and save it, all on your own NVIDIA GPU. The same script scales to bigger models by changing one line. What you will learn Run the full Unsloth flow: load, attach LoRA, format, mask, train, test, save Explain every hyperparameter you set Read the training and validation loss Adapt the script to other model families and sizes Concept: Unsloth An open-source library that speeds up and slims down fine-tuning by patching Hugging Face Transformers, PEFT and TRL. You write ordinary training code with Unsloth's model loader in front, and it can export straight to GGUF. It targets NVIDIA (and AMD) GPUs. Choose your model Start small so mistakes are cheap. Pick from the lesson 9.1 table: 4 GB card (GTX 1650): unsloth/Qwen3.5-0.8B with MAX_LEN = 1024 , batch size 1 and accumulation 8, or use free Colab for bigger models 8 GB card: unsloth/Qwen3.5-2B (about 5 GB) or unsloth/Qwen3.5-0.8B 12 to 16 GB: unsloth/Qwen3.5-4B (about 10 GB) 24 GB: unsloth/Qwen3.5-9B with short sequences, or rent a bigger GPU Model ids change. If an id is not found, search Hugging Face for the family under the unsloth organisation. The script: train_lora.py Save this inside WSL2 at ~/ai-lab/finetune/train_lora.py , next to your data folder. from unsloth import FastLanguageModel # import unsloth before transformers / trl from unsloth.chat_templates import train_on_responses_only from datasets import load_dataset from trl import SFTTrainer, SFTConfig MODEL = \"unsloth/Qwen3.5-2B\" # change to fit your GPU (lesson 9.1) MAX_LEN = 2048 # must cover your longest example OUT = \"my-first-lora\" # 1. Load the base model in 16-bit (Unsloth advises against 4-bit QLoRA for Qwen3.5) model, tokenizer = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained( model_name=MODEL, max_seq_length=MAX_LEN, load_in_4bit=False, load_in_16bit=True, full_finetuning=False) # 2. Attach the LoRA adapter model = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model( model, r=16, lora_alpha=16, lora_dropout=0, bias=\"none\", target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\", \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\", \"out_proj\"], use_gradient_checkpointing=\"unsloth\", random_state=3407, max_seq_length=MAX_LEN) model.print_trainable_parameters() # 3. Render each conversation with the model's own chat template ds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files={\"train\": \"data/train.jsonl\", \"validation\": \"data/valid.jsonl\"}) def to_text(batch): return {\"text\": [tokenizer.apply_chat_template(m, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=False) for m in batch[\"messages\"]]} ds = ds.map(to_text, batched=True, remove_columns=ds[\"train\"].column_names) print(ds[\"train\"][0][\"text\"][:600]) # eyeball the rendered template # 4. Trainer, then mask everything except the assistant replies trainer = SFTTrainer( model=model, tokenizer=tokenizer, train_dataset=ds[\"train\"], eval_dataset=ds[\"validation\"], args=SFTConfig( dataset_text_field=\"text\", max_length=MAX_LEN, per_device_train_batch_size=2, gradient_accumulation_steps=4, num_train_epochs=2, learning_rate=2e-4, warmup_steps=10, lr_scheduler_type=\"linear\", optim=\"adamw_8bit\", weight_decay=0.001, logging_steps=5, eval_strategy=\"steps\", eval_steps=25, save_steps=25, output_dir=\"outputs\", seed=3407, report_to=\"none\")) trainer = train_on_responses_only(trainer, instruction_part=\"<|im_start|>user\\n\", response_part=\"<|im_start|>assistant\\n\") row = trainer.train_dataset[0] # check: only the answer should print print(tokenizer.decode([t for t, l in zip(row[\"input_ids\"], row[\"labels\"]) if l != -100])) trainer.train() # 5. Quick test FastLanguageModel.for_inference(model) msgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"A question like the ones in your dataset\"}] ids = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(msgs, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\", return_dict=True, enable_thinking=False).to(\"cuda\") out = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=300, temperature=0.7, top_p=0.8) print(tokenizer.decode(out[0][ids[\"input_ids\"].shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True)) # 6. Save the adapter (small) and a ready-to-run GGUF model.save_pretrained(OUT) tokenizer.save_pretrained(OUT) model.save_pretrained_gguf(OUT + \"-gguf\", tokenizer, quantization_method=\"q4_k_m\") cd ~/ai-lab/finetune && source .venv/bin/activate python train_lora.py 2>&1 | tee train.log In a second Windows terminal, watch nvidia-smi -l 1 . The first run can pause for a while before step 1 while Unsloth compiles kernels. Concept: train on responses only Sets the label of every token outside the assistant replies to -100, a value the loss ignores. The model still reads the system prompt and the question but is only graded on writing the answer. The decoded check line should print only answers. The markers in instruction_part and response_part are for Qwen-style templates. Other families use different markers, so copy them from your printed template. Common training errors Error or symptom Fix torch.cuda.is_available() is False in WSL2 Update the Windows NVIDIA driver, run wsl --shutdown , reopen Ubuntu. Never install a Linux NVIDIA driver inside WSL. \"no kernel image is available for execution\" Your PyTorch build is too old for the GPU (common with RTX 50-series). Install the newest CUDA build from pytorch.org. CUDA out of memory Batch size 1, more accumulation, shorter MAX_LEN , or a smaller model (lesson 9.1). Long pause before step 1 Normal on the first run while kernels compile. Wait a few minutes. \"bf16 not supported\" or similar Older GPUs (GTX 16, RTX 20, Colab T4) use float16. Let Unsloth choose the dtype automatically. Model id not found Names change. Search Hugging Face for the model under the unsloth organisation. The decoded mask shows prompts too The response markers do not match your model's template. Copy them from the printed template. Loss is nan Halve the learning rate. Reading the loss What you see Meaning Action Both losses fall, then level off Healthy Keep the run Training loss falls, validation loss rises Overfitting Fewer epochs, use an earlier checkpoint, more data Training loss near zero Memorising Fewer epochs, lower learning rate nan or sudden spikes Learning rate too high Halve it Barely moves Learning rate too low, or masking wrong Check the decoded mask line, raise learning rate CUDA out of memory Too big for your card Batch 1, accumulation 8, shorter MAX_LEN , smaller model Hyperparameters, explained Setting What it does Value and why r Adapter capacity 16. Raise to 32 for harder skills lora_alpha Adapter strength (alpha ÷ r) Equal to r, or 2 × r lora_dropout Randomly drops adapter inputs to fight overfitting 0 (fast path). 0.05 for small datasets that overfit learning_rate Step size 2e-4 for short runs. Lower toward 2e-5 for long ones num_train_epochs Passes through the data 1 to 3 batch × accumulation Examples per update 2 × 4 = 8 warmup_steps Gentle start 5 to 10% of total steps optim Optimizer adamw_8bit saves memory eval_steps How often validation loss is computed Enough for 10 to 20 points per run seed Reproducibility Any fixed number Change one thing at a time Keep a table in your notes: run name, model, data size, r, learning rate, epochs, final validation loss, and your verdict. Without it you cannot tell which change helped. Thinking models With Qwen3.5's template, examples without reasoning are rendered with an empty <think> block, which teaches the \"thinking off\" style. That matches running the model with thinking off. If you want your fine-tune to keep thinking, include reasoning in most examples (Unsloth suggests at least 75%) and evaluate with thinking on. Other model families Llama, Gemma, Mistral and many others: change MODEL , use load_in_4bit=True for QLoRA if you need the memory, and drop out_proj from the target list. Change the instruction_part and response_part markers to that family's template. Unsloth publishes a notebook per popular model. Its first cells are the most reliable source of current settings. Check yourself The decoded check line prints the system prompt and question as well as the answer. What is wrong? The response masking markers do not match your template The learning rate The GPU If the markers are wrong, nothing gets masked and the model trains on the prompts too. You run out of memory at step 1. First change to try? Raise the learning rate Raise the rank to 64 Batch size 1 with more accumulation, and a shorter max length Activations dominate memory, and they scale with batch and sequence length. What three things does the script save? Only the dataset The adapter, the tokenizer files, and a Q4_K_M GGUF Nothing, it stays in memory Keep the adapter and tokenizer files. The GGUF is ready for LM Studio or Ollama."},{"id":"evaluate","mod":"9","num":"9.5","title":"Evaluate your fine-tune honestly","mins":13,"optional":false,"text":"A falling loss proves the model learned your training data. It does not prove the model got better at the job, or that it did not get worse at everything else. This lesson builds a small, honest evaluation you can run in minutes. What you will learn Compare base and fine-tuned models on held-out examples Run a blind A/B test where you are the judge Check for forgetting with a regression set Use automatic checks and an LLM judge carefully Four kinds of check Check Measures Cost Automatic checks Format: valid JSON, required keys, length limits, banned phrases Free and instant Blind A/B by you \"Which answer is better?\" with model names hidden Your time. The gold standard. LLM as judge A strong model scores answers against a rubric Cheap, but biased (it favours longer answers and its own style) Regression set Did general ability survive? 20 to 30 unrelated questions Minutes Serve both models Import the base model's GGUF and your fine-tune's GGUF into Ollama (lesson 8.2), for example as base and mine . Use the same quantization for both so quantization does not bias the comparison. The script: ab_eval.py \"\"\"Blind A/B test of two models on test.jsonl, plus optional automatic checks.\"\"\" import json, random, sys from openai import OpenAI client = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\") A, B = \"base\", \"mine\" rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(\"data/test.jsonl\", encoding=\"utf-8\")][:20] def ask(model, messages): r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=messages, temperature=0.2, max_tokens=600) return r.choices[0].message.content.strip() def auto_check(text): \"\"\"Edit for your task: return a list of problems.\"\"\" problems = [] if len(text) > 1500: problems.append(\"too long\") return problems score = {A: 0, B: 0, \"tie\": 0} for i, row in enumerate(rows, 1): prompt = [m for m in row[\"messages\"] if m[\"role\"] != \"assistant\"] answers = [(A, ask(A, prompt)), (B, ask(B, prompt))] random.shuffle(answers) # hide which model is which print(f\"\\n===== {i}/{len(rows)} QUESTION =====\\n{prompt[-1]['content']}\") print(f\"\\n----- reference answer -----\\n{row['messages'][-1]['content']}\") for k, (_, text) in enumerate(answers, 1): print(f\"\\n----- answer {k} {auto_check(text)} -----\\n{text}\") pick = input(\"\\nBetter answer? [1/2/t] \").strip().lower() winner = \"tie\" if pick == \"t\" else answers[int(pick) - 1][0] if pick in {\"1\", \"2\"} else \"tie\" score[winner] += 1 print(\"\\nRESULT:\", score) Run it on Windows with the openai package installed. Do not peek at which is which until the end. How much difference is real? With 20 questions, 11 to 9 is a coin flip. 16 to 4 is a real signal. If the result is close, add more questions before deciding. Small differences on small test sets are mostly noise. The regression set Write 20 to 30 questions unrelated to your fine-tune: explain a concept, write a short Python function, summarise a paragraph, simple arithmetic, a question it should say \"I don't know\" to. Run both models. A clear drop on these means catastrophic forgetting (lesson 9.1): fewer epochs, a lower learning rate, or mix general examples into your data. Signs of overfitting in the answers It copies phrases or whole answers from the training data. Every answer has the same length and opening, even when that does not fit. It applies your format to questions where it makes no sense. It invents details in the confident style of your training answers. LLM as judge, carefully For larger test sets you can ask a strong model to grade answers against a short rubric (\"correct, follows the format, no invented facts, 1 to 5\"). Shuffle answer order, hide model names, and spot-check its judgements against your own on 10 examples. If you use a cloud model as the judge, remember that your test data is sent to it. Lab Import base and fine-tuned GGUFs into Ollama with identical quantization. Run ab_eval.py on 20 test examples. Record the score. Run your regression set on both. Write a three-line verdict in your notes: better at the task? worse at anything? keep or retrain? Check yourself Your fine-tune wins 11 to 9 on 20 questions. What can you conclude? It is clearly better Not much. That is within noise, so test more questions. The base model is better Small margins on small sets are not reliable evidence. Why hide which answer came from which model? So your expectations do not bias your judgement To save time Ollama requires it People favour the model they hope will win. Blinding removes that."},{"id":"export","mod":"9","num":"9.6","title":"Export: merge, convert to GGUF, run in LM Studio and Ollama","mins":15,"optional":false,"text":"Your adapter is trained. LM Studio and Ollama run GGUF files. This lesson covers the quick export Unsloth already did for you, the manual route with llama.cpp when you need control, keeping the chat template intact, and a checklist before you replace your daily model. What you will learn Choose between a merged model and a separate adapter Merge, convert and quantize manually with PEFT and llama.cpp Import into LM Studio and Ollama Verify the chat template and run a sanity checklist The map Route Steps Use when 1. Unsloth one-liner save_pretrained_gguf(..., \"q4_k_m\") in your training script Most of the time 2. Manual merge PEFT merge → convert_hf_to_gguf.py → llama-quantize You want an importance matrix, a different quantization, or the one-liner failed 3. Separate adapter convert_lora_to_gguf.py → Ollama ADAPTER or llama-server --lora You want to switch adapters on and off, or apply one to a close derivative model Concept: merging Computing W' = W + (alpha/r) · B·A for every adapted matrix and saving the result as ordinary weights. The merged model is the same size and speed as the base. Always merge into a 16-bit base and quantize once afterwards. Merging into an already-quantized base rounds your small changes on top of the existing rounding error. Route 1: copy the Unsloth GGUF out of WSL ls -lh ~/ai-lab/finetune/my-first-lora-gguf/ mkdir -p /mnt/c/Users/$USER_WINDOWS/ai-lab/models cp ~/ai-lab/finetune/my-first-lora-gguf/*Q4_K_M*.gguf /mnt/c/Users/$USER_WINDOWS/ai-lab/models/ Route 2: the manual path Merge with PEFT \"\"\"merge_lora.py: merge a LoRA adapter into its 16-bit base.\"\"\" import sys, torch from peft import PeftModel from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer BASE, ADAPTER, OUT = \"unsloth/Qwen3.5-2B\", \"my-first-lora\", \"my-first-merged\" base = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(BASE, dtype=torch.bfloat16) model = PeftModel.from_pretrained(base, ADAPTER) n = sum(1 for name, _ in model.named_modules() if name.endswith(\"lora_A\")) if n == 0: sys.exit(\"No LoRA layers injected: module names did not match. Merge where you trained.\") print(\"LoRA layers injected:\", n) model.merge_and_unload().save_pretrained(OUT) AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(BASE).save_pretrained(OUT) # keeps the chat template The injected-layer check matters If the model class differs from the one used in training (for example a text-only class versus a multimodal one), PEFT may silently attach nothing and you would ship the unmodified base. Merge in the same environment that trained the adapter, or use Unsloth's save_pretrained_merged(OUT, tokenizer, save_method=\"merged_16bit\") . Build llama.cpp (once, inside WSL2) cd ~/ai-lab && git clone https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp && cd llama.cpp cmake -B build # CPU build is enough for converting and quantizing cmake --build build --config Release -j python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install -r requirements.txt && pip install -U transformers Newer models need a newer Transformers than llama.cpp's pinned requirements, hence the last install. Prebuilt Windows binaries (including CUDA builds) are also published on the llama.cpp GitHub releases page if you prefer running tools natively. Convert and quantize python convert_hf_to_gguf.py ~/ai-lab/finetune/my-first-merged --outtype bf16 \\ --outfile ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-BF16.gguf ./build/bin/llama-quantize ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-BF16.gguf ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-Q4_K_M.gguf Q4_K_M Always quantize from the 16-bit file, never from another quantized file. If the converter says the architecture is unknown, your llama.cpp checkout is too old: git pull and rebuild. Optional: an importance matrix An imatrix records which weights matter most on sample text so 4-bit quantization spends its precision wisely. For a fine-tune, use text that looks like its job (your training conversations plus some general text): ./build/bin/llama-imatrix -m ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-BF16.gguf -f calibration.txt -o imatrix.gguf -ngl 99 ./build/bin/llama-quantize --imatrix imatrix.gguf ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-BF16.gguf mine-Q4_K_M.gguf Q4_K_M -ngl 99 needs a CUDA build ( cmake -B build -DGGML_CUDA=ON , with the CUDA toolkit installed in WSL). On a CPU build, drop it and be patient. Route 3: keep the adapter separate python convert_lora_to_gguf.py ~/ai-lab/finetune/my-first-lora \\ --base-model-id unsloth/Qwen3.5-2B --outtype f16 --outfile ~/ai-lab/finetune/mine-lora-F16.gguf Then use it with Ollama's ADAPTER (lesson 8.2), or with llama.cpp's server, where you can also scale it: ./build/bin/llama-server -m base-Q8_0.gguf --lora-scaled mine-lora-F16.gguf:0.7 --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8081 Import and run # LM Studio lms import $HOME\\ai-lab\\models\\mine-Q4_K_M.gguf --copy --user-repo me/mine lms ls # Ollama: Modelfile with FROM ./mine-Q4_K_M.gguf ollama create mine -f Modelfile If the base model supported images, copy its mmproj file into the same LM Studio model folder. Your LoRA did not touch the vision encoder, so the base model's file still works. Check the chat template inside the GGUF \"\"\"show_template.py: print the chat template stored in a GGUF.\"\"\" import sys from gguf import GGUFReader # pip install gguf field = GGUFReader(sys.argv[1]).fields.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\") print(field.contents()[:1500] if field else \"No chat template: the runtime will guess.\") Then watch what LM Studio actually sends: run lms log stream and send a message. The formatted prompt should match the template you trained with (lesson 7.3). Ship-readiness checklist File size and speed are similar to the base model at the same quantization The template prints correctly and the log shows the right markers Short answers stop cleanly, with no leaked end-of-turn tokens The thinking setting behaves as trained The trained habits show on test prompts General ability holds on the regression set (lesson 9.5) Check yourself Why merge into a 16-bit base and quantize afterwards? It is faster to download Quantized bases cannot be loaded Merging into quantized weights re-rounds the small LoRA changes and can wash them out Merge once in high precision, then quantize once. The merge script prints \"LoRA layers injected: 0\". What happened? Training failed Module names did not match, so nothing was applied The adapter is too small Without this check you would ship the unchanged base model."},{"id":"beyond-sft","mod":"9","num":"9.7","title":"Beyond SFT: DPO, distillation, RL and more","mins":11,"optional":true,"text":"Supervised fine-tuning teaches by example. Some goals need other kinds of training: preferring good answers over bad ones, copying a stronger model, reading more domain text, or practising tasks with checkable answers. This lesson is your map of those methods. What you will learn Know when SFT stops being enough Explain DPO, distillation, continued pretraining and GRPO Know what is realistic on a home GPU Where SFT stops SFT shows the model one good answer per prompt. It cannot express \"this answer is better than that one\", and it cannot reward the model for finding a correct answer by itself. That is where preference and reinforcement methods come in. DPO: better versus worse Direct Preference Optimization trains on pairs: for the same prompt, a chosen answer and a rejected one. The model is pushed toward the chosen style and away from the rejected one, without training a separate reward model. It is the same family of methods that gives chat models their manners, and it is also used to \"heal\" quality after abliteration (lesson 10.3). {\"prompt\": \"Explain VRAM briefly.\", \"chosen\": \"VRAM is the graphics card's own memory...\", \"rejected\": \"Great question! As an AI, I'd be happy to...\"} TRL provides DPOTrainer and Unsloth supports it with LoRA, so DPO on small models fits the same GPUs as SFT. Related methods you will see named: ORPO (combines SFT and preference in one step) and KTO (needs only \"good\" or \"bad\" labels, not pairs). Distillation: borrow a stronger model's skill A big teacher answers many prompts and a small student is fine-tuned on those answers. The student picks up much of the teacher's style and some of its skill at a fraction of the size. Your dataset script in lesson 9.2 is a simple form of this. Check the teacher's licence or terms for conditions on training with its outputs. Continued pretraining: more reading Instead of chat examples, train on raw domain text (the {\"text\": ...} format) so the model absorbs vocabulary and style of a field, such as medical notes or a programming language. It needs far more data and compute than SFT, and it is usually followed by an SFT step to restore assistant behaviour. Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (GRPO) For tasks where you can check an answer automatically (maths with a known result, code that must pass tests, output that must be valid JSON), the model generates several attempts, a reward function scores each, and the model is nudged toward the higher-scoring ones. GRPO is the popular method behind recent reasoning models. Unsloth supports it for some small models, but it is slower and fussier than SFT. Try it after you are comfortable with SFT. Model merging Combine two fine-tunes of the same base into one model without further training. Covered in lesson 10.5. What is realistic at home Method 8 to 12 GB 16 to 24 GB Rented 40 to 80 GB SFT with LoRA 0.8B to 4B 4B to 9B Larger models DPO with LoRA Small models Up to about 9B, short sequences Comfortable GRPO Tiny models only Small models (about 4B) Recommended Continued pretraining Experiments only Small models Recommended Check yourself What data does DPO need? Raw text documents Prompts with a chosen and a rejected answer Only prompts It learns from better-versus-worse pairs. Which method fits \"make the model better at producing code that passes my tests\"? RL with verifiable rewards, such as GRPO Continued pretraining on novels A longer system prompt only Test results give an automatic reward signal."},{"id":"directions","mod":"10","num":"10.1","title":"Features are directions: interpretability for builders","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"Steering, ablation and abliteration all rest on one research finding: many behaviours a model tracks show up as a direction in its residual stream. This lesson makes that idea concrete and gives you the toolkit the next three lessons use. What you will learn Explain \"features are directions\" and why superposition complicates it Find a direction with the difference-of-means recipe Set up a small PyTorch toolkit that runs on your NVIDIA GPU Know the other interpretability tools by name The residual stream is a vector space Recall lesson 7.2: every layer reads and adds to the token's running vector. For a small model that vector has 1,024 numbers, for a 9B about 4,096. You can treat it as a point in a space with that many axes. Research consistently finds that concepts the model tracks (positive or negative sentiment, \"this is code\", \"answer briefly\", \"I should refuse\") tend to correspond to directions in that space: moving the vector along a direction strengthens or weakens the concept. Concept: superposition There are far more concepts than axes, so the model packs many features into overlapping, nearly-perpendicular directions. That is why editing one direction usually has small side effects on others. Every technique in this module comes with a side-effect check for this reason. Finding a direction: difference of means Write two sets of prompts that differ only in the property you care about (a contrast pair set). Run each prompt and record the residual stream at the last prompt token, where the model is about to answer. Average each set. Subtract one average from the other. The result points from one behaviour toward the other. Divide by its length to get a pure direction. That is all abliteration does to find its \"refusal direction\": requests the model refuses versus requests it answers. Confounds ruin directions If all your \"verbose\" prompts are about history and all your \"concise\" ones about maths, you have built a history-versus-maths direction. Make the two sets identical except for the property, ideally the same questions with a different instruction. The toolkit: dirkit.py Use the AI Python environment from lesson 2.3 ( .venv-ai ), which already has PyTorch and Transformers. The toolkit uses a small model so experiments take minutes, even on a 4 GB card. Save it as dirkit.py in a folder like ai-lab\\weights ; the scripts in lessons 10.1 to 10.3 sit next to it. cd $HOME\\ai-lab .venv-ai\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 mkdir weights; cd weights \"\"\"dirkit.py: shared helpers for direction experiments.\"\"\" import torch from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer MODEL = \"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\" # small and fast; swap for any chat model with safetensors weights def load(name=MODEL): tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name) model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, dtype=(torch.bfloat16 if torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported() else torch.float16), device_map=\"cuda\") # float16 on GTX 16 / RTX 20 cards model.eval() return tok, model def chat(tok, user, system=None): msgs = ([{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system}] if system else []) + [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user}] return tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=False) @torch.no_grad() def last_token_states(model, tok, texts): \"\"\"Residual stream at the final prompt token: tensor [n_layers + 1, n_texts, hidden]. Index 0 is the embedding output; index i is the output of decoder layer i - 1.\"\"\" out = [] for t in texts: ids = tok(t, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device) hs = model(**ids, output_hidden_states=True).hidden_states out.append(torch.stack([h[0, -1].float().cpu() for h in hs])) return torch.stack(out, dim=1) @torch.no_grad() def generate(model, tok, text, max_new_tokens=200): ids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device) o = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=max_new_tokens, do_sample=False) new = o[0, ids.input_ids.shape[1]:] return tok.decode(new, skip_special_tokens=True), len(new) def read_lines(path): return [l.strip() for l in open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") if l.strip()] First experiment: is there a \"be brief\" direction? \"\"\"find_direction.py: difference of means for concise vs verbose instructions.\"\"\" import torch from dirkit import load, chat, last_token_states QS = [\"What is DNS?\", \"Why is the sky blue?\", \"What does a firewall do?\", \"How do vaccines work?\", \"What is a hash function?\", \"Why do we have seasons?\", \"What is TLS?\", \"How does a compiler work?\", \"What is inflation?\", \"What causes earthquakes?\", \"What is a VPN?\", \"How does GPS work?\"] tok, model = load() verbose = last_token_states(model, tok, [chat(tok, q, \"Answer at length with detail and examples.\") for q in QS]) concise = last_token_states(model, tok, [chat(tok, q, \"Answer in one short sentence.\") for q in QS]) diff = verbose.mean(1) - concise.mean(1) # [layers + 1, hidden] scale = verbose.norm(dim=-1).mean(1) for i in range(0, diff.shape[0], 2): print(f\"layer {i:2d}: |difference| = {diff[i].norm():7.2f} typical |stream| = {scale[i]:7.2f}\") torch.save(diff, \"verbose_minus_concise.pt\") Read the table. The difference is usually small in the earliest layers and grows through the middle. Keep the saved file: the next two lessons use it. Other tools, by name Linear probes : train a tiny classifier on activations to test whether a concept is readable at a layer. Logit lens : decode the residual stream at intermediate layers to see what the model \"would say\" so far. Activation patching : copy activations from one run into another to find which components cause a behaviour. Sparse autoencoders : learn a large dictionary of cleaner features from activations. Used heavily in current research. Check yourself What is the difference-of-means recipe? Average the model's weights Average activations for two contrasting prompt sets and subtract Compare two models' file sizes The subtraction points from one behaviour to the other. Why do direction edits have side effects? Features overlap in the same space (superposition) GPUs are imprecise Prompts are too short Removing one direction slightly affects features that overlap with it."},{"id":"steering","mod":"10","num":"10.2","title":"Activation steering: change behaviour without training","mins":15,"optional":false,"text":"Add a direction to the residual stream while the model runs and you can make it shorter, more formal or more cheerful, with no training and no prompt. This lesson builds a steering vector in Python, then applies one to a GGUF with llama.cpp. What you will learn Build and apply a steering vector, and sweep layer and strength Measure the intended effect and the side effects Create and use llama.cpp control vectors on a GGUF Choose between prompting, steering and fine-tuning The idea Take the direction from lesson 10.1 (verbose minus concise). While the model generates, add α × v to one layer's output at every token. Positive α pushes toward verbose, negative toward concise. Nothing on disk changes, and removing the hook restores the original model. Lab: steer.py \"\"\"steer.py: apply the verbose-vs-concise vector and sweep layer x strength.\"\"\" import torch from dirkit import load, chat, generate TEST = [\"What is a CPU cache?\", \"How do airplanes fly?\", \"What is a reverse proxy?\"] NEUTRAL = \"The committee met on Tuesday to review the budget and agreed to postpone the vote until March.\" class Steer: \"\"\"Add alpha * v to one decoder layer's output while active.\"\"\" def __init__(self, layer, v, alpha): p = next(layer.parameters()) self.layer, self.v, self.alpha = layer, v.to(p.device, p.dtype), alpha def hook(self, module, inputs, output): if isinstance(output, tuple): return (output[0] + self.alpha * self.v, *output[1:]) return output + self.alpha * self.v def __enter__(self): self.h = self.layer.register_forward_hook(self.hook) def __exit__(self, *exc): self.h.remove() @torch.no_grad() def neutral_loss(model, tok): ids = tok(NEUTRAL, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device) return float(model(**ids, labels=ids.input_ids).loss) tok, model = load() diff = torch.load(\"verbose_minus_concise.pt\") # index i = output of layer i - 1 n = len(model.model.layers) base = neutral_loss(model, tok) print(\"layer alpha mean_len side_effect sample\") for L in (n // 4, n // 2, 3 * n // 4): for alpha in (-4.0, -2.0, 0.0, 2.0, 4.0): with Steer(model.model.layers[L], diff[L + 1], alpha): outs = [generate(model, tok, chat(tok, q), 160) for q in TEST] side = neutral_loss(model, tok) - base mean_len = sum(k for _, k in outs) / len(outs) print(f\"{L:5d} {alpha:+5.1f} {mean_len:9.1f} {side:+11.3f} {outs[0][0][:60]!r}\") How to read the results Mean length should rise with positive α and fall with negative α at a good layer. Side effect is how much more surprised the model is by a neutral sentence. It usually starts rising before the text visibly breaks, which makes it an early warning. Read the samples. Length can rise because the model starts repeating itself. A result only counts if the text is still good. Middle layers usually steer best. Past a certain strength, coherence collapses suddenly. Your own direction Swap the instructions for a formal-versus-casual pair, or plain-English versus jargon. Then compare your new vector with the verbosity one using cosine similarity ( torch.nn.functional.cosine_similarity ). Below about 0.3 means a genuinely different direction. Steering a GGUF with llama.cpp LM Studio and Ollama do not currently expose steering, but llama.cpp's own server runs the same GGUF files and supports control vectors . A control vector is a small GGUF holding one direction per layer, and it must come from the same model (same width and layers). 1. Make one with llama-cvector-generator The tool runs paired prompts through the model, takes positive-minus-negative differences at each layer, and condenses them (PCA by default, or --method mean ). Prompt files have one prompt per line, written in the model's raw chat format with newlines escaped as \\n . Generate them from the tokenizer so the template is exact: \"\"\"make_cvec_prompts.py: write positive/negative prompt files for llama-cvector-generator.\"\"\" from transformers import AutoTokenizer tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\") # the same model as your GGUF QS = [\"What is DNS?\", \"Why is the sky blue?\", \"What is TLS?\", \"How do vaccines work?\"] # use 50+ def fmt(system, q): t = tok.apply_chat_template([{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": q}], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=False) return t.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\\\n\") with open(\"positive.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as p, open(\"negative.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as n: for q in QS: p.write(fmt(\"Answer in one short sentence.\", q) + \"\\n\") n.write(fmt(\"Answer at length with detail and examples.\", q) + \"\\n\") ./build/bin/llama-cvector-generator -m model-Q8_0.gguf \\ --positive-file positive.txt --negative-file negative.txt -ngl 99 -o concise.gguf 2. Apply it ./build/bin/llama-server -m model-Q8_0.gguf \\ --control-vector-scaled concise.gguf:0.8 --control-vector-layer-range 8 20 \\ --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8081 Negative scales push the other way. The server speaks the same OpenAI-style API as lesson 6.2. Flag spellings have changed between versions (some older docs show the scale after a space), so run llama-server --help and trust what it prints. The tools are in llama.cpp's Windows release downloads, or you can build them in WSL2 (lesson 9.6). Steering vs prompting vs fine-tuning Prompting Steering Fine-tuning Changes weights? No No Yes (or adds an adapter) Cost to create Minutes An hour Hours, data, a GPU Can a user override it? Easily Not from the prompt Not from the prompt Best for Most tasks One behaviour axis: tone, length, persona New formats and skills Main risk Ignored, injected Incoherence at high strength Forgetting, overfitting Check yourself Mean answer length rises at α = +4, but samples repeat the same phrase. Is the steering a success? Yes, the number went up Yes, if the side-effect metric is below 1 No. The text broke, so lower α or try another layer. Always read the outputs, not only the metric. Can a control vector made from a 0.6B model steer a 9B model? No. It must come from the same model. Yes, any model works Only at scale 1.0 Widths and layers differ, and directions are model-specific."},{"id":"ablation","mod":"10","num":"10.3","title":"Direction ablation and abliteration: editing weights","mins":20,"optional":false,"text":"Steering adds a direction while the model runs. Ablation removes a direction permanently, by editing the weights so no layer can ever write it. Aimed at the refusal direction, this is abliteration, the most common way uncensored models are made. This lesson teaches the maths, then gives you a script that does it. What you will learn Remove a direction from a vector, and from a weight matrix Bake an edit into a model and save it as new weights Run the full abliteration pipeline and evaluate it honestly Know the costs, and how \"healing\" recovers quality Before you start Work on a copy and keep the original. Measure before and after. When you remove a model's ability to refuse, its judgement becomes your job (lesson 5.3). The maths, in two dimensions Take a vector x = (3, 4) and a unit direction r̂ = (1, 0). The dot product x·r̂ = 3 says how far x goes along r̂. Removing that part: x' = x − (x·r̂) r̂ = (3, 4) − 3·(1, 0) = (0, 4) check: x'·r̂ = 0 → nothing left along r̂, everything else untouched Add a strength s: x' = x − s (x·r̂) r̂ . s = 1 removes the component exactly, s = 0.5 halves it, s = 2 flips it. From vectors to weights A writer matrix W (lesson 7.2) adds y = W·x to the residual stream. To make sure its output never contains r̂: W' = W − r̂ (r̂ᵀ W) # for o_proj, out_proj, down_proj (PyTorch shape [hidden, in]) E' = E − (E r̂) r̂ᵀ # for the embedding table (shape [vocab, hidden]) If you edit the embedding and every writer in every layer, the stream can never contain r̂, because it is just the sum of what those writers add. This \"full orthogonalization\" is what abliteration does. Editing only a few writers is \"targeted ablation\", with a smaller effect and fewer side effects. Two classic mistakes Do the maths in float32, then cast back to bf16. Editing in bf16 adds rounding error comparable to the edit itself. Edit Hugging Face safetensors weights, never GGUF tensors. GGUF stores dimensions in reverse order, so the code would edit the wrong axis. The script: ablate.py It uses dirkit.py from lesson 10.1. Give it two prompt files (one prompt per line), optional system prompts for each, a layer, and test prompts. It finds the direction, shows outputs before, edits the weights, shows outputs after, and saves the edited model. \"\"\"ablate.py: find a direction from two prompt sets and remove it from every writer weight.\"\"\" import argparse, torch from dirkit import load, chat, last_token_states, generate, read_lines @torch.no_grad() def orthogonalize(model, r): r = r.float() def fix(W): # writer weight, shape [hidden, in] Wf = W.data.float(); rr = r.to(Wf.device) Wf -= torch.outer(rr, rr @ Wf) W.data.copy_(Wf.to(W.dtype)) E = model.model.embed_tokens.weight # shape [vocab, hidden] Ef = E.data.float(); rr = r.to(Ef.device) Ef -= torch.outer(Ef @ rr, rr) E.data.copy_(Ef.to(E.dtype)) edited = 1 for layer in model.model.layers: for part in (\"self_attn\", \"linear_attn\"): mod = getattr(layer, part, None) for name in (\"o_proj\", \"out_proj\"): if mod is not None and hasattr(mod, name): fix(getattr(mod, name).weight); edited += 1 fix(layer.mlp.down_proj.weight); edited += 1 return edited def main(): ap = argparse.ArgumentParser() ap.add_argument(\"--pos\", required=True); ap.add_argument(\"--neg\", required=True) ap.add_argument(\"--pos-system\"); ap.add_argument(\"--neg-system\") ap.add_argument(\"--test\", required=True); ap.add_argument(\"--test-system\") ap.add_argument(\"--layer\", type=int, help=\"decoder layer whose output defines the direction\") ap.add_argument(\"--out\", default=\"edited-model\") a = ap.parse_args() tok, model = load() pos = last_token_states(model, tok, [chat(tok, q, a.pos_system) for q in read_lines(a.pos)]) neg = last_token_states(model, tok, [chat(tok, q, a.neg_system) for q in read_lines(a.neg)]) diff = pos.mean(1) - neg.mean(1) for i in range(1, diff.shape[0]): print(f\"layer {i - 1:2d}: |difference| = {diff[i].norm():.2f}\") L = a.layer if a.layer is not None else int(0.5 * len(model.model.layers)) r = diff[L + 1] / diff[L + 1].norm() tests = [chat(tok, q, a.test_system) for q in read_lines(a.test)] before = [generate(model, tok, t) for t in tests] print(\"edited\", orthogonalize(model, r), \"weight matrices using layer\", L) after = [generate(model, tok, t) for t in tests] for (b, nb), (c, nc) in zip(before, after): print(f\"\\nBEFORE ({nb} tok): {b[:200]!r}\\nAFTER ({nc} tok): {c[:200]!r}\") model.save_pretrained(a.out); tok.save_pretrained(a.out) torch.save(r, f\"{a.out}/direction.pt\") if __name__ == \"__main__\": main() Lab: remove the \"be verbose\" direction A safe first edit you can measure objectively. Put 30 or more varied questions in questions.txt and 10 different ones in test.txt , then: python ablate.py --pos questions.txt --neg questions.txt ` --pos-system \"Answer at length with detail and examples.\" ` --neg-system \"Answer in one short sentence.\" ` --test test.txt --test-system \"Answer at length with detail and examples.\" --out less-verbose After the edit, the model should struggle to follow \"answer at length\" and its answers should get shorter, because it can no longer represent that instruction along the removed direction. Try several --layer values around the middle of the model and compare. Then run a few unrelated questions to check general quality. Abliteration: the same edit, aimed at refusals Arditi et al. (2024) found that across 13 open chat models up to 72B, refusing is largely mediated by one direction. Abliteration finds it with exactly this script: --pos : requests the model refuses. --neg : similar requests it answers normally, matched in length and style. No system prompts. --test : held-out requests of both kinds, never used to find the direction. The research used a few hundred prompts per side from public instruction datasets. For your own purposes, the most useful positive set is usually the legitimate requests your model over-refuses in your field (fiction, security education, medicine, history), because that is the behaviour you actually want removed. Then pick the layer by testing: the best layer is the one where held-out refusals drop most while answers stay coherent. Evaluate it like an engineer REFUSAL_MARKERS = [\"i can't\", \"i cannot\", \"i'm sorry\", \"i won't\", \"as an ai\", \"i am unable\"] def refused(text): return any(m in text.lower()[:200] for m in REFUSAL_MARKERS) # refusal rate = share of held-out prompts where refused(answer) is True, before vs after Refusal rate on held-out prompts, before and after. This keyword check is a rough first pass, so read the answers too. Quality on your regression set from lesson 9.5. Abliteration usually costs a little. One published comparison of a 27B abliterated model found broad knowledge almost unchanged but truthfulness benchmarks down by about 6 points (lesson 5.2). New problems : more hallucination, weaker instruction following, odd disclaimers. Healing A widely read 2024 tutorial by Maxime Labonne found that abliterating a Llama 3 8B model removed refusals but lowered every benchmark run, and that a short DPO step afterwards recovered most of the loss (though not on maths). That is the standard recipe: abliterate, then a small DPO or SFT run on good-quality examples (lesson 9.7). From edited weights to LM Studio The script saves ordinary safetensors. Convert and quantize them exactly like a merged fine-tune (lesson 9.6): convert_hf_to_gguf.py , then llama-quantize , then import. Bigger models The script loads the whole model onto the GPU in 16-bit, so it suits models up to roughly half your VRAM in billions of parameters (a 4B model needs about 8 GB; on a 4 GB card, stay with 0.6B to 1.5B models). For larger models, compute the direction on the GPU, then edit the safetensors files shard by shard on the CPU with the same two formulas, which needs no GPU memory. Check yourself Why must every writer be edited for full orthogonalization? To make the file smaller The residual stream is the sum of everything writers add, so any unedited writer could still write the direction Because LoRA requires it Only when every source is cleaned can the stream never contain r̂. Why edit in float32 and cast back? bf16 rounding error can be as large as the edit itself GGUF requires float32 It is faster High-precision maths keeps small edits intact. Refusals dropped to zero after abliteration. Is the job done? Yes Yes, if the file is the same size No. Check quality on a regression set and read answers for new problems, then consider healing. \"Answers everything\" says nothing about whether the answers are good."},{"id":"uncensored-routes","mod":"10","num":"10.4","title":"Build your own uncensored custom model: five routes","mins":14,"optional":false,"text":"You now have every piece: fine-tuning, adapters, and weight editing. This lesson puts them together into practical recipes for a model that is both yours (trained on your data and style) and free of the refusals that get in your way. What you will learn Compare five routes to a customised, uncensored model Pick the right route for your hardware and goal Evaluate task quality and refusals separately Handle licences and responsibility The five routes Route How Pros Cons 1. Fine-tune an uncensored base Use an abliterated model published as safetensors as your Unsloth MODEL (lesson 9.4) Cleanest path: train, merge, convert as normal You inherit someone else's uncensoring and its quality cost 2. Fine-tune, then abliterate Train on the official model, merge, then run ablate.py on the merged weights (lesson 10.3) Full control and understanding Two processes to evaluate. May need healing. 3. Fine-tune on non-refusing data Include examples where the assistant fully answers legitimate requests the base used to refuse Removes refusals in exactly your domain Needs careful data. Can shift style broadly. 4. Adapter on an uncensored GGUF Train on the official base, convert the adapter, apply it to a close uncensored derivative with Ollama ADAPTER or llama-server --lora Fast to try, no merge Experimental. Works only on close derivatives. Must be measured. 5. Download a ready-made one Use an existing uncensored GGUF plus your system prompt or Modelfile (module 8) Zero training Not customised beyond prompts Route 1 in practice Community publishers such as huihui-ai release abliterated versions of popular models as safetensors in several sizes. One example is huihui-ai/Huihui-Qwen3.5-9B-abliterated (Apache-2.0). Search Hugging Face for \"abliterated\" plus your model family and size, check the card names its base model and method, then put it in train_lora.py : MODEL = \"huihui-ai/SOME-MODEL-abliterated\" # must be safetensors, not GGUF # everything else in train_lora.py stays the same You can never train on a GGUF-only release. If a model is published only as GGUF, use route 4 or 5 for it. Route 3: training data that does not refuse Research by Qi et al. (2023) showed that fine-tuning on as few as 10 non-refusing examples could strip much of a model's safety behaviour, and that even ordinary benign fine-tuning erodes it somewhat. In practice, for your own model: Write examples in your domain where the ideal answer is complete and direct, with no moralising or disclaimers, for requests the base model hedged on. Keep them correct and high quality. Every flaw is learned too. Mix them with your normal task examples so the model learns your style and your directness together. Route 4 in practice # Ollama Modelfile FROM ./Some-Model-9B-Uncensored-Q4_K_M.gguf ADAPTER ./my-lora-F16.gguf # trained on the OFFICIAL Some-Model-9B An adapter learns a difference from the base it was trained on. A derivative made with small weight edits (such as abliteration) is close enough that it often works, but it is an empirical question. Compare against the same model without the adapter. Evaluate two things separately Question How Did the trained habits survive? Blind A/B on your test set (lesson 9.5) Did the uncensoring survive? Refusal rate on held-out legitimate prompts (lesson 10.3) Did general quality survive? Regression set Fine-tuning can bring refusals back An adapter trained on the official base learned in the presence of its refusal behaviour, and may partly restore it when applied to an uncensored model. Equally, fine-tuning an abliterated model on cautious data can re-teach caution. Measure, do not assume. Choosing a route Want the quickest result? Route 5, then route 4. Want the cleanest custom model? Route 1. Want to understand and control everything? Route 2. Only one domain is over-refused? Route 3. Licences and responsibility Derivatives inherit the base model's licence. Some restrict certain uses or require attribution. Read it before you share a model. If you publish an uncensored model, document the base, the method and your evaluation honestly, as you would want others to. The law applies to what you produce and do with the output, not the tool (lesson 5.3). Removing refusals moves all of the judgement to you. Keep unrestricted models local, bound to localhost, and away from anything other people can use. Check yourself An uncensored model is published only as GGUF. Can you fine-tune it with Unsloth? Yes, directly No. Training needs safetensors. Use an adapter on it (route 4) or a different base. Yes, after renaming the file GGUF is an inference format. After applying your adapter to an uncensored model, what must you measure? Task quality and refusal rate, separately Only file size Only speed Either one can change, and they can move in opposite directions."},{"id":"editing-merging","mod":"10","num":"10.5","title":"Knowledge editing and model merging","mins":10,"optional":true,"text":"Two more ways to change weights without a full training run: surgically rewriting a single fact, and blending several fine-tunes into one model. Both are useful to know about, and merging in particular is popular in the open-model community. What you will learn Explain how ROME and MEMIT rewrite facts, and their limits Explain task arithmetic and the main merge methods Know when merging beats another fine-tune MLPs as key-value memories Lesson 7.2 mentioned that MLPs behave a bit like memories: a pattern in the input (the key, such as \"the capital of France is\") retrieves a stored output (the value, \"Paris\"). Knowledge-editing methods use this view. ROME and MEMIT ROME (2022) locates the MLP layer that stores a fact and applies a rank-one update to its weights so one key retrieves a new value. Change \"the Eiffel Tower is in Paris\" to \"Rome\" and related questions follow. MEMIT extends this to thousands of edits at once across several layers. Limits Edits can be brittle: they may not carry over to rephrased questions or related facts, and many edits can degrade the model. For keeping a model up to date with facts, RAG remains the practical answer (lesson 8.1). Treat knowledge editing as a research tool. Task arithmetic If you fine-tune a base model and subtract the base weights from the result, you get a task vector : the change that skill made. Task vectors can be added (combine skills), scaled (stronger or weaker), or subtracted (remove a behaviour). A LoRA adapter is a compact, low-rank task vector. Model merging Merging combines models that share the same base and architecture into one, with no training. Method Idea Linear Weighted average of the weights SLERP Interpolates two models along a curve instead of a straight line, often smoother TIES Keeps only the largest changes from each fine-tune and resolves sign conflicts DARE Randomly drops most small changes and rescales the rest before merging The common tool is mergekit . A merge is described in a small YAML file listing the models, the method and the weights, and runs on CPU if needed. Many popular community models on Hugging Face are merges. # mergekit example (check its README for current syntax) merge_method: ties base_model: Qwen/Qwen3-4B models: - model: you/qwen3-4b-housestyle-merged parameters: {weight: 0.6, density: 0.5} - model: someone/qwen3-4b-coder-finetune parameters: {weight: 0.4, density: 0.5} dtype: bfloat16 When to merge You have two fine-tunes of the same base (for example your house style and a coding skill) and want both. You want to soften a fine-tune by merging it back toward the base. Combining several LoRA adapters: PEFT can merge adapters ( add_weighted_adapter ), and llama.cpp's server can stack several at chosen scales. Evaluate a merge exactly like a fine-tune (lesson 9.5). Merges can be surprisingly good or quietly broken. Check yourself Can you merge a Llama fine-tune with a Qwen fine-tune? Yes, with SLERP Yes, with TIES No. Merged models must share the same base architecture. Weights only line up between models of the same architecture and base. What is a task vector? Fine-tuned weights minus base weights A list of tasks A steering vector It captures the change a fine-tune made, and can be added, scaled or subtracted."},{"id":"capstone","mod":"11","num":"11.1","title":"Capstone: build your own model, end to end","mins":15,"optional":false,"text":"Time to prove it. In this project you build a model that is genuinely yours: trained on your data, in your style, free of the refusals that get in your way, packaged and served on your own PC. Each step points to the lesson that taught it. What you will deliver A written spec and a reviewed dataset A trained LoRA, evaluated against the base model An uncensored variant made by one of the routes in lesson 10.4 A named custom model in Ollama and LM Studio, called from a Python script Pick a project Choose something you will actually use. Good first projects: Your writing twin : rewrites drafts in your tone and format. A domain explainer : explains your field in plain English in a consistent structure, without hedging. A strict formatter : turns messy notes into a fixed JSON or report structure every time. A fiction partner : writes in a chosen style without over-refusing dark themes. Step 1: write the spec (30 minutes) One page in NOTES.md : what the model does, what a perfect answer looks like (write three), what it must never do, which base model and size fits your GPU (lesson 9.1), and how you will judge success (lesson 9.5). Step 2: baseline (1 hour) Before training anything, try the cheaper rungs (lesson 8.1). Write the best system prompt you can, make a Modelfile (lesson 8.2), and score the base model on 20 test prompts. This is the bar your fine-tune must beat. If the prompt alone is good enough, you have learned something valuable cheaply. Step 3: dataset (an evening or two) Use make_dataset.py (lesson 9.2) on your own documents, then review by hand. Target 300 or more training examples. If your domain is over-refused, include direct, complete answers for those requests (route 3 in lesson 10.4). Keep 10% aside as test data. Step 4: train (1 to 3 hours) Run train_lora.py in WSL2 (lessons 9.3 and 9.4). Do one quick run on a small model or a subset first to check the pipeline. Keep a run log: settings, losses, verdict. Step 5: evaluate (1 hour) Blind A/B against the baseline on the test set, plus the regression set (lesson 9.5). If it does not clearly win, fix the data before changing hyperparameters. Data is almost always the problem. Step 6: uncensor (1 to 3 hours) Pick a route from lesson 10.4. Route 1 if a suitable abliterated safetensors base exists, route 2 if you want to do the abliteration yourself with ablate.py (lesson 10.3). Measure the refusal rate on held-out legitimate prompts and rerun the regression set. Step 7: package and serve (30 minutes) Export to GGUF and check the template (lesson 9.6). Make a Modelfile with your system prompt and settings, then ollama create (lesson 8.2). Import into LM Studio with a preset. Call it from ask.py (lesson 6.2). Step 8: write it up A short model card in your ai-lab folder: base model and licence, data sources, training settings, evaluation results, uncensoring method, known weaknesses. This habit is what separates experiments from engineering. Capstone checklist Spec written, with three perfect example answers Prompt-only baseline scored on 20 test prompts 300+ reviewed training examples, split by source LoRA trained with a run log Blind A/B win over the baseline, regression set holds Uncensored variant made and measured (refusals and quality) GGUF exported, template verified, running in Ollama and LM Studio Called from a Python script Model card written If it does not beat the baseline That is a normal first result. Read 20 training examples critically, fix inconsistencies, add variety, and retrain with the same settings. Change hyperparameters only after the data is good."},{"id":"next-steps","mod":"11","num":"11.2","title":"Where to go next","mins":7,"optional":true,"text":"You can now run, understand, train, customise and uncensor local models. This last lesson lists the topics that build on what you know, and the habits that keep you improving. What you will learn Which advanced topics to learn next, and what each builds on Habits that keep your skills current as models change Topics that build on this course Topic What it is Builds on Agents A model that calls tools in a loop until a goal is met: reading files, running code, searching Tool calling (6.5), memory (6.3) MCP A standard way to plug tools into apps like LM Studio and share them between programs Tool calling (6.5) Advanced RAG Hybrid search, reranking, retrieval evaluation at scale Memory and RAG (6.3) Reinforcement learning (GRPO) Training reasoning on tasks with checkable answers Beyond SFT (9.7) Interpretability research Sparse autoencoders, activation patching, finding circuits Directions (10.1) Serving and scaling vLLM or llama.cpp servers, multiple users, bigger GPUs, quantization formats for servers Modules 3 and 4 Private offline setups Air-gapped machines, hardening, keeping models and data local Hygiene (6.4) Habits that keep you improving Keep your run log and model cards. Your own results are the best teacher. Follow new releases in your size class and retest your capstone on them. A newer base model often beats more training. Read the model card and the Unsloth notebook for every new model family before training it. Re-measure after every tool update: LM Studio, Ollama, llama.cpp and Unsloth change monthly. Join the community (r/LocalLLaMA, Hugging Face discussions) and verify claims yourself. You made it You started using AI through chat apps. You can now explain what is inside a model, run it on your GPU, give it your documents and tools, train it on your own data, change its behaviour by editing its weights, and evaluate the result honestly. That is the full journey you set out on."},{"id":"roadmap","mod":"R","num":"","title":"The 8-week learning plan","mins":7,"optional":false,"text":"Eight weeks at about six hours a week takes you from \"I only use chat apps\" to training, steering and uncensoring your own models. Weeks 1 to 4 build the foundations. Weeks 5 to 8 are about training and editing. Every week ends with something you build. Tick boxes as you go. They are remembered in this browser. A typical week Session 1 (about 2 h): read the first lessons slowly. Session 2 (about 2 h): do the hands-on steps. Session 3 (about 2 h): finish the lessons, answer every \"Check yourself\" question, and write up the deliverable. Phase 1: Foundations Week 1: Concepts and your PC Read Welcome and set up ai-lab and NOTES.md Module 1: What AI is , cloud vs local , training vs inference , tokens and context , why local Module 2: your PC , driver and CUDA , Windows toolbox , tier map Deliverable: NOTES.md with your GPU, VRAM, driver, the size class you will start with, and token counts for five texts. Week 2: Model files and your first model Module 3: quantization , file formats , Will it fit? , speed LM Studio : download a model, chat, watch nvidia-smi Load the same model at 8K and 32K context and record VRAM and speed Deliverable: predicted vs measured VRAM and tokens per second for one model at three settings. Week 3: Mastery and model choice Settings that matter and the sampling demo. Save three presets. Ollama , Hugging Face , choosing a model , troubleshooting Module 5: refusals , uncensored models , safe use Deliverable: a ten-prompt shootout between two models, including one uncensored variant if you wish. Week 4: Use it for real Module 6: prompting , local API , memory and RAG , hygiene , structured output and tools Build ask.py , the RAG scripts over your notes, and the two-tool assistant Deliverable: ask.py , a RAG index of your notes answering with citations, and tools_demo.py working. Phase 2: Training and editing Week 5: How models work and customizing Module 7: gradient descent , the transformer , chat templates , how text gets generated Module 8: customization ladder , Modelfiles . Build a custom model in Ollama. Deliverable: the gradient-descent script run at three learning rates, and one custom Modelfile model. Week 6: Data and your first fine-tune Training 101 and build your dataset . Review 30 examples by hand. Set up WSL2 and Unsloth . torch.cuda.is_available() prints True. Train your first LoRA on a small model Deliverable: 200+ reviewed examples, a trained adapter, and a run log. Week 7: Evaluate, export, go further Evaluate : blind A/B and a regression set Export to GGUF and run it in LM Studio and Ollama Read Beyond SFT Deliverable: your fine-tune running in LM Studio, with an A/B score against the base model. Week 8: Steering, abliteration and the capstone Directions and steering : run steer.py Ablation and abliteration : run ablate.py on the verbosity direction Five routes to your own uncensored model and merging Start the capstone Deliverable: an edited model with before-and-after measurements, and a capstone spec. Adjusting the pace Your situation Suggestion Only 3 hours a week Stretch to 16 weeks. Never skip the hands-on parts. Full-time learning Compress to 3 to 4 weeks Small GPU (4 to 8 GB) Run 3B to 4B models for fast learning and 7B to 12B with offload for quality. Train 0.6B to 1B locally and use free Colab for 2B to 4B training runs. Already comfortable with Python Skim 2.3 and 6.2 After the eight weeks Finish the capstone, then see Where to go next for agents, MCP and other advanced topics."},{"id":"cheatsheet","mod":"R","num":"","title":"Command cheatsheet","mins":4,"optional":false,"text":"The commands and numbers you will want to hand. Everything here appears somewhere in the course. Check your system nvidia-smi # GPU, driver, VRAM use nvidia-smi -l 1 # refresh every second nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,driver_version,memory.total,memory.used,temperature.gpu --format=csv systeminfo | findstr /C:\"Total Physical Memory\" python --version ; git --version Python environment python -m venv .venv .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install openai requests pip list deactivate Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned # if activation is blocked Ollama ollama run MODEL # chat (downloads if needed) ollama run MODEL --verbose # show speed ollama pull MODEL ollama list ollama ps # loaded models and CPU/GPU split ollama show MODEL ollama stop MODEL ollama rm MODEL ollama create NAME -f Modelfile # API: http://localhost:11434 OpenAI-style: http://localhost:11434/v1 # Env vars: OLLAMA_MODELS, OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH, OLLAMA_HOST (careful) LM Studio command line lms ls # downloaded models lms get MODEL # search and download lms load MODEL --gpu=max --context-length=8192 lms ps # loaded models lms unload --all lms server start # API at http://localhost:1234/v1 lms server stop lms log stream Hugging Face pip install -U \"huggingface_hub[cli]\" hf auth login hf download REPO FILE --local-dir .\\models Memory maths What Formula or value Weights (GB) parameters in billions × bits per weight ÷ 8 Bits per weight F16 16 · Q8_0 ≈ 8.5 · Q6_K ≈ 6.6 · Q5_K_M ≈ 5.7 · Q4_K_M ≈ 4.9 · Q3_K_M ≈ 3.9 · Q2_K ≈ 3.4 KV cache per token 2 × layers × KV heads × head size × bytes (2 for 16-bit, about 1 for 8-bit) Overhead About 1 to 1.5 GB plus Windows ≈ 0.5 to 1 GB Speed ceiling memory bandwidth (GB/s) ÷ active weights (GB); real ≈ 55 to 75% Tokens vs words 1 token ≈ 0.75 English words ≈ 4 characters Ports and paths Thing Value LM Studio API http://localhost:1234/v1 Ollama API http://localhost:11434 Ollama models %HOMEPATH%\\.ollama or OLLAMA_MODELS Ollama logs %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Ollama Training (inside WSL2) wsl --install # in PowerShell, once nvidia-smi # inside Ubuntu: must show your GPU python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install --upgrade unsloth unsloth_zoo python make_dataset.py --notes notes --out data --model MODEL_ID # on Windows, LM Studio server running python train_lora.py 2>&1 | tee train.log python ab_eval.py # blind A/B against the base model Export with llama.cpp git clone https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp && cd llama.cpp cmake -B build && cmake --build build --config Release -j # add -DGGML_CUDA=ON for GPU tools python convert_hf_to_gguf.py MERGED_DIR --outtype bf16 --outfile model-BF16.gguf ./build/bin/llama-quantize model-BF16.gguf model-Q4_K_M.gguf Q4_K_M python convert_lora_to_gguf.py LORA_DIR --base-model-id BASE_ID --outtype f16 --outfile lora-F16.gguf lms import model-Q4_K_M.gguf --copy --user-repo me/mine # LM Studio (PowerShell) Steering and editing ./build/bin/llama-cvector-generator -m model.gguf --positive-file pos.txt --negative-file neg.txt -o vec.gguf ./build/bin/llama-server -m model.gguf --control-vector-scaled vec.gguf:0.8 --host 127.0.0.1 ./build/bin/llama-server -m base.gguf --lora-scaled lora-F16.gguf:0.7 --host 127.0.0.1 python ablate.py --pos pos.txt --neg neg.txt --test test.txt --layer 12 --out edited-model LoRA starting values Setting Value r / alpha / dropout 16 / 16 / 0 Learning rate 2e-4 (lower toward 2e-5 for long runs) Epochs 1 to 3 Batch × accumulation 2 × 4 Targets q, k, v, o, gate, up, down (+ out_proj for Qwen3.5) Qwen3.5 16-bit LoRA VRAM 0.8B ≈ 3 GB · 2B ≈ 5 GB · 4B ≈ 10 GB · 9B ≈ 22 GB PyTorch CUDA check import torch print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0)) WSL2 wsl --install wsl --list --verbose wsl --shutdown"},{"id":"glossary","mod":"R","num":"","title":"Glossary","mins":5,"optional":false,"text":"Plain-English definitions of every term in the course. Type to filter."},{"id":"resources","mod":"R","num":"","title":"Resources and reading list","mins":3,"optional":false,"text":"Good places to go deeper. All are free to read unless noted. Tools LM Studio : desktop app and documentation for local models Ollama : command-line model runner and model library llama.cpp : the engine under LM Studio and Ollama, and the home of GGUF Hugging Face : models, datasets and docs PyTorch install selector Unsloth docs : fine-tuning guides, per-model notebooks and VRAM tables TRL SFTTrainer and PEFT : the libraries under Unsloth llama.cpp quantize guide : converting and quantizing mergekit : model merging WSL installation guide NVIDIA driver downloads Learning Local AI Lab by Sethu: much of this course's material on how models work, training and weight editing is adapted from it and rewritten for Windows and NVIDIA 3Blue1Brown: Neural networks : the best visual introduction, including how transformers work Andrej Karpathy on YouTube : \"Intro to Large Language Models\" and \"Let's build GPT\" Hugging Face Learn : free courses on LLMs and Transformers r/LocalLLaMA : the community for local models. Useful, but verify claims. Papers mentioned Arditi et al. (2024), Refusal in Language Models Is Mediated by a Single Direction : the idea behind abliteration Vaswani et al. (2017), Attention Is All You Need : the transformer architecture Hu et al. (2021), LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models Dettmers et al. (2023), QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs Zhou et al. (2023), LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment : quality over quantity in fine-tuning data Rafailov et al. (2023), Direct Preference Optimization Qi et al. (2023), Fine-tuning Aligned Language Models Compromises Safety, Even When Users Do Not Intend To! Meng et al. (2022), Locating and Editing Factual Associations in GPT (ROME) Ilharco et al. (2023), Editing Models with Task Arithmetic Maxime Labonne (2024), Uncensor any LLM with abliteration : tutorial with the healing result in lesson 10.3 Sources used for this course's figures Ollama's Windows documentation ( docs.ollama.com/windows ): requirements, storage location and port LM Studio documentation ( lmstudio.ai/docs ): server, API and CLI VRAM tier guide ( vdf.ai , October 2026): example model sizes by tier LessWrong, What does abliteration actually cost (June 2026): the benchmark figures in lesson 5.2 Check dates Model names, app versions and prices change monthly. Cross-check anything time-sensitive with the official source."}]};
